{"version":"6.9","hitCount":209881,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQGTPNig1NjU1MTc3Nw==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=%28TITLE%3Aasthma+OR+ABSTRACT%3Aasthma%29&cursorMark=AoIIQGTPNig1NjU1MTc3Nw%3D%3D&resultType=core&pageSize=100&format=json","request":{"queryString":"(TITLE:asthma OR ABSTRACT:asthma)","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":100,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"PPR1341665","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9528447/v1","title":"Platelet Activation in Bronchial Asthma according to asthma endotype and clinical severity Short Title: Platelet Activation in Bronchial Asthma","authorString":"Liani R, Altomare I, Simeone PG, Alfonsetti M, Mucci M, Lattanzio S, Ricci A, Draicchio AM, Sanctis A, Mattia P, Papa F, Spadano F, Cavallucci E, Cipollone F, Recchiuti A, Santilli F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liani R","lastName":"Liani","firstName":"Rossella","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Altomare I","lastName":"Altomare","firstName":"Isotta","initials":"I"},{"fullName":"Simeone PG","lastName":"Simeone","firstName":"Paola Giustina","initials":"PG"},{"fullName":"Alfonsetti M","lastName":"Alfonsetti","firstName":"Margherita","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Mucci M","lastName":"Mucci","firstName":"Matteo","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Lattanzio S","lastName":"Lattanzio","firstName":"Stefano","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Ricci A","lastName":"Ricci","firstName":"Alessia","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Draicchio AM","lastName":"Draicchio","firstName":"Anna Maria","initials":"AM"},{"fullName":"Sanctis A","lastName":"Sanctis","firstName":"Antonello","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Mattia P","lastName":"Mattia","firstName":"Paolo","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Papa F","lastName":"Papa","firstName":"Francesco","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Spadano F","lastName":"Spadano","firstName":"Francesca","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Cavallucci E","lastName":"Cavallucci","firstName":"Enrico","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Cipollone F","lastName":"Cipollone","firstName":"Francesco","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Recchiuti A","lastName":"Recchiuti","firstName":"Antonio","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Santilli F","lastName":"Santilli","firstName":"Francesca","initials":"F"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Bronchial asthma is a chronic disease characterized by distinct inflammatory endotypes and variable clinical severity. Beyond their role in hemostasis, platelets are increasingly implicated in asthma pathophysiology. Defective resolution of inflammation, mediated by D-series resolvins, may contribute to disease severity. This study investigated the association between platelet activation, asthma severity, inflammatory endotypes, and pro-resolving mediators. Thirty‑nine patients with bronchial asthma and twelve controls were enrolled. Asthma severity was classified as moderate (steps 3–4) or severe (step 5) according to GINA 2024 guidelines. Patients were stratified into type-2-allergic (GT2A), type-2-non-allergic (GT2NA), and non-type-2 (GNT2) endotypes. Platelet activation (P-selectin and CD40) and D-series resolvins were measured. Asthma was evaluated using the Asthma Control Test. Asthma patients exhibited increased platelet activation, with higher P-selectin and CD40, in severe versus moderate disease. Platelet CD40 expression inversely correlated with ACT scores, indicating poorer disease control. Type-2 asthma endotypes, especially GT2A, showed enhanced platelet activation compared with GNT2 asthma. Severe asthma showed reduced levels of RvD1 and 17(R)-RvD1, while RvD2 was reduced in GT2A. These findings suggest that an imbalance between enhanced platelet activation and impaired resolution of inflammation may contribute to persistent airway inflammation and poor disease control, particularly in severe allergic asthma.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9528447/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1341665","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-10-09","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42999986","source":"MED","pmid":"42999986","doi":"10.1080/02770903.2026.2759488","title":"Asthma prevalence and influenza and COVID-19 vaccination coverage among children aged ≤17 years with asthma.","authorString":"Hou S, Lu P, Liu P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hou S","lastName":"Hou","firstName":"Shuaikang","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Arts and Science, Georgia State University, Atlanta, GA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lu P","lastName":"Lu","firstName":"Patrick","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Westminster School, Atlanta, GA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu P","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Pengbo","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Rollins School of Public Health, Emory University, Atlanta, GA, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4304304,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma","title":"The Journal of asthma : official journal of the Association for the Care of Asthma","essn":"1532-4303","issn":"0277-0903","nlmid":"8106454","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-6","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>Children with asthma are at increased risk for complications from respiratory infectious diseases. This study assessed asthma prevalence and influenza and COVID-19 vaccination coverage among children with asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>Data from the 2024 National Health Interview Survey (NHIS) were analyzed to assess asthma prevalence and influenza and COVID-19 vaccination coverage by asthma status among children.<h4>Results</h4>Current asthma prevalence was 6.5% among children aged ≤17 years. Prevalence was higher among children aged 5-12 years (8.2%) and 13-17 years (7.8%) compared with those aged <5 years (2.2%). Influenza and COVID-19 vaccination coverage among children with current asthma were 54.1% and 50.9%, respectively. Asthma prevalence and vaccination coverage varied by demographic and access-to-care characteristics.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Asthma prevalence was higher among older children, males, and non-Hispanic Black children. Influenza and COVID-19 vaccination coverage among children with asthma remained low. Tailored strategies are needed to reduce the asthma burden and improve vaccination coverage in populations with lower uptake.","affiliation":"College of Arts and Science, Georgia State University, Atlanta, GA, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Influenza","Asthma","Asthma Prevalence","Vaccination Coverage","National Health Interview Survey (Nhis)","Covid-19"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/02770903.2026.2759488"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-10","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42551975","source":"MED","pmid":"42551975","pmcid":"PMC13448598","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13448598"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjresp-2026-004245","title":"Parental asthma and childhood asthma phenotypes: maternal associations with persistent asthma.","authorString":"Rørholt Grefslie M, Håberg SE, Magnus MC, Omsland TK, Magnus P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Rørholt Grefslie M","lastName":"Rørholt Grefslie","firstName":"Marianne","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-9651-8743"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Fertility and Health, Norwegian Institute of Public Health, Oslo, Norway mariannehopen.rorholt@fhi.no."},{"affiliation":"Department of Paediatric Medicine, Oslo University Hospital, Oslo, Norway."}]}},{"fullName":"Håberg SE","lastName":"Håberg","firstName":"Siri Eldevik","initials":"SE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Fertility and Health, Norwegian Institute of Public Health, Oslo, Norway."},{"affiliation":"University of Bergen Department of Global Public Health and Primary Care, Bergen, Norway."}]}},{"fullName":"Magnus MC","lastName":"Magnus","firstName":"Maria C","initials":"MC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Fertility and Health, Norwegian Institute of Public Health, Oslo, Norway."}]}},{"fullName":"Omsland TK","lastName":"Omsland","firstName":"Tone K","initials":"TK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Oslo Department of Community Medicine and Global Health, Oslo, Norway."}]}},{"fullName":"Magnus P","lastName":"Magnus","firstName":"Per","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Fertility and Health, Norwegian Institute of Public Health, Oslo, Norway."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0568-3774"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-9651-8743"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4239043,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Open Respir Res","title":"BMJ open respiratory research","essn":"2052-4439","issn":"2052-4439","nlmid":"101638061","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Open Respir Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e004245","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Maternal asthma has been more strongly associated with asthma in offspring than paternal asthma, but the influence of parental asthma on distinct asthma phenotypes remains unclear.<h4>Methods</h4>We studied 16 055 children from the Norwegian Mother, Father and Child Cohort Study (MoBa). Parental asthma was reported at recruitment and classified as no parental asthma (reference), maternal asthma only, paternal asthma only or both parents with asthma. Offspring asthma was assessed at ages 3, 7 and 14 years through questionnaire-based parental reports, and four phenotypes were defined: never asthma, early-onset transient, early-onset persistent and late-onset asthma. Logistic regression was used to estimate associations between parental asthma status and offspring phenotypes, adjusting for sociodemographic, lifestyle and atopic factors, including atopic eczema and pollen/hay fever.<h4>Results</h4>Offspring asthma phenotypes were distributed as follows: never asthma 88.8%, early-onset transient asthma 3.3%, early-onset persistent asthma 2.7% and late-onset asthma 5.2%. Maternal asthma was associated with increased odds of all offspring asthma phenotypes: early-onset transient asthma (OR 2.09, 95% CI 1.52 to 2.88), early-onset persistent asthma (OR 3.47, 95% CI 2.56 to 4.71) and late-onset asthma (OR 2.18, 95% CI 1.69 to 2.81). Paternal asthma showed similar associations, although point estimates were generally smaller: early-onset transient asthma (OR 2.01, 95% CI 1.48 to 2.73), early-onset persistent asthma (OR 2.49, 95% CI 1.81 to 3.44) and late-onset asthma (OR 1.85, 95% CI 1.44 to 2.38). Asthma in both parents was most strongly associated with early-onset persistent asthma (OR 5.34, 95% CI 2.86 to 9.98), whereas associations with early-onset transient and late-onset asthma were smaller and accompanied by wider CIs. The overall pattern of associations was similar across analyses stratified by parental atopy.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Associations with maternal asthma were more pronounced than those with paternal asthma, particularly for early-onset persistent and late-onset phenotypes, although CIs overlapped for most comparisons. In contrast, early-onset transient asthma showed less differentiation by parental asthma status.","affiliation":"Centre for Fertility and Health, Norwegian Institute of Public Health, Oslo, Norway mariannehopen.rorholt@fhi.no.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Logistic Models"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cohort Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mothers","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descrip