orList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kenyon CC","lastName":"Kenyon","firstName":"Chén C","initials":"CC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Pediatrics, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"PolicyLab, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."}]}},{"fullName":"Negro D","lastName":"Negro","firstName":"DanaRose","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Pediatrics, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"PolicyLab, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."}]}},{"fullName":"Fluehr J","lastName":"Fluehr","firstName":"Jaime","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Asthma Prevention Program, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."}]}},{"fullName":"Shults J","lastName":"Shults","firstName":"Justine","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Informatics, Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."}]}},{"fullName":"Seay H","lastName":"Seay","firstName":"Hannah","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pediatric Pulmonary Medicine, Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Pappas J","lastName":"Pappas","firstName":"Julie","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Westat Inc, Rockville, Maryland."}]}},{"fullName":"Bryant-Stephens T","lastName":"Bryant-Stephens","firstName":"Tyra","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Pediatrics, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"PolicyLab, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."},{"affiliation":"Community Asthma Prevention Program, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"158","journalIssueId":4251445,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatrics","title":"Pediatrics","essn":"1098-4275","issn":"0031-4005","nlmid":"0376422","isoabbreviation":"Pediatrics"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e2025074267","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>School-based asthma management is a key facet of child asthma care. We aimed to describe the proportion of students whose schools have child-specific components of asthma care, derive a composite metric of these components (\"school asthma readiness\"), and assess its association with asthma exacerbations (asthma risk) in the preceding year.<h4>Methods</h4>Within a nested cohort of children enrolled in a larger randomized clinical trial, we assessed the baseline proportion of children whose school had elements of necessary asthma care. We then derived a \"school asthma readiness\" composite and used ordinal logistic regression to model the association between number of asthma exacerbations in the preceding year and the composite, accounting for demographic, clinical, and school characteristics.<h4>Results</h4>Of 202 participants aged 5 to 13 years, most identified as Black (95%) and non-Hispanic (98%), and most participants (73%) had an emergency department visit for asthma in the year before enrollment. Most students' schools (79%) had awareness of the child's asthma diagnosis, whereas fewer had reliever medications and valved holding chambers (both 31%) and asthma care plans (7%). Asthma exacerbations in the prior year were associated with a significantly higher school asthma readiness in bivariate (odds ratio [OR], 1.44 [95% CI, 1.14-1.82]; P = .002) and multivariable analysis (OR, 1.31 [95% CI, 1.02-1.7]; P = .037).<h4>Conclusions</h4>A minority of children attended schools that were equipped to manage asthma symptoms. More past exacerbations were associated with higher school readiness, suggesting that more work is needed to support proactive asthma care in schools.","affiliation":"Division of General Pediatrics, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disease Progression"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cohort Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child, Preschool"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"School Health Services"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2025-074267"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"42571888","reference":"Pediatrics. 2026 Sep 1;158(3):e2026077133. doi: 10.1542/peds.2026-077133.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-31","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42971323","source":"MED","pmid":"42971323","doi":"10.1016/j.jaip.2026.10.030","title":"Health-Related Social Needs, Depression and Anxiety, and Asthma Outcomes in Adults with Utilization-defined Mild Asthma.","authorString":"Schatz M, Zhou B, Zeiger RS, Crawford WW, Rozema E, Puttock EJ, Xie F, Vollmer WM, Xu S, Chen W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Schatz M","lastName":"Schatz","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allergy Kaiser Permanente Southern California (KPSC), San Diego, CA, USA. Electronic address: Michael.X.Schatz@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou B","lastName":"Zhou","firstName":"Botao","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Botao.X.Zhou@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Zeiger RS","lastName":"Zeiger","firstName":"Robert S","initials":"RS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allergy Kaiser Permanente Southern California (KPSC), San Diego, CA, USA; Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA; Department of Clinical Science, Kaiser Permanente Bernard J. Tyson School of Medicine, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Robert.S.Zeiger@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Crawford WW","lastName":"Crawford","firstName":"William W","initials":"WW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allergy KPSC, Harbor City, CA, USA. Electronic address: William.W.Crawford@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Rozema E","lastName":"Rozema","firstName":"Emily","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Emily.Rozema@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Puttock EJ","lastName":"Puttock","firstName":"Eric J","initials":"EJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Eric.J.Puttock@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Xie F","lastName":"Xie","firstName":"Fagen","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Fagen.Xie@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Vollmer WM","lastName":"Vollmer","firstName":"William M","initials":"WM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Kaiser Permanente Center for Health Research, Portland, OR, USA. Electronic address: cyclinguybill@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu S","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Stanley","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Stan.Xu@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen W","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Wansu","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, KPSC, Pasadena, CA, USA. Electronic address: Wansu.Chen@kp.org."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4305359,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract","title":"The journal of allergy and clinical immunology. In practice","essn":"2213-2201","issn":"2213-2198","nlmid":"101597220","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S2213-2198(26)00912-8","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Health-related social needs (HRSN) and depression or anxiety are associated with adverse asthma outcomes in patients with moderate-to-severe asthma, but their individual and combined associations with asthma outcomes among adults with mild asthma have not been well characterized.<h4>Objective</h4>To examine the individual and combined associations of HRSN, depression, and anxiety with asthma control and asthma-related healthcare utilization among adults with utilization-defined mild asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>A stratified sample of 3,787 adults with utilization-defined mild asthma completed a survey including the CMS HRSN Screening Tool, PHQ-2, GAD-2, AIRQ®, and self-reported asthma-related emergency department or urgent care visits during the previous 12 months. Modified Poisson regression evaluated associations after adjustment for age, sex, and race and ethnicity.<h4>Results</h4>More than half of participants reported at least one HRSN, and approximately one-quarter screened positive for depression or anxiety. Depression and HRSN frequently coexisted. Depression was associated with very poorly controlled asthma (adjusted relative risk [aRR] 1.48, 95% CI 1.17-1.86) after adjusting for age, sex and race/ethnicity. Compared with no HRSN, one or more HRSN were associated with very poorly controlled asthma, and two or more HRSN were associated with asthma-related emergency department or urgent care visits. Anxiety was not independently associated with either outcome. Participants with both HRSN and depression or anxiety experienced the greatest burden of adverse asthma outcomes.<h4>Conclusions</h4>HRSN and depression are associated with poor asthma outcomes among adults with utilization-defined mild asthma. Assessing both social needs and mental health may help identify patients at greatest risk for poor asthma outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Allergy Kaiser Permanente Southern California (KPSC), San Diego, CA, USA. Electronic address: Michael.X.Schatz@kp.org.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"R01 HL163049","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Depression","Anxiety","Asthma Control","Patient-reported Outcomes","Mild Asthma","Social Determinants Of Health","Asthma Exacerbations","Health-related Social Needs"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaip.2026.10.030"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-08","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42873426","source":"MED","pmid":"42873426","pmcid":"PMC13668936","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13668936"]},"doi":"10.1007/s11882-026-01302-4","title":"Obesity-Related Asthma: Structure and Inflammation.","authorString":"Tzeng TH, Kamau P, Forno E, Kloepfer KM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tzeng TH","lastName":"Tzeng","firstName":"Tony H","initials":"TH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatric Pulmonology, Allergy/Immunology, and Sleep Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Indiana University School of Medicine and Riley Hospital for Children, Indianapolis, IN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamau P","lastName":"Kamau","firstName":"Pauline","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatric Pulmonology, Allergy/Immunology, and Sleep Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Indiana University School of Medicine and Riley Hospital for Children, Indianapolis, IN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Forno E","lastName":"Forno","firstName":"Erick","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatric Pulmonology, Allergy/Immunology, and Sleep Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Indiana University School of Medicine and Riley Hospital for Children, Indianapolis, IN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kloepfer KM","lastName":"Kloepfer","firstName":"Kirsten M","initials":"KM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1504-8114"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatric Pulmonology, Allergy/Immunology, and Sleep Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Indiana University School of Medicine and Riley Hospital for Children, Indianapolis, IN, USA. kloepfer@iu.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1504-8114"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4275657,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Allergy Asthma Rep","title":"Current allergy and asthma reports","essn":"1534-6315","issn":"1529-7322","nlmid":"101096440","isoabbreviation":"Curr Allergy Asthma Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"57","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose of review</h4>Asthma and obesity are affecting children and teenagers at higher rates than previous generations. Epidemiological data link obesity during childhood with the subsequent development of asthma. In this review we describe developmental and inflammatory factors that contribute to asthma severity in children with obesity.<h4>Recent findings</h4>Asthma driven by obesity is likely due to both structural and inflammatory changes. Excess weight places mechanical stress on the airway thereby reducing lung volumes. In addition, obesity is linked to chronic inflammation. Adipose tissue releases pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-6 and TNF-α) which drives the airway towards hyperresponsiveness. This \"obesity-related asthma\" phenotype does not respond well to corticosteroids making these patients difficult to treat. In children and teenagers, treatment options are limited to currently approved asthma medications; however, improvement in asthma control has been observed in adults utilizing metformin and GLP-1 agonists. Children with obesity are more likely to develop asthma. Their asthma symptoms tend to be more frequent and harder to control. With the rate of obesity on the rise, it is paramount to identify the mechanisms underlying systemic inflammation in those with elevated BMIs. This will allow us to find better treatment options for \"obesity-related asthma.\"","affiliation":"Pediatric Pulmonology, Allergy/Immunology, and Sleep Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Indiana University School of Medicine and Riley Hospital for Children, Indianapolis, IN, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adipose Tissue","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Obesity","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Inflammation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cytokines","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Obesity","Asthma","Airway inflammation","Dysanapsis","Obesity-related Asthma"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Cytokines","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11882-026-01302-4"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13668936"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13668936?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42674280","source":"MED","pmid":"42674280","doi":"10.1016/j.chest.2026.08.032","title":"The Airway Microbiome in Asthma.","authorString":"Richardson H, Pollock J, Chan R, Chalmers JD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Richardson H","lastName":"Richardson","firstName":"Hollian","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of Dundee, Dundee, Scotland."}]}},{"fullName":"Pollock J","lastName":"Pollock","firstName":"Jennifer","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of Dundee, Dundee, Scotland."}]}},{"fullName":"Chan R","lastName":"Chan","firstName":"Rory","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2805-8266"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of Dundee, Dundee, Scotland."}]}},{"fullName":"Chalmers JD","lastName":"Chalmers","firstName":"James D","initials":"JD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5514-7868"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of Dundee, Dundee, Scotland. Electronic address: j.chalmers@dundee.ac.uk."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5514-7868"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2805-8266"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4238989,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Chest","title":"Chest","essn":"1931-3543","issn":"0012-3692","nlmid":"0231335","isoabbreviation":"Chest"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S0012-3692(26)06527-X","abstractText":"<h4>Topic importance</h4>Asthma is a heterogenous airways disease characterized by variable airflow limitation, airway inflammation, and bronchial hyperresponsiveness. Evidence suggests that the airway microbiome plays an important role in asthma pathophysiologic characteristics. Most microbiome studies have investigated the bacterial constituents of the microbiome, and further studies are needed to investigate the role of the airway virome and mycobiome in asthma.<h4>Review findings</h4>Airway microbiome dysbiosis is associated with asthma development, disease severity, and inflammatory endotypes. However, it remains unclear whether dysbiosis drives inflammation or is a result of inflammation. Haemophilus and Moraxella alongside rhinovirus and respiratory syncytial virus have been shown to be associated with the development of asthma in children. In established asthma, airway microbiome dysbiosis is associated with severity and risk of exacerbation. Distinct airway microbiome profiles are observed for the different inflammatory endotypes. Patients with asthma and eosinophilic inflammation have higher microbial diversity similar to that of healthy individuals, whereas neutrophilic inflammation is associated with reduced microbial diversity, higher bacterial load, and a pathogen-dominated microbiome profile, suggesting an important role for dysbiosis in asthma that currently is refractory to antitype 2 biologic therapy.<h4>Summary</h4>To understand the role of the microbiome in asthma, microbiome data must be integrated with other so-called omic approaches such as proteomics. Recent studies have used a multiomic approach to investigate the microbiome and host interaction as well as identifying asthma endotypes and potential therapeutic targets. Antibiotics, probiotics, monoclonal antibodies, and diet theoretically could be used to target the airway and gut microbiome therapeutically in asthma.","affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of Dundee, Dundee, Scotland.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Microbiome"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chest.2026.08.032"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-23","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-31","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42964450","source":"MED","pmid":"42964450","doi":"10.5409/wjcp.122880","title":"Clinical and functional correlates of blood eosinophilia in pediatric asthma: A retrospective cohort study.","authorString":"Das RR, Panigrahi MK, Mahapatro S, John J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Das RR","lastName":"Das","firstName":"Rashmi Ranjan","initials":"RR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Bhubaneswar 751019, Odisha, India. ped_rashmi@aiimsbhubaneswar.edu.in."}]}},{"fullName":"Panigrahi MK","lastName":"Panigrahi","firstName":"Manoj Kumar","initials":"MK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine and Critical Care, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Bhubaneswar 751019, Odisha, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Mahapatro S","lastName":"Mahapatro","firstName":"Samarendra","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Bhubaneswar 751019, Odisha, India."}]}},{"fullName":"John J","lastName":"John","firstName":"Joseph","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Bhubaneswar 751019, Odisha, India."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4303258,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"World J Clin Pediatr","title":"World journal of clinical pediatrics","essn":"2219-2808","issn":"2219-2808","nlmid":"101627548","isoabbreviation":"World J Clin Pediatr"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-12-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"122880","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Pediatric asthma is a heterogeneous disease with distinct inflammatory phenotypes. Asthma with blood eosinophilia (driven by type 2 inflammation) is associated with increased asthma severity and poor asthma control. However, pediatric data including that from resource-limited settings [low-and-middle-income country (LMIC)] remains limited.<h4>Aim</h4>To evaluate the clinical characteristics, asthma severity, and lung function in children with asthma and blood eosinophilia from a LMIC setting.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective cohort study included children aged 5-14 years with asthma evaluated at a tertiary care center in Eastern India between March 1, 2017 and February 29, 2020. Blood eosinophilia was defined as an absolute eosinophil count (AEC) of ≥ 400 cells/mm<sup>3</sup>. The primary outcome was the association between blood eosinophilia and asthma control. Secondary outcomes included asthma severity, spirometry parameters, serum immunoglobulin E (IgE) levels, and factors independently associated with eosinophilia. Clinical characteristics, asthma control test scores, lung function parameters, and serum IgE levels were compared between children with and without eosinophilia. Multivariate analysis was performed to identify factors independently associated with blood eosinophilia.<h4>Results</h4>Among 176 children included in the analysis, 84 (47.8%) had blood eosinophilia. Poor asthma control was more common in children with eosinophilia than in those without eosinophilia (51.2% <i>vs</i> 34.8%, <i>P</i> = 0.027). Eosinophilia was significantly associated with moderate and severe asthma and with greater airway obstruction, reflected by a lower mean forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1)/forced vital capacity (FVC) ratio (65.5% <i>vs</i> 69.7%, <i>P</i> = 0.006). Median serum IgE levels were significantly increased in the eosinophilic group (295 <i>vs</i> 98 kIU/L, <i>P</i> < 0.001). On multivariate analysis, age > 8 years, elevated serum IgE (> 300 kIU/L), poor asthma control, and reduced FEV1/FVC ratio were independently associated with blood eosinophilia.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Childhood asthma with blood eosinophilia (an AEC of ≥ 400 cells/mm<sup>3</sup>) is associated with poor control, greater disease severity, and increased airflow limitation. Peripheral eosinophil count may serve as an important biomarker for risk stratification and personalized asthma management in a LMIC setting.","affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Bhubaneswar 751019, Odisha, India. ped_rashmi@aiimsbhubaneswar.edu.in.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biomarker","Asthma Control","Pediatric Asthma","Type 2 Inflammation","Blood Eosinophilia"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.5409/wjcp.122880"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-31","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-12-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-12-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42706200","source":"MED","pmid":"42706200","doi":"10.3760/cma.j.cn112147-20260514-00275","title":"[Global initiative for asthma (GINA) 2026 update: persistent attention to asthma-related cough].","authorString":"Lai KF, Yi F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lai KF","lastName":"Lai","firstName":"K F","initials":"KF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, the First Affiliated Hospital of Guangzhou Medical University, National Center for Respiratory Medicine, National Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Disease, Guangzhou Institute of Respiratory Health, Guangzhou 510120, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yi F","lastName":"Yi","firstName":"F","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, the First Affiliated Hospital of Guangzhou Medical University, National Center for Respiratory Medicine, National Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Disease, Guangzhou Institute of Respiratory Health, Guangzhou 510120, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"49","journalIssueId":4259362,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Zhonghua Jie He He Hu Xi Za Zhi","title":"Zhonghua jie he he hu xi za zhi = Zhonghua jiehe he huxi zazhi = Chinese journal of tuberculosis and respiratory diseases","issn":"1001-0939","nlmid":"8712226","isoabbreviation":"Zhonghua Jie He He Hu Xi Za Zhi"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"921-923","abstractText":"The 2026 update of the Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) further strengthens cough-related content in the diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and assessment of asthma. The major updates include refinement of cough-related asthma phenotypes, incorporation of induced sputum differential cell as a diagnostic method for asthma, inclusion of chronic cough-related etiologies in the differential diagnosis of asthma, and the integration of cough symptoms into asthma assessment. The expanded coverage of cough in the GINA 2026 update reflects the growing global attention to cough in asthma, and also highlights GINA's acknowledgment of research achievements from China in this field.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, the First Affiliated Hospital of Guangzhou Medical University, National Center for Respiratory Medicine, National Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Disease, Guangzhou Institute of Respiratory Health, Guangzhou 510120, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"chi","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article","English Abstract"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sputum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cough","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Diagnosis, Differential"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"China"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chronic Cough"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3760/cma.j.cn112147-20260514-00275"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42823689","source":"MED","pmid":"42823689","pmcid":"PMC13628815","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13628815"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12931-026-03872-3","title":"Relationship between asthma, obesity and other comorbidities: the Australasian Severe Asthma Registry.","authorString":"Scott HA, Lee J, Denton E, Barnett A, Griffiths P, Middleton PG, Hansbro PM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Scott HA","lastName":"Scott","firstName":"Hayley A","initials":"HA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biomedical Sciences and Pharmacy, The University of Newcastle, University Drive, Callaghan, NSW, Australia. hayley.scott@newcastle.edu.au."},{"affiliation":"Immune Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Level 2 West, Lot 1 Kookaburra Circuit, New Lambton Heights, NSW, 2305, Australia. hayley.scott@newcastle.edu.au."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee J","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Joy","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, Austin Health, Melbourne, VIC, Australia."},{"affiliation":"School of Translational Medicine, Monash University, Melbourne, VIC, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Asthma, Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Respiratory Medicine, Alfred Hospital, Melbourne, VIC, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Denton E","lastName":"Denton","firstName":"Eve","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Translational Medicine, Monash University, Melbourne, VIC, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Asthma, Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Respiratory Medicine, Alfred Hospital, Melbourne, VIC, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Barnett A","lastName":"Barnett","firstName":"Adrian","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, Mater Hospital, South Brisbane, QLD, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Griffiths P","lastName":"Griffiths","firstName":"Paul","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, North Shore Hospital, Auckland, New Zealand."}]}},{"fullName":"Middleton PG","lastName":"Middleton","firstName":"Peter G","initials":"PG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3361-4941"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, Westmead Hospital, Sydney, New South Wales, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Ludwig Engel Centre for Respiratory Research, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Hansbro PM","lastName":"Hansbro","firstName":"Philip M","initials":"PM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Inflammation, Faculty of Science, School of Life Sciences, Centenary Institute and University of Technology Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3361-4941"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"27","journalIssueId":4238734,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Res","title":"Respiratory research","essn":"1465-993X","issn":"1465-9921","nlmid":"101090633","isoabbreviation":"Respir Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"377","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The impact of obesity and sex on asthma outcomes and inflammation in the real-world setting is not completely understood. The aim of this study was to profile obesity and other comorbidities in a cohort of male and female adults with asthma; to understand the impact of obesity on asthma outcomes; and to examine sex-specific relationships between obesity and airway inflammation.<h4>Methods</h4>We profiled comorbidities including obesity, as well as clinical asthma outcomes and airway inflammation, in patients with asthma enrolled in the Australasian Severe Asthma Registry. Data were analysed using t-tests (parametric data) or Wilcoxon rank sum tests (non-parametric data). Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) was used to examine the effects of obesity and asthma severity on clinical and inflammatory outcomes.<h4>Results</h4>We assessed 1,019 patients with severe (n = 882) and non-severe (n = 137) asthma. Patients with severe asthma were 67% more likely to have obesity, while patients with severe asthma and obesity were more likely to be female. Obesity was associated with a higher prevalence of asthma-related hospitalisation in the previous 12 months, in both severe (26.2% vs. 15.7%, p < 0.001) and non-severe asthma (12.5% vs. 3.1%, p < 0.001). Obesity was also associated with poorer asthma control and lung function, and higher sputum neutrophils. Interestingly, sputum %neutrophils correlated with body mass index in females (r<sub>s</sub>=0.370, p = 0.003), but not males (r<sub>s</sub>=0.092, p = 0.495). Neutrophilic airway inflammation was associated with poorer lung function and asthma control.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This study demonstrates that obesity is associated with a significantly worse clinical asthma presentation in the real-world setting and increases the risk of asthma-related hospitalisation. Critically, our findings suggest that obesity is associated with increased neutrophilic airway inflammation in females, but not males, providing some insight into possible sex-related differences in severe asthma.","affiliation":"School of Biomedical Sciences and Pharmacy, The University of Newcastle, University Drive, Callaghan, NSW, Australia. hayley.scott@newcastle.edu.au.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Obesity","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Severity of Illness Index"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Registries"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prevalence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cohort Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Comorbidity"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sex Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Australia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Australasia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Sex","Obesity","Asthma","Airway inflammation","Severe asthma","Comorbidity","Asthma Symptoms"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12931-026-03872-3"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13628815"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13628815?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42969352","source":"MED","pmid":"42969352","doi":"10.4103/aam.aam_698_26","title":"Effectiveness of Multimodal Educational Program on Asthma Control, Quality of Life, and Medication Adherence among Children Diagnosed with Asthma: A Study Protocol for Randomized Controlled Trial.","authorString":"Mohan R, Sharma MC, Kumar P, Sharma SK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mohan R","lastName":"Mohan","firstName":"Remiya","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Nursing, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Sharma MC","lastName":"Sharma","firstName":"Mukesh Chand","initials":"MC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Nursing, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar P","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Prawin","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Sharma SK","lastName":"Sharma","firstName":"Suresh Kumar","initials":"SK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Nursing, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4303979,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Afr Med","title":"Annals of African medicine","essn":"0975-5764","issn":"1596-3519","nlmid":"101231417","isoabbreviation":"Ann Afr Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Approximate 80% of cases of poor adherence still occur due to improper inhaler technique and numerous studies show that one of the most important aspects of managing asthma is patient education.<h4>Aims and objectives</h4>The study aims to evaluate the impact of Multimodal Educational Program [MEP] on asthma control, quality of life, and medication adherence among children diagnosed with asthma.<h4>Method/design</h4>This Randomized Controlled Trial (Parallel group, pre-test post-test design) targets children of age group 7-15 Years, diagnosed with asthma and visiting OPD with their parents. Randomization will be done by adopting the computerized randomized block design. Sequentially Numbered, Opaque, and Sealed Envelopes will be used to ensure allocation concealment. Interventional group will receive Multimodal Educational Program [MEP] that will be provided via VARK Model designed by Neil Fleming. Data on Asthma Control test, quality of life and medication adherence will be collected at baseline, one month, and three months by the data collector who will be blinded.<h4>Discussion</h4>Asthma is a prevalent illness in children, and even with the availability of excellent treatments, the incidence and severity of asthma-related sufferings remain unchanged. Studies show that one of the most important aspects of managing asthma is patient education. This study is planned considering the need and significance of educational interventions among children diagnosed with asthma which is crucial for keeping an eye on symptoms, asthma control and to prevent exacerbations.","affiliation":"College of Nursing, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"fre","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Quality of life","Education","Inhaled Corticosteroids","Medication Adherence","Observance Thérapeutique","Éducation","Qualité De Vie","Asthme","Corticostéroïdes Inhalés"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4103/aam.aam_698_26"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"43009016","source":"MED","pmid":"43009016",