ation":[{"affiliation":"Program in Emerging Infectious Diseases, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Xie M","lastName":"Xie","firstName":"Min","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Tongji Hospital, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen ZH","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Zhi Hong","initials":"ZH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Xinhua Hospital, Shanghai Jiaotong University School of Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qin L","lastName":"Qin","firstName":"Ling","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Xiangya Hospital, Central South University, Changsha, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang F","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Fang","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang J","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Jie","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Second Affiliated Hospital of Jilin University, Changchun, Jilin, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Luo FM","lastName":"Luo","firstName":"Feng Min","initials":"FM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Center of Excellence in Severe Asthma and Treatable Traits, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Health and Multimorbidity, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunology and Inflammation, Frontiers Science Center for Disease-Related Molecular Network, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li WM","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Wei Min","initials":"WM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Center of Excellence in Severe Asthma and Treatable Traits, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Health and Multimorbidity, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Institute of Respiratory Health, Respiratory Microbiome Laboratory, Frontiers Science Center for Disease-related Molecular Network, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China. Electronic address: weimin003@163.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang G","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Gang","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Center of Excellence in Severe Asthma and Treatable Traits, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Health and Multimorbidity, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunology and Inflammation, Frontiers Science Center for Disease-Related Molecular Network, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China. Electronic address: wcums-respiration@hotmail.com."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4305359,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract","title":"The journal of allergy and clinical immunology. In practice","essn":"2213-2201","issn":"2213-2198","nlmid":"101597220","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S2213-2198(26)00962-1","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Variable clinical remission rates on biologics may reflect heterogeneity within type 2 (T2) asthma, but such heterogeneity remains largely unexplored.<h4>Objectives</h4>To identify clinically distinct T2 asthma subtypes using multidimensional profiling and hierarchical clustering.<h4>Methods</h4>All T2 participants (n=764) were from the Australasian Severe Asthma Network, identified by elevated eosinophil levels, elevated FeNO, or allergic sensitization. Baseline clinical and inflammatory data were integrated to identify potential clusters in the training set using hierarchical clustering, followed by prospective assessment of exacerbation over 12 months. A random forest classifier was internally cross-validated and applied to a held-out validation set to assess profile consistency.<h4>Results</h4>Three subtypes emerged as: Cluster 1 (n=241, 45.0%) represented a female-predominant, well-controlled allergic subtype; Cluster 2 (n=110, 20.6%) constituted older T2 asthma patients with overlapping non-T2 features, including increased sputum neutrophil counts, fixed airflow limitation, and multiple comorbidities, with higher severe exacerbation rates (incidence rate ratios [IRR]=2.54, P=0.025) and emergency visit rates (IRR=5.06, P=0.040); and Cluster 3 (n=184, 34.4%) showed multiple elevated T2 biomarkers, poor symptom control and increased severe exacerbation (IRR=2.49, P=0.007) and hospitalization risk (IRR=3.28, P=0.009). The classifier showed 83.6% agreement with the original cluster assignments (κ=0.738), with post-bronchodilator FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC, age, and BMI as leading discriminators.<h4>Conclusions</h4>T2 asthma encompasses distinct subtypes with prognostic and therapeutic implications. Our findings support a precision medicine approach for these subtypes: Cluster 3 patients may warrant early assessment for biologic therapy when eligible, while Cluster 2 may benefit from integrated strategies addressing non-T2 traits.","affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Clinical Research Center for Respiratory Disease, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Center of Excellence in Severe Asthma and Treatable Traits, State Key Laboratory of Respiratory Health and Multimorbidity, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China; Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunology and Inflammation, Frontiers Science Center for Disease-Related Molecular Network, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cluster analysis","Heterogeneity","Exacerbation","Random Forest","Type 2 Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaip.2026.10.039"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-10","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42811565","source":"MED","pmid":"42811565","doi":"10.3760/cma.j.cn112147-20260512-00270","title":"[Standardized establishment scheme for asthma outpatient clinics (pilot)].","authorString":"Chinese Thoracic Society, Chinese Medical Association, Respiratory Medicine Professional Committee, Chinese Research Hospital Association, Expert Group of the Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine Asthma Specialty Capacity Enhancement Program.","authorList":{"author":[{"collectiveName":"Chinese Thoracic Society, Chinese Medical Association"},{"collectiveName":"Respiratory Medicine Professional Committee, Chinese Research Hospital Association"},{"collectiveName":"Expert Group of the Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine Asthma Specialty Capacity Enhancement Program"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"49","journalIssueId":4274112,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Zhonghua Jie He He Hu Xi Za Zhi","title":"Zhonghua jie he he hu xi za zhi = Zhonghua jiehe he huxi zazhi = Chinese journal of tuberculosis and respiratory diseases","issn":"1001-0939","nlmid":"8712226","isoabbreviation":"Zhonghua Jie He He Hu Xi Za Zhi"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1082-1094","abstractText":"Bronchial asthma (asthma) is a common chronic respiratory disease in China, yet the quality of diagnosis and treatment varies across regions and levels of health care institutions. Asthma, particularly severe asthma, is often poorly controlled and associated with frequent acute exacerbations, substantially impairing patients' quality of life and imposing a considerable socioeconomic burden. Considering the current state of asthma diagnosis and treatment in China, the writing group drew on domestic and international asthma guidelines and literature to develop standards for asthma outpatient clinics within the framework of the standardized development criteria for Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine (PCCM) and formulated this implementation plan. The plan is intended to provide a reference for the establishment of disease-specific asthma clinics in China and to enhance the capacity for the management of asthma, particularly severe asthma.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"chi","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Ar