:{"issue":"11","volume":"51","journalIssueId":4300097,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nurse Pract","title":"The Nurse practitioner","essn":"1538-8662","issn":"0361-1817","nlmid":"7603663","isoabbreviation":"Nurse Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"34-38","abstractText":"<h4>Abstract</h4>Biologic medications provide opportunities to improve the health, safety, quality of life, and long-term outcomes of patients with moderate-to-severe uncontrolled asthma. This article provides primary care nurse practitioners with an overview of the pathophysiology of Type 2 inflammation in asthma, the mechanism of action of biologics approved for asthma, monitoring considerations, and practice considerations.","affiliation":"Mara Thrasher is an NP at the Allergy, Asthma, and Immunology Center in East Tennessee.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"NU","qualifierName":"nursing"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Biological Products","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Biological Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"NU","qualifierName":"nursing"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nurse Practitioners"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Primary Care Nursing"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma Medications","Asthma Management","Uncontrolled Asthma","Asthma Biologic Therapies"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Biological Products","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/01.NPR.0000000000000490"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42904911","source":"MED","pmid":"42904911","doi":"10.1080/02770903.2026.2751828","title":"The Effect of Nasal Corticosteroids and Antihistamines on Asthma Control in Patients with Asthma and Comorbid Allergic Rhinitis: A Systematic Review.","authorString":"Alsufyani A, Saaty L, Mansur A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Alsufyani A","lastName":"Alsufyani","firstName":"Adel","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anaesthesia Specialist. Teaching assistant at the Department of Anaesthesia at King Khalid University, Abha, Saudi Arabia."},{"affiliation":"Postgraduate Student, University of Birmingham, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Saaty L","lastName":"Saaty","firstName":"Layan","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Respiratory Therapist, Saudi Arabia; Postgraduate Student, University of Birmingham, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Mansur A Professor","lastName":"Mansur","firstName":"Adel","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, University of Birmingham, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4281971,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma","title":"The Journal of asthma : official journal of the Association for the Care of Asthma","essn":"1532-4303","issn":"0277-0903","nlmid":"8106454","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-24","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To determine whether treating allergic rhinitis (AR) with intranasal corticosteroids (INCS) or antihistamines improves asthma outcomes in adults with confirmed AR-asthma comorbidity.<b><i>Data Sources</i>:</b> A systematic search of PubMed, MEDLINE (Ovid), Embase, and Google Scholar was conducted from the inception of the databases to May 2025. In addition, a supplementary hand search of the reference lists of included studies was conducted.<b><i>Study Selections</i>:</b> Ten trials met the inclusion criteria (n = 12,924 participants; duration: 2-24 weeks). Seven studies contributed to the meta-analysis of asthma symptom scores, while other outcomes were narratively synthesized owing to incomplete or heterogeneous reporting.<h4>Results</h4>Both interventions were associated with statistically significant improvement in asthma symptom control. The pooled effect favoured both treatment arms (standardized mean difference (SMD) = -0.55; 95% CI: - 0.67 to - 0.43), with subgroup analysis showing a consistent benefit from antihistamines (SMD = - 0.51) and a numerically larger, though nonsignificantly different, effect for INCS (SMD = - 0.87). <b>However, no included trial compared INCS with antihistamines directly, so this comparison remains indirect</b>. Evidence was limited for lung function, rescue medication use, bronchial hyperresponsiveness, and exacerbations, and most trials lacked data that could be extracted. Certainty level (GRADE) shows moderate for symptom outcomes and low to very low for all other domains.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Adjunctive treatment of AR with antihistamines or INCS, shows a clinically relevant improvement in asthma symptom control among adults with asthma. However, the evidence base is constrained by small sample sizes, short follow-up periods, and suboptimal reporting. More Powered clinical trials using standardized asthma metrics are needed to clarify the long-term benefit and cost-effectiveness.","affiliation":"Anaesthesia Specialist. Teaching assistant at the Department of Anaesthesia at King Khalid University, Abha, Saudi Arabia.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Airway inflammation","allergic rhinitis","Immunopathology","Evidence-based Therapy","Unified Airway Disease","Inhaled Corticosteroid Responsiveness"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/02770903.2026.2751828"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-23","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42447991","source":"MED","pmid":"42447991","pmcid":"PMC13580716","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13580716"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jaip.2026.07.007","title":"Asthma Burden in Mild Adult Asthma in the PEARL Survey: Asthma Control and Health Status.","authorString":"Zeiger RS, Zhou B, Puttock EJ, Xie F, Rozema E, Schatz M, Crawford WW, Vollmer WM, Xu S, Chen W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zeiger RS","lastName":"Zeiger","firstName":"Robert S","initials":"RS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allergy, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, San Diego, Calif; Department of Clinical Science, Kaiser Permanente Bernard J. Tyson School of Medicine, Pasadena, Calif; Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif. Electronic address: robert.s.zeiger@kp.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou B","lastName":"Zhou","firstName":"Botao","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Puttock EJ","lastName":"Puttock","firstName":"Eric J","initials":"EJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Xie F","lastName":"Xie","firstName":"Fagen","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Rozema E","lastName":"Rozema","firstName":"Emily","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Schatz M","lastName":"Schatz","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allergy, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, San Diego, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Crawford WW","lastName":"Crawford","firstName":"William W","initials":"WW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allergy, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Harbor City, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Vollmer WM","lastName":"Vollmer","firstName":"William M","initials":"WM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research, Kaiser Permanente Center for Health Research, Portland, Ore."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu S","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Stanley","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen W","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Wansu","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4309346,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract","title":"The journal of allergy and clinical immunology. In practice","essn":"2213-2201","issn":"2213-2198","nlmid":"101597220","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2578-2586.e6","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Asthma classified as \"mild\" is commonly assumed to have a low symptom burden and limited impact on quality of life.<h4>Objective</h4>To characterize patient-reported asthma burden among adults with mild asthma identified using electronic health record-based criteria.<h4>Methods</h4>A total of 3787 adults aged 18-85 years completed a survey assessing asthma burden. Asthma control and health-related quality of life (HRQOL) were measured using the Asthma Impairment and Risk Questionnaire (AIRQ) and the Veterans RAND 12-item Health Survey (VR-12), respectively. Investigator-developed items were used to assess symptoms, functional impact, and health care utilization in the prior year. Associations between patient characteristics and prespecified outcomes-very poorly controlled asthma in the past 2 weeks, symptoms ≥2 days/wk, and ≥2 patient-reported asthma-related emergency department (ED) or urgent care (UC) visits in the prior year-were evaluated using multivariable analyses.<h4>Results</h4>Asthma control was distributed across AIRQ categories as follows: 34.6% well controlled, 32.9% not well controlled, and 32.5% very poorly controlled. Patient-reported burden was notable: 27.6% reported symptoms at least a few times per week, 52.7% reported systemic corticosteroid use ever, and 41.4% reported ≥1 ED/UC visit in the prior year, including 19.7% with ≥2 visits. The mean [standard deviation] VR-12 scores indicated impaired HRQOL (physical component summary: 43.3 [9.7]; mental component summary: 44.7 [11.8]). Hispanic/Latino and Black/African American patients had higher risks of very poorly controlled asthma and ≥2 ED/UC visits in the prior year compared with non-Hispanic White patients.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Among surveyed adults with utilization-defined mild asthma and a recent asthma-related healthcare encounter, substantial patient-reported burden was observed across multiple domains.","affiliation":"Department of Allergy, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, San Diego, Calif; Department of Clinical Science, Kaiser Permanente Bernard J. Tyson School of Medicine, Pasadena, Calif; Department of Research and Evaluation, Kaiser Permanente Southern California, Pasadena, Calif. Electronic address: robert.s.zeiger@kp.org.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NHLBI","grantId":"R01 HL163049","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"R01 HL163049","orderIn":0}]},"meshHea