","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2197688","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-05","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-17","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42833742","source":"MED","pmid":"42833742","doi":"10.1016/j.iac.2026.06.004","title":"Using Asthma Phenotypes and Endotypes to Guide Treatment in Pediatric Asthma.","authorString":"Ngo SY, Anderson WC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ngo SY","lastName":"Ngo","firstName":"Suzanne Y","initials":"SY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Section of Allergy and Immunology, Department of Pediatrics, Children's Hospital Colorado, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, CO, USA; Division of Pulmonary Allergy, and Critical Care, Department of Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, 12700 East 19th Avenue, R2, B164, Room 10C03, Aurora, CO 80045, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Anderson WC 3rd","lastName":"Anderson","firstName":"William C","initials":"WC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Section of Allergy and Immunology, Department of Pediatrics, Children's Hospital Colorado, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, CO, USA. Electronic address: william.anderson@childrenscolorado.org."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"46","journalIssueId":4285354,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Immunol Allergy Clin North Am","title":"Immunology and allergy clinics of North America","essn":"1557-8607","issn":"0889-8561","nlmid":"8805635","isoabbreviation":"Immunol Allergy Clin North Am"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"615-630","abstractText":"Understanding asthma phenotypes, including asthma severity and presence of type 2 (T2) inflammation, can help guide a personalized approach to treatment. Clinically accessible biomarkers, such as blood eosinophils, fractional exhaled nitric oxide, and immunoglobulin E, can help to differentiate between T2-high and non-T2 asthma phenotypes. For mild-to-moderate asthma, inhaled corticosteroids are the mainstay with best response in T2-high asthma. The addition of a long-acting β2-agnoist, however, is the best step-up option for most patients regardless of phenotype. Anti-inflammatory and single maintenance and reliever therapies are also emerging treatment options. For severe asthma, T2 status can guide biological treatment.","affiliation":"Section of Allergy and Immunology, Department of Pediatrics, Children's Hospital Colorado, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, CO, USA; Division of Pulmonary Allergy, and Critical Care, Department of Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, 12700 East 19th Avenue, R2, B164, Room 10C03, Aurora, CO 80045, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Eosinophils","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Th2 Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"major