009049","doi":"10.1016/j.acap.2026.103750","title":"Patterns of Physical Activity Across BMI Categories in Urban Children with Asthma.","authorString":"Serrano-Gonzalez M, Kopel SJ, Tanzer JR, Dunsinger S, Oliva I, Mattice C, Teape D, Jelalian E, Koinis-Mitchell D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Serrano-Gonzalez M","lastName":"Serrano-Gonzalez","firstName":"Monica","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Pediatrics, Hasbro Children's, Brown University Health, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: Monica_serrano@brown.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Kopel SJ","lastName":"Kopel","firstName":"Sheryl J","initials":"SJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Pediatrics, Hasbro Children's, Brown University Health, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Psychiatry and Human Behavior, The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: SJKopel@brownhealth.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Tanzer JR","lastName":"Tanzer","firstName":"Joshua R","initials":"JR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lifespan Biostatistics Core, Brown University Health, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: JTanzer@brownhealth.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Dunsinger S","lastName":"Dunsinger","firstName":"Shira","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Survey Research Center and the Center for Health Promotion and Health Equity in the Department of Behavioral and Social Sciences at Brown University. Electronic address: shira_dunsiger@brown.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Oliva I","lastName":"Oliva","firstName":"Isabelle","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Human Behavior, The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: IOliva@brownhealth.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Mattice C","lastName":"Mattice","firstName":"Carly","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Human Behavior, The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: CMattice@brownhealth.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Teape D","lastName":"Teape","firstName":"Daniella","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Pediatrics, Hasbro Children's, Brown University Health, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: daniella_teape@brown.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Jelalian E","lastName":"Jelalian","firstName":"Elissa","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Psychiatry and Human Behavior, The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Weight Control and Diabetes Research Center, The Miriam Hospital, The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: elissa_jelalian@brown.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Koinis-Mitchell D","lastName":"Koinis-Mitchell","firstName":"Daphne","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Pediatrics, Hasbro Children's, Brown University Health, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Psychiatry and Human Behavior, The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: daphne_koinis-mitchell@brown.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4305452,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Acad Pediatr","title":"Academic pediatrics","essn":"1876-2867","issn":"1876-2859","nlmid":"101499145","isoabbreviation":"Acad Pediatr"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"103750","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>Urban children with asthma engage in less physical activity (PA) than their peers. The relationships between levels of PA, sedentary behavior, and asthma control across the weight continuum has not been sufficiently studied. Our main objectives were to examine, in urban children with asthma: 1) how patterns of activity (sedentary time [ST], light PA [LPA], and moderate-to-vigorous PA [MVPA]) varied according to body mass index (BMI) status, and 2) whether ST decreased as MVPA time increased.<h4>Methods</h4>Urban children (n = 142) ages 7-9 years with persistent asthma and varying weight status completed a 4-week study of asthma and PA. Asthma control was assessed via Asthma Control Test (ACT) scores, and PA was measured via accelerometry. We used dynamic factor analysis within a Structural Equation Modeling framework.<h4>Results</h4>There were no significant differences in time spent in different types of PA based on BMI. Across weight categories, youth who spent more time in MVPA spent more time in LPA, and healthy weight youth who spent more time in MVPA also spent less time in ST (p = 0.02). Youth with OW/OB and better asthma control spent less time in LPA (p = 0.02).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Among urban children with asthma, there are different patterns of PA according to BMI status and level of asthma control. Interventions need to encourage engagement in all levels of activity, not just MVPA, and tailor strategies to optimize PA levels in the context of asthma control in this population.","affiliation":"The Warren Alpert Medical School of Brown University, Providence, RI, United States; Department of Pediatrics, Hasbro Children's, Brown University Health, Providence, RI, United States. Electronic address: Monica_serrano@brown.edu.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Obesity","Asthma","body mass index","Pediatrics","Physical Activity"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.acap.2026.103750"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-10","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42731672","source":"MED","pmid":"42731672","doi":"10.1016/j.anai.2026.09.001","title":"Household food hardship and asthma morbidity among US children with current asthma.","authorString":"Lian D, Lin C, Wei J, Tang Q.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lian D","lastName":"Lian","firstName":"Di","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fujian Children's Hospital (Fujian Branch of Shanghai Children's Medical Center), College of Clinical Medicine for Obstetrics & Gynecology and Pediatrics, Fujian Medical University, Fuzhou, Fujian, China; Children's Medical Center, Peking University First Hospital, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin C","lastName":"Lin","firstName":"Chenye","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fujian Children's Hospital (Fujian Branch of Shanghai Children's Medical Center), College of Clinical Medicine for Obstetrics & Gynecology and Pediatrics, Fujian Medical University, Fuzhou, Fujian, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wei J","lastName":"Wei","firstName":"Jianxing","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fujian Children's Hospital (Fujian Branch of Shanghai Children's Medical Center), College of Clinical Medicine for Obstetrics & Gynecology and Pediatrics, Fujian Medical University, Fuzhou, Fujian, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tang Q","lastName":"Tang","firstName":"Qiuyu","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fujian Children's Hospital (Fujian Branch of Shanghai Children's Medical Center), College of Clinical Medicine for Obstetrics & Gynecology and Pediatrics, Fujian Medical University, Fuzhou, Fujian, China. Electronic address: tell.rain@fjmu.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4252385,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol","title":"Annals of allergy, asthma & immunology : official publication of the American College of Allergy, Asthma, & Immunology","essn":"1534-4436","issn":"1081-1206","nlmid":"9503580","isoabbreviation":"Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S1081-1206(26)00450-3","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Household food hardship may accompany pediatric asthma morbidity; however, national evidence is limited.<h4>Objective</h4>To evaluate the associations between household food hardship and asthma morbidity among children in the United States with current asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>We analyzed children aged 5 to 17 years with current asthma in the 2016 to 2024 National Survey of Children's Health (NSCH) and the 2019 to 2024 National Health Interview Survey (NHIS). The NSCH exposure captured difficulty affording preferred food quality or sufficient quantity over 12 months; NHIS compared marginal, low, or very low food security with high food security using a 10-item, 30-day scale. Outcomes were caregiver-reported moderate or severe asthma (NSCH), asthma attack, and asthma-related emergency department or urgent care use (NHIS). Survey-weighted modified Poisson regression estimated adjusted prevalence ratios (aPRs) and predictive margins estimated adjusted prevalence differences (aPDs) with 95% CIs.<h4>Results</h4>The NSCH model included 23,466 children (6699 events), whereas NHIS models included 2774 children (with 1201 attacks) and 2775 children (with 438 acute-care events). Half were aged 5 to 11 years; 54.9% to 57.6% were male; 59.3% to 80.0% had a household adult with more than a high-school education; and 92.6% to 97.5% were insured. Food hardship was associated with moderate or severe asthma (aPR, 1.23; 95% CI, 1.12-1.35; aPD, 6.8 percentage points; 95% CI, 3.8-9.9), attacks (aPR, 1.30; 95% CI, 1.16-1.46; aPD, 12.5 percentage points; 95% CI, 6.7-18.3), and acute-care use (aPR, 1.31; 95% CI, 1.04-1.65; aPD, 4.9 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.6-9.3). At the highest level of hardship, the aPRs were 1.52, 1.55, and 1.92, respectively.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Household food hardship was associated with caregiver-reported asthma morbidity across 2 national surveys.","affiliation":"Fujian Children's Hospital (Fujian Branch of Shanghai Children's Medical Center), College of Clinical Medicine for Obstetrics & Gynecology and Pediatrics, Fujian Medical University, Fuzhou, Fujian, China; Children's Medical Center, Peking University First Hospital, Beijing, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Fujian Provincial Natural Science Foundation","grantId":"2025J011201","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","a