horAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Institute of Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Population Health Data Center, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Mu HW","lastName":"Mu","firstName":"Han-Wei","initials":"HW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7449-0327"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Institute of Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, China Medical University Hospital, Taichung, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Su KW","lastName":"Su","firstName":"Kuan-Wen","initials":"KW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8841-0108"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy, Asthma and Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Linkou Chang Gung Memorial Hospital, Taoyuan, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, College of Medicine, Chang Gung University, Taoyuan, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Shao SC","lastName":"Shao","firstName":"Shih-Chieh","initials":"SC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4867-6686"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Institute of Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Population Health Data Center, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Lai EC","lastName":"Lai","firstName":"Edward Chia-Cheng","initials":"EC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5852-7652"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Institute of Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Population Health Data Center, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8841-0108"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5852-7652"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7449-0327"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8640-3593"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2841-0338"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4867-6686"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9919-2162"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4275542,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Clin Pharmacol Ther","title":"Clinical pharmacology and therapeutics","essn":"1532-6535","issn":"0009-9236","nlmid":"0372741","isoabbreviation":"Clin Pharmacol Ther"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Emerging evidence suggests potential respiratory benefits from sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitors, a glucose-lowering agent. However, the effects of these agents on asthma exacerbations remain unclear. A retrospective cohort study used Taiwan's National Health Insurance Research Database, covering 2016-2020, and included adults with type 2 diabetes and asthma. Exposure was defined as the initiation of SGLT2 or dipeptidyl peptidase 4 (DPP4) inhibitors, and the primary outcome was asthma exacerbation, defined as an asthma diagnosis accompanied by systemic corticosteroid use. Demographic characteristics, comorbidities, the Adapted Diabetes Complications Severity Index and concomitant medications were used to estimate propensity scores, and inverse probability of treatment weighting was applied. Altogether, 3,057 initiators of SGLT2 inhibitors and 15,050 initiators of DPP4 inhibitors were identified and followed from the index date until the occurrence of asthma exacerbation, death, or the end of the observation period (May 31, 2020). Over a mean follow-up of 2.06 years, initiation of SGLT2 inhibitors was associated with a lower risk of asthma exacerbation (HR: 0.87, 95% CI: 0.81-0.93), corresponding to incidence rates of 3.73 vs. 4.03 events per 100 person-years. The results were consistent across care settings, including outpatient- (HR: 0.88, 95% CI: 0.80-0.96), inpatient- (HR: 0.88, 95% CI: 0.78-1.00), and emergency department visits (HR: 0.72, 95% CI: 0.63-0.83), as also across subgroup and sensitivity analyses. Among adults with type 2 diabetes and asthma, initiation of SGLT2 inhibitors was associated with a reduced risk of asthma exacerbation, highlighting a potential respiratory benefit that warrants confirmation through future clinical trials.","affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Institute of Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Science and Technology Council of Taiwan","grantId":"NSTC 115-2628-B-006-002-","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Higher Education Sprout Project, Ministry of Education to the Headquarters of University Advancement at National Cheng Kung University (NCKU)","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Science and Technology Council of Taiwan","grantId":"115-2320-B-006-014-MY3","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/cpt.70514"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-09","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42653648","source":"MED","pmid":"42653648","pmcid":"PMC13516457","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13516457"]},"doi":"10.3390/ph19081149","title":"Global Prevalence of Asthma in Adults with Atopic Dermatitis and the Atopic Dermatitis-Asthma Association.","authorString":"Xu H, Liu Y, Tu J, Liu M, Zhao L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xu H","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Haojie","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-3275-261X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Peking University Third Hospital, Beijing 100191, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy Administration and Clinical Pharmacy, Peking University, Beijing 100191, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu Y","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Yichen","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-3801-0881"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Peking University Third Hospital, Beijing 100191, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tu J","lastName":"Tu","firstName":"Jiachen","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Peking University Third Hospital, Beijing 100191, China."},{"affiliation":"Research Group of Jian Gong on Pharmacoepidemiology and Clinical Drug Evaluation, School of Clinical Pharmacy, Shenyang Pharmaceutical University, Shenyang 110016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu M","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Mengmeng","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9872-4212"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Peking University Third Hospital, Beijing 100191, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao L","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Libo","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Peking University Third Hospital, Beijing 100191, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy Administration and Clinical Pharmacy, Peking University, Beijing 100191, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9872-4212"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-3275-261X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-3801-0881"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4234003,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pharmaceuticals (Basel)","title":"Pharmaceuticals (Basel, Switzerland)","essn":"1424-8247","issn":"1424-8247","nlmid":"101238453","isoabbreviation":"Pharmaceuticals (Basel)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1149","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Atopic dermatitis (AD) and asthma are common type 2 inflammatory diseases linked by the atopic march. In adults with AD, the prevalence of comorbid asthma and the strength of the AD-asthma association remain uncertain, with marked heterogeneity across studies. Moreover, the role of modern systemic therapies in this comorbidity is increasingly debated. <b>Objective:</b> To estimate the global pooled prevalence of asthma in adults with AD, quantify the AD-asthma association, and discuss the potential impact of targeted therapies (dupilumab, abrocitinib, omalizumab) on asthma comorbidity. <b>Methods:</b> Systematic review and random-effects meta-analysis (ID CRD420261304804) of observational studies from PubMed, EMBASE, and Cochrane Library (inception to 19 January 2026). Pooled prevalence and odds ratios (ORs) were calculated, with subgroup analyses by region, ethnicity, and disease definition. <b>Results:</b> Seventy-five studies were included. The global pooled prevalence of asthma in adults with AD was 25.9% (95% CI 22.1-30.0%; I<sup>2</sup> = 99.9%). AD was significantly associated with asthma (OR 3.64, 95% CI 2.89-4.57; I<sup>2</sup> = 98.6%). Prevalence varied from 9.3% in Asia to 63.2% in South America. Although dupilumab and other targeted agents are effective in both diseases, isolated reports of new-onset asthma after treatment were identified; these cases more likely reflect the natural progression of AD or unmasking of pre-existing asthma than a direct adverse drug reaction. <b>Conclusions:</b> Asthma is highly prevalent in adults with AD and is strongly associated with AD. Geographic and ethnic disparities call for stratified screening. Clinicians should be aware that new-onset respiratory symptoms during targeted therapy may represent AD-related comorbidity rather than drug-induced asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Peking University Third Hospital, Beijing 100191, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Prevalence","Asthma","Pharmacotherapy","Atopic Dermatitis","Comorbidity"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ph19081149"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13516457"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13516457?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-24","firstPublicat