astName":"Haggie","firstName":"Stuart","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Paediatrics, Shoalhaven District Memorial Hospital, Illawarra Shoalhaven Local Health District, Nowra, NSW, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Respiratory Department, Children's Hospital Westmead, Sydney Children's Hospital Network, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Doyle AK","lastName":"Doyle","firstName":"Aunty Kerrie","initials":"AK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Discipline of Indigenous Health, School of Medicine, Western Sydney University, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Gould B","lastName":"Gould","firstName":"Bronwyn","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Health, University of Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Woolfenden S","lastName":"Woolfenden","firstName":"Sue","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sydney Institute for Women, Children and Families, Sydney Local Health District, Sydney, NSW, Australia."},{"affiliation":"General Practice, Paddington, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ward F","lastName":"Ward","firstName":"Flic","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Parent of child with asthma."}]}},{"fullName":"Flynn A","lastName":"Flynn","firstName":"Anthony","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Asthma Australia, Chatswood, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Lingam R","lastName":"Lingam","firstName":"Raghu","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Discipline of Paediatrics and Child Health, School of Clinical Medicine, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Jaffe A","lastName":"Jaffe","firstName":"Adam","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1963-5415"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Discipline of Paediatrics and Child Health, School of Clinical Medicine, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Respiratory Department, Sydney Children's Hospital Randwick, Sydney Children's Hospital Network, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Homaira N","lastName":"Homaira","firstName":"Nusrat","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Discipline of Paediatrics and Child Health, School of Clinical Medicine, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Respiratory Department, Sydney Children's Hospital Randwick, Sydney Children's Hospital Network, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1963-5415"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0600-7265"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"12","journalIssueId":4253159,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"ERJ Open Res","title":"ERJ open research","essn":"2312-0541","issn":"2312-0541","nlmid":"101671641","isoabbreviation":"ERJ Open Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"389-2026","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Asthma is a leading cause of childhood hospitalisation and emergency department (ED) presentations in Australia. Barriers persist in accessing paediatric asthma care, particularly in rural areas. We implemented and evaluated a multicomponent model to reduce preventable hospital presentations across rural New South Wales.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted pre-post evaluation of children aged 5-12 years with asthma exacerbations across 13 hospitals in four rural NSW health districts. Children received: 1) a standardised discharge pack, 2) general practitioners (GP) and school linkage, 3) text reminders, and 4) nurse-led virtual home visits for uncontrolled asthma. Primary outcome was healthcare utilisation (ED presentations, admissions and GP visits) from electronic records, comparing matched pre-post periods at 1, 3, 6 and 12 months. Secondary outcomes included asthma control and quality of life at 6 and 12 months. Analyses used generalised estimating equations and mixed-effects models to calculate adjusted rate ratios (aRRs) and mean differences.<h4>Results</h4>188 children participated (62% male, mean age 7.6 years). ED presentations decreased 93% at 1 month (aRR 0.07, 95% confidence interval 0.04-0.12), 85% at 3 months (aRR 0.15, 0.10-0.22), 78% at 6 months (aRR 0.22, 0.16-0.30) and 69% at 12 months (aRR 0.31, 0.25-0.40). Hospital admissions decreased 97%, 95%, 90% and 81%. Uncontrolled asthma decreased from 73% to 37% (6 months) and 30% (12 months). Asthma control and quality of life improved significantly at both timepoints.<h4>Conclusion</h4>This multisite study achieved sustained reductions in preventable hospitalisations and meaningful improvements in asthma control and quality of life. This model offers a replicable approach for underserved populations.","affiliation":"Discipline of Paediatrics and Child Health, School of Clinical Medicine, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/23120541.00389-2026"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13617586"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13617586?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-30","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42839791","source":"MED","pmid":"42839791","pmcid":"PMC13728535","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13728535"]},"doi":"10.1002/resp.70322","title":"Asthma: From Phenotypes to Endotypes-Beyond Type 2 Inflammation.","authorString":"Fernando M, King GG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fernando M","lastName":"Fernando","firstName":"Mario","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9124-5585"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, Royal North Shore Hospital, St Leonards, New South Wales, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Northern Clinical School, Faculty of Health and Medicine, University of Sydney, Camperdown, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"King GG","lastName":"King","firstName":"Gregory G","initials":"GG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3024-2380"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, Royal North Shore Hospital, St Leonards, New South Wales, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Northern Clinical School, Faculty of Health and Medicine, University of Sydney, Camperdown, Australia."},{"affiliation":"The Woolcock Institute of Medical Research, Macquarie University, Macquarie Park, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3024-2380"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9124-5585"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"31","journalIssueId":4305726,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respirology","title":"Respirology (Carlton, Vic.)","essn":"1440-1843","issn":"1323-7799","nlmid":"9616368","isoabbreviation":"Respirology"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1077-1090","abstractText":"Asthma is a heterogeneous syndrome featuring variable pathophysiology, clinical presentation and treatment response. This review examines a comprehensive endotype-phenotype framework for asthma assessment with an emphasis on characteristics beyond T2 inflammatory status alone. Endo-phenotype assessment can be performed across three domains: clinical (symptoms, exacerbations, co-morbidities), structural and physiological, and inflammatory endotype. The clinical domain includes mucus hypersecretion, perception of airway narrowing, and obesity-related asthma. Airway remodelling is the central, structural endo-phenotype that defines asthma, given its relationship with fixed airflow limitation, airway hyperresponsiveness, small airway dysfunction, lung function decline, and asthma severity. However, it is the most difficult parameter to measure, and it is increasingly recognised that there is a critical need to be able to measure it longitudinally, beyond bronchial biopsy. Finally, T2-high asthma is relatively well characterised with several effective anti-inflammatory therapies available, including a range of biologics. In contrast, T2-low asthma remains a heterogenous and potentially difficult to characterise endo-phenotype, although some treatment studies have demonstrated benefit when subjects have been appropriately characterised and selected. Emerging multi-omic approaches may enhance the detailed characterisation of patients in future. Adopting an endo-phenotype framework allows clinicians and researchers to confront the complexity of asthma, better manage patients with a precision medicine approach and enhance mechanistic understanding, which is critical for new therapeutics to be developed for asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, Royal North Shore Hospital, St Leonards, New South Wales, Australia.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Health and Medical Research Council","grantId":"2050302","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Inflammation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Phenotype"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Airway Remodeling","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Phenotype","Asthma","Airway Remodelling","Personalised Medicine","Endotype","Multi‐omics","T2‐high Asthma","T2‐low Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/resp.70322"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13728535"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13728535?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-03","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-07","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42964482","source":"MED","pmid":"42964482","doi":"10.5409/wjcp.124311","title":"Asthma burden and its impact on health-related quality of life among school adolescents in Anuradhapura district, Sri Lanka.","authorString":"Kumari MV, Amarasiri L, Rajindrajith S, Illangasinghe T, Devanarayana NM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kumari MV","lastName":"Kumari","firstName":"Manori Vijaya","initials":"MV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Faculty of Medicine and Allied Sciences, Rajarata University of Sri Lanka, Anuradhapura 50008, North Central Province, Sri Lanka."}]}},{"fullName":"Amarasiri L","lastName":"Amarasiri","firstName":"Lakmali","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Faculty of Medicine, University of Colombo, Colombo 00800, Western Province, Sri Lanka."}]}},{"fullName":"Rajindrajith S","lastName":"Rajindrajith","firstName":"Shaman","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Paediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Colombo, Colombo 00800, Western Province, Sri Lanka."}]}},{"fullName":"Illangasinghe T","lastName":"Illangasinghe","firstName":"Thilini","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Faculty of Medicine and Allied Sciences, Rajarata University of Sri Lanka, Anuradhapura 50008, North Central Province, Sri Lanka."}]}},{"fullName":"Devanarayana NM","lastName":"Devanarayana","firstName":"Niranga Manjuri","initials":"NM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Faculty of Medicine, University of Kelaniya, Ragama 11010, Western Province, Sri Lanka. niranga@kln.ac.lk."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4303258,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"World J Clin Pediatr","title":"World journal of clinical pediatrics","essn":"2219-2808","issn":"2219-2808","nlmid":"101627548","isoabbreviation":"World J Clin Pediatr"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-12-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"124311","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Asthma is a common chronic airway disease in adolescents, causing respiratory symptoms such as wheeze, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and cough. Asthma significantly impacts health-related quality of life (HRQoL). Its prevalence and symptom patterns in Southeast Asia, particularly among Sri Lankan adolescents, are not well known.<h4>Aim</h4>To assess the prevalence of asthma and its impact on the quality of life in Sri Lankan adolescents.<h4>Methods</h4>A cross-sectional survey was conducted among 1099 adolescents aged 13-15 years in mixed-gender schools in the Anuradhapura district, Sri Lanka (46.4% boys, mean age 14.03 years). The International Study of Asthma and Allergies in Childhood questionnaire was used to assess asthma prevalence. Students with physician-diagnosed asthma and wheezing in the previous 12 months were categorized as having current asthma. The PedsQL inventory was used to assess HRQoL.<h4>Results</h4>The prevalence of current asthma, physician-diagnosed asthma, and severe asthma was 13.8% [95% confidence interval (CI): 11.8-15.8], 20.2% (95%CI: 17.8-22.5), and 4.6% (95%CI: 3.4-5.8), respectively. Exercise-induced wheeze, sleep, and speech disturbances were significantly associated with asthma (<i>P</i> < 0.001). Overall, HRQoL was consistently reduced across all asthma diagnostic categories (current asthma, physician-diagnosed asthma, and severe asthma) compared with adolescents without asthma (<i>P</i> < 0.001).<h4>Conclusion</h4>There is a significant burden of asthma among adolescents, with 13.8% having current asthma and 4.6% having severe asthma. Adolescents with current asthma have reduced HRQoL compared to non-asthmatics, highlighting the need for comprehensive management strategies beyond symptom control.","affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Faculty of Medicine and Allied Sciences, Rajarata University of Sri Lanka, Anuradhapura 50008, North Central Province, Sri Lanka.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Quality of life","Adolescents","Wheeze","Health-related Quality Of Life"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.5409/wjcp.124311"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-31","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-12-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-12-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42813456","source":"MED","pmid":"42813456","pmcid":"PMC13625169","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13625169"]},"doi":"10.1002/14651858.cd016394","title":"Systemic azoles for asthma with fungal sensitisation.","authorString":"Fukuda Y, Homma T, Miyamoto Y, Watanabe N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fukuda Y","lastName":"Fukuda","firstName":"Yosuke","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"