"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8552-4510"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Sciences, Respiratory-, Allergy- and Sleep Research, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Malinovschi A","lastName":"Malinovschi","firstName":"Andrei","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4098-7765"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Sciences, Clinical Physiology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Janson C","lastName":"Janson","firstName":"Christer","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5093-6980"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Sciences, Respiratory-, Allergy- and Sleep Research, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5093-6980"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8509-7603"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2452-7347"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4098-7765"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5306-1697"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5700-7284"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7227-5113"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7405-1682"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8552-4510"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2057-8558"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4095019,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy","title":"Journal of asthma and allergy","essn":"1178-6965","issn":"1178-6965","nlmid":"101543450","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"639747","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>This study compared sleep quality and insomnia symptoms in individuals with and without asthma and identified potentially treatable traits that could be used to improve sleep in asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>This cross-sectional analysis included 28,828 participants (aged 50-64). Post-bronchodilator forced expiratory volume (FEV<sub>1</sub>) and forced vital capacity (FVC) were measured. Questionnaires assessed physician-diagnosed asthma, asthma-related symptoms, exacerbations, allergic rhinitis, chronic rhinosinusitis (CRS), nocturnal breathing disorders and reflux (snoring, sleep apnea, nocturnal gastroesophageal reflux [nGER]), and sleep quality. Insomnia symptoms included difficulties initiating sleep (DIS), difficulties maintaining sleep (DMS), and early morning awakening (EMA).<h4>Results</h4>Current asthma was associated with impaired sleep quality (OR=1.56; [95% CI:] 1.39-1.75), DIS (1.59; [1.40-1.81]), DMS (1.36; [1.22-1.51]), EMA (1.31; [1.16-1.48]), and any insomnia symptom (1.38; [1.25-1.52]). Among individuals with asthma, allergic rhinitis was linked to impaired sleep quality (1.28; [1.01-1.62]), while CRS, asthma symptoms, exacerbations, snoring, sleep apnea and nGER were associated with impaired sleep quality and insomnia symptoms. Those with uncontrolled asthma, CRS, or nocturnal breathing disorders and reflux had higher odds of impaired sleep quality and any insomnia symptom, with the strongest associations in those with all three conditions (5.68; [3.47-9.29] and 3.63; [2.26-5.85], respectively).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Asthma is associated with impaired sleep quality and insomnia symptoms in middle-aged adults. Our study also indicates that in asthma, impaired sleep quality and insomnia symptoms are linked to three potentially treatable traits, namely lack of asthma control, upper airway disorders, and nocturnal breathing disorders and reflux.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Sciences, Respiratory-, Allergy- and Sleep Research, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Insomnia","Allergies","Asthma Control","Sleep Quality","Nocturnal Breathing Disorders"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JAA.S639747"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13738100"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13738100?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-06","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-07","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42965826","source":"MED","pmid":"42965826","doi":"10.1007/s41030-026-00399-2","title":"Depression, Anxiety, and Healthcare Utilization in Adults with Uncontrolled Asthma.","authorString":"Yohannes AM, Rane D, Hannania NA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yohannes AM","lastName":"Yohannes","firstName":"Abebaw Mengistu","initials":"AM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8341-270X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physical Therapy, University of Alabama at Birmingham, 1716 9th Avenue South, Birmingham, AL, 35296, USA. amyohann@uab.edu."},{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Allergy, and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, University of Alabama at Birmingham, Birmingham, AL, USA. amyohann@uab.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Rane D","lastName":"Rane","firstName":"Dhanashree","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physical Therapy, University of Alabama at Birmingham, 1716 9th Avenue South, Birmingham, AL, 35296, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hannania NA","lastName":"Hannania","firstName":"Nicola Alexander","initials":"NA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Baylor College of Medicine, Houston, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8341-270X"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4285398,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pulm Ther","title":"Pulmonary therapy","essn":"2364-1746","issn":"2364-1754","nlmid":"101687144","isoabbreviation":"Pulm Ther"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Uncontrolled asthma is often associated with recurrent acute exacerbations, progressive and chronic systemic inflammation and decreased lung function, impaired quality of life, and healthcare utilization. A considerable proportion of adult patients with uncontrolled asthma exhibit comorbid elevated symptoms of depression and anxiety. These symptoms are often underrecognized and undertreated in adults with asthma. We examined and synthesized the available literature on the efficacy of pulmonary rehabilitation, cognitive behavioral therapy, and antidepressant drug therapy, and healthcare utilization in adults with uncontrolled asthma with comorbid depression and anxiety.<h4>Interpretation and discussion</h4>The prevalence of anxiety in adults with uncontrolled asthma ranges from 25 to 54%, and depression ranges from 14 to 50%. To date, the available literature on the efficacy of pulmonary rehabilitation, cognitive behavioral therapy, and antidepressant drug therapy in the treatment of anxiety and depression is limited. Untreated depression and anxiety are associated with recurrent episodes of acute exacerbations, hospitalizations, poorer quality of life, longer hospital stays, and emergency healthcare utilization. Therefore, well-controlled randomized controlled trials are needed to improve the evidence base for optimal care of adults with uncontrolled asthma and ideally to reduce healthcare utilization.","affiliation":"Department of Physical Therapy, University of Alabama at Birmingham, 1716 9th Avenue South, Birmingham, AL, 35296, USA. amyohann@uab.edu.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Depression","Anxiety","Social Isolation","Healthcare Utilization","Uncontrolled Asthma"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s41030-026-00399-2"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-31","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42986107","source":"MED","pmid":"42986107","doi":"10.1080/14656566.2026.2760015","title":"Emerging pharmacotherapeutic options for adult asthma.","authorString":"Hassan W, Hansi RK, Ranjbar M, Whetstone CE, Gauvreau GM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hassan W","lastName":"Hassan","firstName":"Wafa","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Hansi RK","lastName":"Hansi","firstName":"Ravneet K","initials":"RK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranjbar M","lastName":"Ranjbar","firstName":"Maral","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Whetstone CE","lastName":"Whetstone","firstName":"Christiane E","initials":"CE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Gauvreau GM","lastName":"Gauvreau","firstName":"Gail M","initials":"GM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4304316,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Expert Opin Pharmacother","title":"Expert opinion on pharmacotherapy","essn":"1744-7666","issn":"1465-6566","nlmid":"100897346","isoabbreviation":"Expert Opin Pharmacother"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-33","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Current biologics have transformed asthma management; however, incomplete responses in some patients with type 2-high disease and the absence of approved targeted therapies for type 2-low asthma highlight the need for novel treatment strategies summarized in this review.<h4>Areas covered</h4>This review examines literature gathered from searches of PubMed, ClinicalTrials.gov and conference abstracts from 2014 to the present for listings of approved biologics and investigational therapies across the severe asthma treatment landscape. This review includes clinical trials, preclinical studies, and guideline recommendations to evaluate established and emerging approaches, including next-generation anti-IgE, anti-IL-5/IL-5R, anti-IL-4/IL-13, and anti-TSLP therapies, together with novel targets that are in early-stage clinical trials such as IL-33/ST2, mast cell pathways, and transcription factor inhibitors.<h4>Expert opinion</h4>There is a shift from single-cytokine blockade toward broader modulation of airway inflammation through multi-specific antibodies and intracellular signaling targets. Major unmet needs remain in type 2-low asthma, where no biologic is specifically approved for this endotype. Future progress will depend on improved biomarker-guided patient stratification and the development of therapies capable of achieving individualized, mechanism-driven disease modification.","affiliation":"Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Immunoglobulin E","Airway inflammation","eosinophils","Biologics","Alarmins","Asthma Endotype","T2 Cytokines"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14656566.2026.2760015"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-06","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42754147","source":"MED","pmid":"42754147","doi":"10.1016/j.jaip.2026.08.045","title":"Treatable Traits in Pregnant Women With Asthma: a Retrospective Study of Prevalence and Associations With Exacerbation and Poor Asthma Control.","authorString":"Joshi E, Gibson PG, Brew BK, Jensen ME, Barker D, Nolan E, McDonald VM, Murphy VE.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Joshi E","lastName":"Joshi","firstName":"Esha","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Breathing and Lung Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Gibson PG","lastName":"Gibson","firstName":"Peter G","initials":"PG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5865-489X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Breathing and Lung Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, John Hunter Hospital, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Brew BK","lastName":"Brew","firstName":"Bronwyn K","initials":"BK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9885-8261"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Breathing and Lung Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Jensen ME","lastName":"Jensen","firstName":"Megan E","initials":"ME","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Breathing and Lung Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Barker D","lastName":"Barker","firstName":"Daniel","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Data Sciences, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Nolan E","lastName":"Nolan","firstName":"Erin","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Data Sciences, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"McDonald VM","lastName":"McDonald","firstName":"Vanessa M","initials":"VM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; School of Nursing and Midwifery, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Murphy VE","lastName":"Murphy","firstName":"Vanessa E","initials":"VE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Breathing and Lung Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. Electronic address: vanessa.murphy@newcastle.edu.au."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5865-489X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9885-8261"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4259321,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract","title":"The journal of allergy and clinical immunology. In practice","essn":"2213-2201","issn":"2213-2198","nlmid":"101597220","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S2213-2198(26)00769-5","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Treatable traits personalize asthma management, yet their prevalence and associations with exacerbations and asthma control in pregnancy are understudied.<h4>Objective</h4>To determine the prevalence of 18 treatable traits and their associations with exacerbations and asthma control in pregnant women with asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>We examined Breathing for Life Trial clinical records to identify 18 potential treatable traits from mid-pregnancy. Regression models adjusted for confounders determined associations with future exacerbations during pregnancy. In a subgroup, traits associated with asthma control were assessed at multiple time points during pregnancy using a generalized linear mixed model.<h4>Results</h4>Pregnant women (n = 761) with mild to moderate asthma exhibited a median of 36% of possible traits assessed. Prevalent treatable traits included no asthma action plan (84.2%), inhaler device polypharmacy (82.1%), and iron deficiency (70.4%). A history of exacerbation (adjusted odds ratio [OR] = aOR 2.47; 95% CI, 1.61-3.78; P < .002) and significant nausea (OR = 1.88; 95% CI, 1.24-2.85; P = .039) were associated with increased exacerbation whereas a history of exacerbation was associated with increasing exacerbations (adjusted incidence rate ratio = 2.26; 95% CI, 1.51-3.40; P = .002) after adjusting for confounders and multiple comparisons. In mid-pregnancy, inhaler device polypharmacy (OR = 2.72; 95% CI, 2.08-3.57; P = .002), current smoking (OR = 2.51; 95% CI, 1.36-4.63; P = .039), and a history of exacerbations (OR = 1.95; 95% CI, 1.26-3.02; P = .039) were associated with poorer asthma control.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Behavioral and risk factor traits are common in asthma during pregnancy. A history of exacerbation and significant nausea were associated with increased exacerbation risk. Inhaler device polypharmacy, smoking, and a history of exacerbations were associated with poor asthma control in mid-pregnancy.","affiliation":"School of Medicine and Public Health, University of Newcastle, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia; Breathing and Lung Health Research Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Pregnancy","Asthma","Pulmonary","Exacerbation","Asthma Control","Extrapulmonary","Treatable Traits","Behavioral And Risk Factor"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaip.2026.08.045"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-17","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-18","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42919833","source":"MED","pmid":"42919833","pmcid":"PMC13694709","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13694709"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41533-026-00571-w","title":"Assessment of teachers' knowledge, awareness, and response to students with asthma in Greater Cairo schools.","authorString":"Abdel-Hamid HM, Elsayed RM, El-Sayed Abd El-Ghani S, Zein MM, Hassaan HM, Elshenawy A, Abdelmoneim MA, Ismail JH, Ahmed Abd Elaleem FE, Mohamed HAEW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Abdel-Hamid HM","lastName":"Abdel-Hamid","firstName":"Hoda M","initials":"HM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4536-7941"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chest Diseases, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt. Hodam.mabdelhamid@cu.edu.eg."}]}},{"fullName":"Elsayed RM","lastName":"Elsayed","firstName":"Radwa M","initials":"RM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3219-3703"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"El-Sayed Abd El-Ghani S","lastName":"El-Sayed Abd El-Ghani","firstName":"Sara","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3619-3740"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, Clinical Hematology unit, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Zein MM","lastName":"Zein","firstName":"Marwa M","initials":"MM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7908-4021"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Public Health and Community Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Hassaan HM","lastName":"Hassaan","firstName":"Hebatallah M","initials":"HM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-3300-040X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Elshenawy A","lastName":"Elshenawy","firstName":"Abdelhameed","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3936-5695"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care and Emergency Nursing, Faculty of Nursing, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Abdelmoneim MA","lastName":"Abdelmoneim","firstName":"Mostafa Alaa","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-5519-4496"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Intern, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Ismail JH","lastName":"Ismail","firstName":"Jumana Hesham","initials":"JH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5019-5502"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chest Diseases, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahmed Abd Elaleem FE","lastName":"Ahmed Abd Elaleem","firstName":"Fatma Elzahraa","initials":"FE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5037-8357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chest Diseases, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"Mohamed HAEW","lastName":"Mohamed","firstName":"Hadeel Abd El Wahab","initials":"HAEW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7070-1027"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chest Diseases, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5019-5502"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7908-4021"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3219-3703"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5037-8357"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7070-1027"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3619-3740"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4536-7941"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-5519-4496"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-3300-040X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3936-5695"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"36","journalIssueId":4298669,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Prim Care Respir Med","title":"NPJ primary care respiratory medicine","essn":"2055-1010","issn":"2055-1010","nlmid":"101631999","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Prim Care Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"70","abstractText":"Bronchial asthma remains a significant public health challenge, especially among school-aged children. Schoolteachers are often the initial responders to asthma attacks, making their role vital for early symptom recognition and first aid provision. Therefore, this study aimed to explore the knowledge, awareness, and response of schoolteachers to students with asthma. Between March 2024 and October 2024, a self-administered questionnaire was created on Google Forms and distributed to schoolteachers through WhatsApp groups of private and public schools across different districts in Greater Cairo. The questionnaire consisted of three sections assessing: (1) teachers' experience and demographics, (2) basic knowledge of asthma, and (3) knowledge related to first aid response in an asthma attack. A total of 634 schoolteachers participated in the study. Of these, 361 (57%) reported having previously taught students with bronchial asthma, whereas only 176 (27.8%) had witnessed a student having an acute asthma attack before. A majority of participants (445/634, 70.2%) correctly identified the short-acting beta agonist (SABA) inhaler or nebulizer as appropriate first aid for an acute asthma attack. However, only 187/634 (29.5%) reported knowing the correct technique for using SABA inhalers or nebulizers during an asthma attack. More than half of the participants, 362/634 (57.1%), provided appropriate answers regarding the etiology of the disease, specifically whether it was a contagious disease or not. There was no statistically significant association between teachers' knowledge level and their sociodemographic characteristics. Teachers in Greater Cairo demonstrated fair knowledge and awareness regarding asthma. However, they did not fully understand appropriate first-aid responses, a gap that underscores the need for more asthma education programs.","affiliation":"Department of Chest Diseases, Faculty of Medicine, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt. Hodam.mabdelhamid@cu.edu.eg.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"maj