,"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical and Biological Sciences, University of Turin, Turin, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Severe Asthma, Rare Lung Disease and Respiratory Pathophysiology Unit, San Luigi Gonzaga University Hospital, Orbassano, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Translational Pharmacology, National Research Council (IFT-CNR), Palermo, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Bertolini F","lastName":"Bertolini","firstName":"Francesca","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical and Biological Sciences, University of Turin, Turin, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Poto R","lastName":"Poto","firstName":"Remo","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4723-0167"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Medical Sciences, University of Naples Federico II, Naples, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Chan R","lastName":"Chan","firstName":"Rory","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Dundee, School of Medicine, Dundee, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Varricchi G","lastName":"Varricchi","firstName":"Gilda","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Medical Sciences, University of Naples Federico II, Naples, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Center for Basic and Clinical Immunology Research (CISI), University of Naples Federico II, Naples, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Giovannini M","lastName":"Giovannini","firstName":"Mattia","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9568-6882"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Sciences, University of Florence, Florence, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Allergy Unit, Meyer Children's Hospital IRCCS, Florence, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8467-2557"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9568-6882"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4517-1749"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4723-0167"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1826-5018"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3953-9225"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"20","journalIssueId":4095683,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Biologics","title":"Biologics : targets & therapy","essn":"1177-5491","issn":"1177-5475","nlmid":"101321511","isoabbreviation":"Biologics"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"624174","abstractText":"Allergen immunotherapy (AIT) is the only available disease-modifying treatment for IgE-mediated allergic disease. Unlike conventional pharmacotherapy, AIT targets the underlying immune dysregulation driving allergic asthma (AA), with the potential to induce sustained off-treatment benefits. This narrative review examines the available evidence from both randomized controlled trials (RCTs) and real-world evidence to provide an updated and balanced overview of the clinical role of AIT in AA across different age groups. RCTs indicate that AIT can improve asthma symptom scores, reduce medication requirements and exacerbation risk, with additional steroid-sparing effects; however, findings are not consistent across trials. The strongest evidence is confined to house dust mite SLIT tablets, and the most recent Cochrane review judged the evidence insufficient for firm conclusions. Observational database studies enrolling up to 445,000 patients are consistent with these findings, reporting reductions in asthma events, medication use and treatment step-up, although their design precludes causal inference. Beyond symptomatic control, AIT has demonstrated a preventive role by interrupting the allergic trajectory, reducing the risk of new-onset asthma in patients with allergic rhinitis, and slowing progression to more severe asthma phenotypes. Long-term follow-up studies, mostly small and open in design, suggest that benefit may persist for up to 15 years after AIT discontinuation. Preliminary uncontrolled evidence also suggests that AIT may be feasible in selected patients with well-controlled severe asthma. In conclusion, AIT represents a uniquely positioned, disease-modifying strategy in the management of AA, with potential benefits spanning symptom control, prevention of disease onset and progression, and long-term benefits persisting after treatment discontinuation.","affiliation":"Department of Health Sciences, University of Florence, Florence, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Allergic asthma","Slit","Allergen Immunotherapy","Scit","Disease-modifying","Asthma Prevention"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/BTT.S624174"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13701092"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13701092?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-27","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42358591","source":"MED","pmid":"42358591","pmcid":"PMC13290962","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13290962"]},"doi":"10.3389/falgy.2026.1840756","title":"Cat exposure and asthma outcomes in a cohort of children with asthma and allergy.","authorString":"Putri RR, Lundholm C, Hedman A, Mubanga M, Karim H, Konradsen JR, Almqvist C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Putri RR","lastName":"Putri","firstName":"Resthie R","initials":"RR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Lundholm C","lastName":"Lundholm","firstName":"Cecilia","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Hedman A","lastName":"Hedman","firstName":"Anna","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Mubanga M","lastName":"Mubanga","firstName":"Mwenya","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Strategic Information Unit, The Centre for Infectious Disease Research in Zambia, Lusaka, Zambia."}]}},{"fullName":"Karim H","lastName":"Karim","firstName":"Hanna","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Pediatric Allergy and Pulmonology Unit, Astrid Lindgren Children's Hospital, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Konradsen JR","lastName":"Konradsen","firstName":"Jon R","initials":"JR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Pediatric Allergy and Pulmonology Unit, Astrid Lindgren Children's Hospital, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Almqvist C","lastName":"Almqvist","firstName":"Catarina","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Pediatric Allergy and Pulmonology Unit, Astrid Lindgren Children's Hospital, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":4117486,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Allergy","title":"Frontiers in allergy","essn":"2673-6101","issn":"2673-6101","nlmid":"9918227355906676","isoabbreviation":"Front Allergy"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1840756","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>The impact of current pet exposure on children with allergic asthma is not yet fully understood. This study aimed to investigate the association between cat exposure and asthma outcomes in children with established asthma and allergy.<h4>Methods</h4>A population-based cohort study using Swedish national health, sociodemographic, and quality registers was conducted. We included 30 277 children aged 4-17 years (born 2006-2020), with validated diagnoses of asthma and allergy, who had asthma care during the two years preceding exposure assessment. Cat exposure was defined as parental cat ownership recorded in the National Cat Register in 2023. Asthma outcomes, assessed during 2023-2024, included: asthma exacerbation and moderate-to-severe asthma. In a subset with available data (<i>n</i> = 1428), Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 Second (FEV<sub>1</sub>) and asthma control test (ACT) were evaluated. Odds ratios (ORs) were adjusted for sex, age, initial asthma severity, baseline airway allergy severity, parental asthma and allergy, population density, parental birth country, and socioeconomic status.<h4>Results</h4>Of the study population (median age: 9.5 years, 39% females), 9.4% (<i>n</i> = 2,862) had cat exposure. Initial asthma severity was comparable between the cat-exposed and non-exposed groups (19.5% vs. 20.6% with moderate-to-severe asthma). Asthma exacerbation occurred in 3.3% of the cat-exposed group vs. 3.5% of the non-exposed group (adjusted OR: 1.12, 95% CI: 0.90-1.39). Moderate-to-severe asthma was observed in 9.6% vs. 10.1% (adjusted OR: 0.96, 0.84-1.10). No significant differences were found in ACT or FEV₁ z-scores. Within the cat-exposed group, no association between the number of cats, cat sex, or age and asthma outcomes was observed.<h4>Conclusion</h4>In this cohort of children with asthma and allergy, no association between cat exposure and asthma exacerbations, severity, lung function, or asthma control was observed. These findings suggest that cat exposure may not adversely affect asthma outcomes in this population.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Allergy","Asthma","cat","allergic rhinitis","Asthma Exacerbation","Cat Ownership","Pediatric Asthma","Cat Exposure"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/falgy.2026.1840756"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13290962"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13290962?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"42964262","reference":"Front Allergy. 2026 Oct 16;7:1997917. doi: 10.3389/falgy.2026.1997917.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1331581","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.09.24.26363868","title":"Blood epigenetic remodeling in asthma associated with biologic therapy","authorString":"Martin-Almeida M, Perez-Garcia J, Martin-Gonzalez E, Poza-Guedes P, Cañas JA, Sanchez-Machin I, Hernández-Pérez JM, Gonzalez-Garcia D, Mederos-Luis E, Román-Bernal B, Villar J, Valverde-Monge M, Betancor D, Rodriguez-Nieto MJ, Lorenzo-Diaz F, del Pozo V, González-Pérez R, Pino-Yanes M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Martin-Almeida M","lastName":"Martin-Almeida","firstName":"Mario","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Perez-Garcia J","lastName":"Perez-Garcia","firstName":"Javier","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Martin-Gonzalez E","lastName":"Martin-Gonzalez","firstName":"Elena","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Poza-Guedes P","lastName":"Poza-Guedes","firstName":"Paloma","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8516-6648"}},{"fullName":"Cañas JA","lastName":"Cañas","firstName":"José Antonio","initials":"JA"},{"fullName":"Sanchez-Machin I","lastName":"Sanchez-Machin","firstName":"Inmaculada","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0109-8735"}},{"fullName":"Hernández-Pérez JM","lastName":"Hernández-Pérez","firstName":"José María","initials":"JM"},{"fullName":"Gonzalez-Garcia D","lastName":"Gonzalez-Garcia","firstName":"Daniel","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Mederos-Luis E","lastName":"Mederos-Luis","firstName":"Elena","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Román-Bernal B","lastName":"Román-Bernal","firstName":"Berta","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Villar J","lastName":"Villar","firstName":"Jesús","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Valverde-Monge M","lastName":"Valverde-Monge","firstName":"Marcela","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Betancor D","lastName":"Betancor","firstName":"Diana","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Rodriguez-Nieto MJ","lastName":"Rodriguez-Nieto","firstName":"Ma Jesus","initials":"MJ"},{"fullName":"Lorenzo-Diaz F","lastName":"Lorenzo-Diaz","firstName":"Fabian","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"del Pozo V","lastName":"del Pozo","firstName":"Victoria","initials":"V"},{"fullName":"González-Pérez R","lastName":"González-Pérez","firstName":"Ruperto","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Pino-Yanes M","lastName":"Pino-Yanes","firstName":"Maria","initials":"M"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8516-6648"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0460-2813"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2920-212X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0109-8735"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>  Polygenic risk scores (PRS) capture inherited susceptibility to asthma, whereas methylation risk scores (MRS) may reflect disease-associated epigenetic variation. We assessed whether these scores differ in patients requiring biologic therapy and whether MRS change after mepolizumab initiation. <h4>Methods</h4>  Cross-sectional analyses in the GEMAS study included 699 participants with genotype data (428 with asthma, 271 controls) and 668 with blood DNA methylation data (408 with asthma, 260 controls). We evaluated 35 existing asthma PRS and derived six MRS from published epigenomic asthma studies. Longitudinal MRS changes after mepolizumab initiation were assessed in 16 GEMAS patients and were replicated in 20 patients from the MEGA study. <h4>Results</h4>  Twenty-eight PRS differentiated biologic-treated patients from controls (areas under the curve [AUC], 0.68-0.75), but none distinguished biologic-treated and non-treated patients after multiple-testing correction. MRS was higher in asthma without biologic than in controls (odds ratio [OR], 1.64; 95% CI, 1.34-1.99) and lower in biologic-treated than in asthma without biologic patients (OR, 0.54; 95% CI, 0.35-0.81). MRS did not differ significantly between biologic-treated patients and controls. Following mepolizumab initiation, MRS decreased at 6 and 12 months in GEMAS (  p =4.81×10  −5 and  p =2.40×10  −5 , respectively). The 6-month decrease was also observed in MEGA (  p =3.25×10  −9 ). MRS epigenetic markers were predominantly hypermethylated, opposite to their described pattern in asthma risk.  <h4>Conclusions</h4>  Asthma PRS did not differ significantly between biologic-treated and non-biologic-treated patients, but asthma MRS were lower in biologic-treated patients. The MRS decreased after mepolizumab initiation in two independent cohorts, consistent with a reversal of asthma-associated epigenetic patterns.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.09.24.26363868"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42947468","source":"MED","pmid":"42947468","doi":"10.1080/02770903.2026.2751851","title":"The application of proteomics in asthma research: a bibliometric analysis.","authorString":"Li J, Wang S, Li Z, Liu X, Shi Z, Xi Q, Lin J, Jia W, Li G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Li J","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Jing","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School, Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang S","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Song","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Scientific Research Management, Shanghai TCM- Integrated Hospital, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li Z","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Zhaoxuan","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School, Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu X","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Xinglu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School, Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi Z","lastName":"Shi","firstName":"Zuoqiao","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Shanghai TCM- Integrated Hospital, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xi Q","lastName":"Xi","firstName":"Qing","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Shanghai TCM- Integrated Hospital, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin J","lastName":"Lin","firstName":"Jie","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory, Shanghai TCM- Integrated Hospital, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Jia W","lastName":"Jia","firstName":"Wei","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory, Shanghai TCM- Integrated Hospital, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li G","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Guowen","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Shanghai TCM- Integrated Hospital, Shanghai, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4281971,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma","title":"The Journal of asthma : official journal of the Association for the Care of Asthma","essn":"1532-4303","issn":"0277-0903","nlmid":"8106454","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-14","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Asthma is a common inflammatory disease of the respiratory system. Following the advent of genomics, proteomics has emerged as a valuable research approach. This study aimed to pinpoint key research areas and trends in the use of proteomics for asthma through bibliometric analysis, offering insights into future research paths.<h4>Methods</h4>Articles related to asthma and proteomics published between 2000 and 2024 were retrieved from the Web of Science Core Collection. The data were analyzed and visualized using the bibliometric software CiteSpace (version number: v.6.1 R6).<h4>Results</h4>A total of 520 articles were included in this study, and a gradual increase in the annual publication volume was observed. The USA, China, and Sweden ranked as the top three countries in terms of publication output. The USA exhibited the highest centrality (0.42), indicating its influence in this field. At the institutional level, the Karolinska Institute had the highest number of publications. The keyword clustering analysis revealed that the research focuses on airway inflammation mechanisms, proteomics methodologies, precision medicine applications, and clinical phenotype characterization. Keywords like \"proteomics,\" \"mass spectrometry,\" and \"precision medicine\" have seen significant citation increases recently, highlighting proteomics' role in advancing asthma research, biomarker discovery, and personalized therapies.<h4>Conclusion</h4>By systematically examining the application and development of proteomics in the field of asthma research <i>via</i> a bibliometric analysis, we have generated a comprehensive knowledge framework for researchers. Future studies should place greater emphasis on the clinical application of biomarkers, multi-omics integration, and the development of personalized treatment strategies.","affiliation":"Graduate School, Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Shanghai, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Hongkou District Healthcare Medical Project, Hongkou District Health Commission, Shanghai","grantId":"Hongwei 2401-01","orderIn":0},{"agency":"The “Strengthening Traditional Chinese Medicine Excellence” Initiative","grantId":"HKGYQYXM-2022-11","orderIn":0},{"agency":"The Clinical Key Specialty Support Program","grantId":"HKZK2020A04","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Proteomics","Asthma","Bibliometric","Research Trends","Citespace"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/02770903.2026.2751851"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42618217","source":"MED","pmid":"42618217","pmcid":"PMC13489788","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13489788"]},"doi":"10.1002/phar.70194","title":"Antihyperglycemic Drugs and Risk of Asthma Exacerbation in Patients With Asthma and Type 2 Diabetes: A Multinational Retrospective Cohort Study.","authorString":"Hung CT, An CY, Tsai YC, Suk CW, Wu CH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hung CT","lastName":"Hung","firstName":"Chun-Tse","initials":"CT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3485-9775"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacy, College of Pharmacy, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, Michigan, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"An CY","lastName":"An","firstName":"Chen-Yen","initials":"CY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3975-4664"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Taipei Medical University Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Tsai YC","lastName":"Tsai","firstName":"Yao-Cheng","initials":"YC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-8762-891X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, College of Medicine, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Division of General Medicine, Chang Gung Memorial Hospital, New Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Suk CW","lastName":"Suk","firstName":"Chi-Won","initials":"CW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4967-165X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Taipei Municipal Wan-Fang Hospital, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu CH","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Chung-Hsuen","initials":"CH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0495-1922"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, College of Pharmaceutical Sciences, National Yang Ming Chiao Tung University, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3485-9775"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0495-1922"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4967-165X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3975-4664"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-8762-891X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"46","journalIssueId":4242440,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pharmacotherapy","title":"Pharmacotherapy","essn":"1875-9114","issn":"0277-0008","nlmid":"8111305","isoabbreviation":"Pharmacotherapy"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70194","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Antihyperglycemic drugs have shown benefits in asthma control. However, evidence regarding the comparative effectiveness of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) and sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors (SGLT-2is) in reducing asthma exacerbations remains inconsistent.<h4>Objective</h4>We aimed to evaluate the association of GLP-1 RAs versus SGLT-2is with risk of asthma exacerbation among patients with asthma and type 2 diabetes.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective cohort study used electronic health record data from the TriNetX Global Collaborative Network. Adults with asthma and type 2 diabetes who initiated GLP-1 RAs or SGLT-2is between January 1, 2013, and June 30, 2024, were included. The primary outcome was the time to first asthma exacerbation. Secondary outcomes included first episode of asthma symptoms or systemic corticosteroid use. Patients were followed for up to 6 months in the main analysis. Propensity score matching was performed to balance baseline characteristics between groups. Cox proportional hazards models were used to estimate hazard ratios (HRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs).<h4>Results</h4>A total of 100,746 patients were included, comprising 61,955 GLP-1 RA users and 38,791 SGLT-2i users. After 1:1 matching, both groups consisted of 28,831 patients. GLP-1 RAs were associated with a decreased risk of asthma exacerbation compared with SGLT-2is (HR, 0.88; 95% CI, 0.85-0.91). GLP-1 RAs were also associated with a decreased risk of experiencing asthma symptoms (HR, 0.89; 95% CI, 0.82-0.97) and receiving systemic corticosteroids (HR, 0.79; 95% CI, 0.76-0.82). Results from sensitivity analyses were generally consistent, but the association for asthma exacerbation was not observed when follow-up was extended to 1 year (HR, 0.95; 95% CI, 0.89-1.02).<h4>Conclusion</h4>GLP-1 RAs were associated with a decreased risk of asthma exacerbation compared to SGLT-2is among patients with asthma and type 2 diabetes. As these findings reflect associations rather than causal effects, further investigation through randomized trials is warranted.","affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacy, College of Pharmacy, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, Michigan, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","research-article","Multicenter Study","Journal Article","Observational Study"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disease Progression"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Hypoglycemic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AD","qualifierName":"administration & dosage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retrospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cohort Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Sodium-Glucose Transporter 2 Inhibitors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AD","qualifierName":"administration & dosage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonists"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Type 2 diabetes","Electronic Health Records","Asthma Exacerbation","Glucagon‐like Peptide‐1 Receptor Agonists","Sodium‐glucose Cotransporter‐2 Inhibitors"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Hypoglycemic Agents","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Sodium-Glucose Transporter 2 Inhibitors","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonists","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/phar.70194"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13489788"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13489788?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/phar.70194"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42471219","source":"MED","pmid":"42471219","doi":"10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109047","title":"Immune checkpoint inhibitors were not associated with poor asthma control in patients with pre-existing asthma.","authorString":"Cordial P, Devineni S, Ma J, Quadri I, Wilson R, Kim J, Ho K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cordial P","lastName":"Cordial","firstName":"Parker","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Devineni S","lastName":"Devineni","firstName":"Sritej","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0590-9032"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ma J","lastName":"Ma","firstName":"Jianing","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Biostatistics, College of Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Quadri I","lastName":"Quadri","firstName":"Iman","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wilson R","lastName":"Wilson","firstName":"Ryan","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim J","lastName":"Kim","firstName":"Joanne","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ho K","lastName":"Ho","firstName":"Kevin","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, and Sleep Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA. Electronic address: kevin.ho@osumc.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0590-9032"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9803-4465"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"261","journalIssueId":4242846,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109047","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) are associated with various pulmonary toxicities. Their impact on pre-existing asthma remains poorly characterized.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a retrospective cohort study of adult cancer patients with pre-existing asthma who received at least one ICI dose. Asthma control before and after ICI therapy was assessed based on changes in asthma medications, exacerbations requiring systemic corticosteroids, and asthma-related hospitalizations. Poor asthma control was defined as worsening in any of these parameters following ICI initiation.<h4>Results</h4>Among 340 patients, 56 (16.5%) experienced poor asthma control after ICI initiation, including medication escalation in 40 (11.7%), increased corticosteroid-treated exacerbations in 22 (6.5%), and increased asthma-related hospitalizations in 9 (2.7%). ICI doses and CIP were associated with poor asthma control in univariate analyses, and ICI doses remained independently associated in multivariable analysis. Traditional clinical factors associated with poor asthma control or ICI-related pulmonary toxicity were not associated with poor asthma control. Sensitivity analyses excluding patients with CIP yielded similar findings.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Most patients with pre-existing asthma did not experience poor asthma control during ICI treatment and within 6 months of discontinuation. No clear baseline clinical predictors were identified, and the observed association with ICI doses may reflect longer treatment exposure and follow-up time rather than a dose-dependent effect. These findings support the continued use of ICIs in patients with asthma, with appropriate monitoring. Larger, prospective studies are needed to determine whether specific patient populations are at higher risk for poor asthma control after ICI treatment.","affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, Columbus, OH, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hospitalization","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retrospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cohort Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Dose-Response Relationship, Drug"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AD","qualifierName":"administration & dosage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109047"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-21","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42858289","source":"MED","pmid":"42858289","doi":"10.1159/iaa/adfag018","title":"Age-Related Differences in Asthma Recognition, Follow-Up and Home-Based Exacerbation Management among Children Hospitalized for Acute Asthma Exacerbations.","authorString":"Butun Turk S, Kazancioglu A, Turktas I.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Butun Turk S","lastName":"Butun Turk","firstName":"Seyma","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Ankara Etlik City Hospital, Ankara, Turkey, seymabutunturk@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Kazancioglu A","lastName":"Kazancioglu","firstName":"Alp","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Hacettepe University School of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey."}]}},{"fullName":"Turktas I","lastName":"Turktas","firstName":"Ipek","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Gazi University School of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4285186,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int Arch Allergy Immunol","title":"International archives of allergy and immunology","essn":"1423-0097","issn":"1018-2438","nlmid":"9211652","isoabbreviation":"Int Arch Allergy Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>The objectives of the study were to evaluate age-related differences in asthma diagnosis and management among children hospitalized for moderate-to-severe asthma exacerbations and to examine the relationship of regular follow-up and home-based exacerbation management with clinical outcomes.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective observational study included children aged 25 months to <18 years who were hospitalized for moderate-to-severe asthma exacerbations. Patients were categorized as preschool-aged (25-60 months) or school-aged (61 months to <18 years). Asthma diagnosis before hospitalization, regular follow-up, controller therapy use, caregiver education, initiation of home-based treatment, exacerbation history, and healthcare utilization were compared.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 104 children were included. Preschool children were less likely than school-aged children to have received an asthma diagnosis before hospitalization (56.5% vs. 83.3%, p = 0.008) and were more frequently managed with diagnoses such as bronchiolitis or bronchitis. Antibiotic use before asthma diagnosis was reported in 87.5% of the cohort. Patients attending regular follow-up had higher rates of controller therapy use, home-based exacerbation management education, and initiation of treatment at home (all p < 0.001). They also had fewer moderate-to-severe exacerbations (p < 0.001) and fewer emergency department visits (p = 0.036). Although children initiating home treatment had a greater previous exacerbation burden, they had fewer moderate-to-severe exacerbations during the preceding year than those who did not initiate treatment at home (p = 0.004). In multivariable analysis, regular follow-up remained independently associated with lower odds of recurrent moderate-to-severe exacerbations (aOR 0.03, 95% CI: 0.004-0.20; p < 0.001).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Asthma was less often recognized before hospitalization in preschool children, and antibiotic use before diagnosis was common. Children attending regular follow-up more often received controller therapy and home-management education and had fewer moderate-to-severe exacerbations and emergency department visits. These findings highlight the importance of timely asthma recognition, regular follow-up, and practical caregiver education in children with recurrent wheezing or asthma, while the observational design precludes causal inference.","affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Ankara Etlik City Hospital, Ankara, Turkey, seymabutunturk@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma Exacerbation","Pediatric Asthma","Caregiver Education","Home-based Management","Regular Follow-up"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1159/iaa/adfag018"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-19","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42840719","source":"MED","pmid":"42840719","pmcid":"PMC13687689","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13687689"]},"doi":"10.4274/thoracrespract.2026.2026-1-7","title":"Small Airway Dysfunction in Asthma: Associations with Control and Quality of Life.","authorString":"Polat Yuluğ D, Naycı S, Taşdelen B, Özgür ES, Özge C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Polat Yuluğ D","lastName":"Polat Yuluğ","firstName":"Demet","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8122-4457"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonology, Mersin University Faculty of Medicine, Mersin, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Naycı S","lastName":"Naycı","firstName":"Sibel","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8415-5717"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonology, Mersin University Faculty of Medicine, Mersin, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Taşdelen B","lastName":"Taşdelen","firstName":"Bahar","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8146-4912"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics, Mersin University Faculty of Medicine, Mersin, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Özgür ES","lastName":"Özgür","firstName":"Eylem Sercan","initials":"ES","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4459-232X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonology, Mersin University Faculty of Medicine, Mersin, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Özge C","lastName":"Özge","firstName":"Cengiz","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6063-1646"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonology, Mersin University Faculty of Medicine, Mersin, Türkiye."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6063-1646"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8146-4912"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8122-4457"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8415-5717"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4459-232X"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"Spec Issue 1","volume":"27","journalIssueId":4296096,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Thorac Res Pract","title":"Thoracic research and practice","essn":"2979-9139","issn":"2979-9139","nlmid":"9918609982606676","isoabbreviation":"Thorac Res Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"11-19","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>Small airway dysfunction (SAD) is an important but frequently underrecognized component of asthma. This study aimed to determine the prevalence of SAD in asthma, as assessed by impulse oscillometry (IOS), and to evaluate its association with clinical outcomes.<h4>Material and methods</h4>This cross-sectional study, with a 12-month prospective follow-up, included adult patients with asthma. SAD was defined by IOS as R5-R20 ≥0.07 kPa·s·L-<sup>1</sup>. The asthma control test (ACT) and the asthma quality of life questionnaire (AQLQ) were administered. Exacerbations were prospectively recorded during the 12 months after enrollment. Patients were stratified according to the presence of SAD. Clinical and functional parameters were compared between groups.<h4>Results</h4>SAD was identified in 63 of 98 participants (64.3%). SAD prevalence differed significantly across the five Global Initiative for Asthma treatment steps (<i>P</i> < 0.001). Patients with SAD had significantly lower ACT scores (<i>P</i> = 0.021), poorer quality of life (AQLQ, <i>P</i> = 0.002), and more frequent exacerbations (<i>P</i> < 0.001) than those without SAD. In multivariable regression analyses, higher R5-R20 was independently associated with a lower AQLQ score (β = -0.18, 95% confidence interval -0.30 to -0.07; <i>P</i> = 0.002) and higher odds of being in a worse asthma control category (odds ratio 1.33, <i>P</i> = 0.010).<h4>Conclusion</h4>SAD is common among patients with asthma and is associated with poorer asthma control, lower quality of life, and an increased exacerbation burden. Higher R5-R20 remained associated with poorer quality of life after adjustment for spirometry, whereas its association with asthma control was attenuated and became non-significant after adjustment for forced expiratory volume in the first second. These findings support further evaluation of IOS-defined SAD as a potentially clinically relevant trait in asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Pulmonology, Mersin University Faculty of Medicine, Mersin, Türkiye.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Quality of life","Impulse oscillometry","Asthma Control","Small Airway Dysfunction"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4274/ThoracResPract.2026.2026-1-7"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13687689"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13687689?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-21","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-07","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42755259","source":"MED","pmid":"42755259","doi":"10.1097/med.0000000000000980","title":"Asthma and female reproductive health: implications for fertility and pregnancy.","authorString":"Kantroo V, Kalra B, Shaikh S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kantroo V","lastName":"Kantroo","firstName":"Viny","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory, Critical Care and Sleep Medicine, Indraprastha Apollo Hospitals, New Delhi."}]}},{"fullName":"Kalra B","lastName":"Kalra","firstName":"Bharti","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics, Bharti Hospital, Karnal."}]}},{"fullName":"Shaikh S","lastName":"Shaikh","firstName":"Shehla","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endocrinology, Saifee Hospital, Mumbai, Maharashtra, India."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"33","journalIssueId":4301698,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Opin Endocrinol Diabetes Obes","title":"Current opinion in endocrinology, diabetes, and obesity","essn":"1752-2978","issn":"1752-296X","nlmid":"101308636","isoabbreviation":"Curr Opin Endocrinol Diabetes Obes"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-12-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"230-236","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose of review</h4>Asthma is a chronic respiratory disease prevalent among women of reproductive age and has important implications for fertility, pregnancy, and offspring health. This review summarizes current evidence on the bidirectional interactions between asthma and female reproductive physiology, with particular emphasis on fertility and pregnancy.<h4>Recent findings</h4>Perimenstrual asthma represents a distinct phenotype associated with greater disease severity and increased healthcare utilization. Asthma impairs fertility and is associated with longer time to conception, higher rates of infertility, and greater use of fertility treatment, although appropriately controlled asthma does not appear to adversely affect fertility rates or assisted reproductive technique outcomes. During pregnancy, poor asthma control and exacerbations are the principal drivers of adverse maternal and fetal outcomes, including hypertensive disorders, preterm birth, fetal growth restriction, and neonatal morbidity. Contemporary evidence supports continuation of guideline-directed asthma therapy, including inhaled corticosteroids and selected biologics when indicated. Prenatal vitamin D supplementation has shown a reduction in early childhood asthma or recurrent wheeze in pooled analyses, while omega-3 fatty acid supplementation has demonstrated variable benefit and is being evaluated in an ongoing confirmatory trial.<h4>Summary</h4>Asthma influences multiple aspects of female reproductive health. Optimizing disease control before conception and throughout pregnancy remains the most effective strategy for improving maternal and offspring outcomes. Although asthma has been associated with delayed conception and greater use of fertility treatment, appropriate disease control does not reduce overall fertility.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory, Critical Care and Sleep Medicine, Indraprastha Apollo Hospitals, New Delhi.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Infertility, Female","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pregnancy Complications","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy Outcome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Fertility","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Reproductive Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Pregnancy","Infertility","Asthma","Assisted Reproductive Technique","Perimenstrual Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/MED.0000000000000980"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-30","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-18","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1326739","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-10935196/v1","title":"Emotional distress and disease control in asthma during two phases of the pandemic","authorString":"Hall WG, Salsman ML, Rosenfield D, Ritz T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hall WG","lastName":"Hall","firstName":"Windsor","initials":"WG"},{"fullName":"Salsman ML","lastName":"Salsman","firstName":"Margot","initials":"ML"},{"fullName":"Rosenfield D","lastName":"Rosenfield","firstName":"David","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Ritz T","lastName":"Ritz","firstName":"Thomas","initials":"T"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Background. This study aimed to examine aspects of emotional distress and their association with asthma control among individuals with asthma across two phases of the pandemic. Individuals with asthma report emotional distress at higher rates than healthy controls, an association thought to be exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic. Although early studies suggested elevated mental health vulnerability in asthma, changes throughout the pandemic and the role of asthma control remain unclear. Methods. Data were collected via online surveys in an early phase in 2020 and a late phase in 2022. Participants (N=414; asthma n=180, controls n=234) completed measures of general anxiety and depression, COVID-19 related fear, emotional exhaustion/burnout, and asthma control. Analyses were conducted using multivariate multilevel models, adjusting for demographics and pandemic-specific adverse events. Results. Those with asthma reported higher distress compared to controls, regardless of pandemic phase. Specifically, those with asthma reported higher COVID-19 fear, anxiety, and emotional exhaustion, but not depression. In both groups, there was reduction in distress from the early phase to the late phase of the pandemic, which was evident for depression, anxiety, COVID-19 fear, but not for emotional exhaustion. lower asthma control was associated with higher distress among participants with asthma, which was significant on all 4 measures of distress. Conclusion. Asthma was associated with psychological vulnerability throughout the pandemic. Integrated asthma care needs to address sustained emotional distress during times of a public health crisis.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10935196/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-25","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1326739","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-09-24","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42867248","source":"MED","pmid":"42867248","doi":"10.4081/monaldi.2026.3788","title":"Variable expiratory airflow, and not airway obstruction, is the defining physiological feature of asthma.","authorString":"Chhabra SK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chhabra SK","lastName":"Chhabra","firstName":"Sunil K","initials":"SK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Primus Superspeciality Hospital, New Delhi."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4286178,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Monaldi Arch Chest Dis","title":"Monaldi archives for chest disease = Archivio Monaldi per le malattie del torace","essn":"2532-5264","issn":"1122-0643","nlmid":"9307314","isoabbreviation":"Monaldi Arch Chest Dis"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Asthma is usually diagnosed by history and presence of airway obstruction with a significant bronchodilator response on spirometry. However, airway obstruction may not always be present, or a bronchodilator response may not be significant. Contrary to wide perception, airway obstruction is not the defining functional feature of asthma. Definitions of asthma have never included the term 'airways obstruction'. According to the Global Initiative for Asthma, the diagnosis of asthma requires a history of typical variable symptoms and confirmation by variable expiratory airflow. It has emphasized that variable expiratory airflow is the key physiological feature of asthma. Airway obstruction is a manifestation of this variability and represents one end of the spectrum of this variation. When airways obstruction is observed, a positive bronchodilator response is the most convenient method to confirm the diagnosis. However, when airway obstruction or bronchodilator responsiveness is not demonstrable, there are other options to demonstrate variability in not demonstrable, there are other options to demonstrate variability of expiratory airflows and confirm the diagnosis.","affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Primus Superspeciality Hospital, New Delhi.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Spirometry","Airway Obstruction","Bronchodilator Responsiveness"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4081/monaldi.2026.3788"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42545877","source":"MED","pmid":"42545877","doi":"10.1080/02770903.2026.2712840","title":"Parental numeracy skills and pediatric asthma outcomes in Jordan: a cross-sectional study of asthma-related quality of life.","authorString":"Al-Qerem W, Jarab A, Al Bawab AQ, Eberhardt J, Al-Zayadneh E, Al-Iede M, Khdour M, Al-Sa'di L, Zidan R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Al-Qerem W","lastName":"Al-Qerem","firstName":"Walid","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Al-Zaytoonah University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Jarab A","lastName":"Jarab","firstName":"Anan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Jordan University of Science and Technology, Irbid, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Al Bawab AQ","lastName":"Al Bawab","firstName":"Abdel Qader","initials":"AQ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Al-Zaytoonah University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Eberhardt J","lastName":"Eberhardt","firstName":"Judith","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0745-178X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, School of Social Sciences, Humanities and Law, Teesside University, Middlesbrough, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Al-Zayadneh E","lastName":"Al-Zayadneh","firstName":"Enas","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, School of Medicine, University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Al-Iede M","lastName":"Al-Iede","firstName":"Montaha","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, School of Medicine, University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Khdour M","lastName":"Khdour","firstName":"Maher","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Pharmacy, Al-Quds University, Abu Deis, Palestine."}]}},{"fullName":"Al-Sa'di L","lastName":"Al-Sa'di","firstName":"Lujain","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Al-Zaytoonah University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Zidan R","lastName":"Zidan","firstName":"Rahaf","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Al-Zaytoonah University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0745-178X"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4237134,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma","title":"The Journal of asthma : official journal of the Association for the Care of Asthma","essn":"1532-4303","issn":"0277-0903","nlmid":"8106454","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-13","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>Parental numeracy skills, reflecting a quantitative domain within health literacy, may be relevant to pediatric asthma management. However, the association between parental numeracy skills and the asthma-related quality of life (QOL) of children with asthma remains insufficiently examined. This study aimed to assess the QOL of Jordanian children with asthma and to examine its association with parental numeracy skills.<h4>Methods</h4>A cross-sectional study was performed on a sample of 400 parents (66.5% mothers) of children with asthma (59.5% male) undergoing treatment at the University of Jordan Hospital. The Arabic version of the Asthma Numeracy Questionnaire (Ar-ANQ) was utilized to evaluate parental numeracy skills, while the parent-report version of the Pediatric Quality of Life Inventory (PedsQL) Asthma Module was employed to assess QOL. Quantile regression analyzed associations between parental numeracy skills, asthma control, and QOL.<h4>Results</h4>The median Ar-ANQ score was 3 (IQR: 1-4) out of 4, and the median QOL score was 60 (IQR: 50-67) out of 100. Higher parental numeracy skills were significantly associated with better asthma-related QOL among children (<i>β</i> = 1.99, 95% CI [0.81, 3.18], <i>p</i> = 0.001). Children with uncontrolled or partially controlled asthma had significantly lower QOL scores (<i>β</i> = -9.70, 95% CI [-15.57, -3.82], <i>p</i> = 0.001; <i>β</i> = -10.06, 95% CI [-15.83, -4.29], <i>p</i> = 0.001).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Higher parental numeracy skills were associated with better asthma-related QOL among children with asthma. Given the cross-sectional design, this finding should be interpreted as an association rather than evidence of a causal effect.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Al-Zaytoonah University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Children","Health Literacy","Medication Adherence","Asthma Control","Caregiver Education"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/02770903.2026.2712840"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-10","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42660368","source":"MED","pmid":"42660368","pmcid":"PMC13618691","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13618691"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109122","title":"Mitochondrial antioxidant therapy fails to improve obese asthma outcomes.","authorString":"Dixon AE, Garrow OJ, Callas P, Morris C, Bates JH, Martin F, Barbieri S, Higdon M, Anathy V, Que LG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Dixon AE","lastName":"Dixon","firstName":"Anne E","initials":"AE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: anne.dixon@uvmhealth.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Garrow OJ","lastName":"Garrow","firstName":"Olivia J","initials":"OJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: olivia.garrow@uvm.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Callas P","lastName":"Callas","firstName":"Peter","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3840-4081"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: peter.callas@uvm.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Morris C","lastName":"Morris","firstName":"Carolyn","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: carolyn.morris@uvm.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Bates JH","lastName":"Bates","firstName":"Jason Ht","initials":"JH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: Jason.bates@uvm.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Martin F","lastName":"Martin","firstName":"Francesca","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: frankiekamartin@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Barbieri S","lastName":"Barbieri","firstName":"Savannah","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University, PO Box 2629 DUMC, Rm 279 MSRB1, Research Dr., Durham, NC, 27710, USA. Electronic address: savannah.barbieri@duke.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Higdon M","lastName":"Higdon","firstName":"Maggie","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1761-6453"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University, PO Box 2629 DUMC, Rm 279 MSRB1, Research Dr., Durham, NC, 27710, USA. Electronic address: maggie.higdon@duke.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Anathy V","lastName":"Anathy","firstName":"Vikas","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: vikas.anathy@uvm.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Que LG","lastName":"Que","firstName":"Loretta G","initials":"LG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University, PO Box 2629 DUMC, Rm 279 MSRB1, Research Dr., Durham, NC, 27710, USA. Electronic address: loretta.que@duke.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3840-4081"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1761-6453"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"263","journalIssueId":4298452,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109122","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Obesity is associated with poorly controlled asthma. One reason for this may be the increased mitochondrial oxidative stress in obese asthma. Indeed, targeting oxidative stress reduces airway reactivity in animal models. The purpose of this trial was to investigate the potential efficacy of the mitochondrial-targeted antioxidant MitoQuinone (MitoQ) for the treatment of poorly controlled asthma in people with obesity.<h4>Methods</h4>A twelve week, randomized, double-masked, placebo-controlled trial of 40 mg MitoQ versus placebo in adults with obesity and poorly controlled asthma was performed. The primary outcome was change in airway reactivity, with secondary outcomes of change in asthma control, quality of life, and lung function.<h4>Results</h4>Twenty participants were randomized to MitoQ, 18 to placebo. After 12 weeks, there was no difference in change in airway reactivity to methacholine (provocative dose producing a 20% decrease in FEV<sub>1</sub>) between the group assigned to MitoQ (median change = 0.0 μg [IQR -25.5 to 1.4]) or placebo (change = -0.8 μg [IQR -57 to 0.0]), p = 0.81. There was no difference in the change in Asthma Control Test score (MitoQ 1 [IQR -1 to 2], placebo 1 [-2 to 2]), p = 0.62, or Marks Asthma Quality of Life Score (MitoQ -0.08 [IQR -0.35 to 0.10], placebo -0.05 [-0.30 to 0.15]), p = 0.80.<h4>Conclusion</h4>MitoQ was well tolerated but did not improve airway reactivity, asthma control, or lung function. Targeting oxidative stress in people with obesity and poorly controlled asthma with MitoQ is unlikely to be efficacious for the treatment of asthma.","affiliation":"Larner College of Medicine, University of Vermont, University of Vermont Medical Center, 110 Colchester Avenue, Burlington, VT, 05401, USA. Electronic address: anne.dixon@uvmhealth.org.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"R01 HL136917","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mitochondria","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO",