 Wang H, Xu G, Xu L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xie J","lastName":"Xie","firstName":"Jing","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7536-0972"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan X","lastName":"Yan","firstName":"Xiaopei","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Song Y","lastName":"Song","firstName":"Ye","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang H","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Hui","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu G","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Guopeng","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu L","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Li","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7532-3928"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7532-3928"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7536-0972"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4095019,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy","title":"Journal of asthma and allergy","essn":"1178-6965","issn":"1178-6965","nlmid":"101543450","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"644651","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>Early identification of asthma in pregnancy is challenging due to limited feasibility of pulmonary function testing. We aimed to characterize serum CCL23 expression in pregnant women with asthma and to assess its potential as an auxiliary clinical marker.<h4>Methods</h4>Transcriptomic sequencing first identified differentially expressed genes (DEGs) in peripheral blood between pregnant women with asthma and healthy pregnant controls. A validation cohort of 37 pregnant women with asthma and 36 healthy pregnant controls was then enrolled. Clinical data (EOS, pregnancy outcomes) were collected, and serum CCL23, IL-4, and IL-5 levels were measured. The expression characteristics and potential adjunctive identification value of CCL23 were evaluated using the Mann-Whitney <i>U</i>-test, Spearman correlation analysis, and binary logistic regression.<h4>Results</h4>Transcriptomic sequencing revealed CCL23 upregulation in asthma (P < 0.01). ELISA showed higher serum CCL23 in pregnant women with asthma than in healthy pregnant controls (adjusted means 775.0 ± 91.42 vs 512.92 ± 92.95 pg/mL; adjusted P = 0.07). CCL23 correlated positively with EOS (<i>ρ</i>=0.74, P<0.01) and FeNO (<i>ρ</i>=0.35, P = 0.03) in the asthma group. Elevated CCL23 was independently associated with an increased risk of asthma in pregnancy (overall P < 0.01). ROC analysis showed an AUC of 0.74 (95% CI 0.63-0.86) for CCL23, 0.88 (0.80-0.95) for EOS, 0.87 (0.79-0.95) for CCL23 + EOS; combined model sensitivity 94.60% and specificity 66.70% at optimal cut-off. The combination did not improve discrimination over EOS alone (DeLong P = 0.13).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Serum CCL23 is elevated in pregnant women with asthma and positively correlates with peripheral blood EOS and FeNO, implicating a role in systemic type 2 inflammation. CCL23 was independently associated with asthma after adjustment for gestational age and maternal age, but adding it to EOS did not improve discrimination over EOS alone. These exploratory findings require validation in large-scale, multicenter cohorts before any clinical use.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, The Affiliated Suzhou Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou Municipal Hospital, Gusu School, Nanjing Medical University, Suzhou, Jiangsu, People's Republic of China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biomarkers","eosinophils","Asthma In Pregnancy","Ccl23"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JAA.S644651"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13744192"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13744192?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-09","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-08","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42954310","source":"MED","pmid":"42954310","doi":"10.1177/15248399261494603","title":"Assessing Context to Inform Cultural Tailoring of a School-Based Asthma Program for Dissemination to Rural Colorado.","authorString":"Jimenez-Zambrano AM, Reedy J, Garcia-Hernandez S, Brewer SE, Szefler S, DeCamp LR, Kempe A, Huebschmann AG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jimenez-Zambrano AM","lastName":"Jimenez-Zambrano","firstName":"Andrea M","initials":"AM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7274-9553"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Reedy J","lastName":"Reedy","firstName":"Julia","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-7553-1847"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Garcia-Hernandez S","lastName":"Garcia-Hernandez","firstName":"Sandra","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Brewer SE","lastName":"Brewer","firstName":"Sarah E","initials":"SE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0063-6626"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Szefler S","lastName":"Szefler","firstName":"Stanley","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6911-3199"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."},{"affiliation":"Children's Hospital Colorado Breathing Institute, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"DeCamp LR","lastName":"DeCamp","firstName":"Lisa Ross","initials":"LR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5210-4675"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kempe A","lastName":"Kempe","firstName":"Allison","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Huebschmann AG","lastName":"Huebschmann","firstName":"Amy G","initials":"AG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA."},{"affiliation":"Ludeman Family Center for Women's Health, Aurora, CO, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7274-9553"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5210-4675"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6911-3199"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0063-6626"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-7553-1847"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4275546,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Health Promot Pract","title":"Health promotion practice","essn":"1552-6372","issn":"1524-8399","nlmid":"100890609","isoabbreviation":"Health Promot Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"15248399261494603","abstractText":"School-based asthma programs have reduced asthma disparities for low-income and racial/ethnic minority children in urban settings, including Latinx families. However, rural parent perspectives have not yet been incorporated into these programs. To inform implementation of our Better Asthma Control for Kids (BACK) program in rural Colorado, we sought to understand the barriers and facilitators to asthma management in rural settings for Latinx and non-Latinx families. Between June and September 2022, interviews with Latinx and non-Latinx caregivers of children with asthma in rural Colorado explored barriers and facilitators to pediatric asthma management, including self-management practices, cultural beliefs, and interest in BACK. Interviews were coded and analyzed for thematic domains of commonalities and unique experiences by ethnicity/language spoken. We interviewed 22 caregivers (68% Latinx, 45% Spanish-speaking) living in two rural regions of Colorado. Barriers across caregivers included three themes: (a) Rural families experience challenges accessing asthma care due to transportation challenges and limited health care availability. (b) Families have common knowledge, language, and cultural gaps that lead to misperceptions of asthma. Latinx caregivers, who often struggled to understand why their child had asthma, leading to limited diagnosis acceptance and obstacles to self-management. (c) Rural caregivers desire tailored education to address asthma knowledge gaps and were interested in school-based asthma programs like BACK. The novel barriers to pediatric asthma management identified among rural non-Latinx and Latinx families will guide tailoring of BACK to enhance its impact-other programs seeking to improve asthma disparities in rural and Latinx communities should consider these barriers.","affiliation":"University of Colorado School of Medicine, Aurora, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Pediatrics","Rural Health","Health Care Disparities","Cultural Tailoring","School-based Program"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/15248399261494603"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42781720","source":"MED","pmid":"42781720","pmcid":"PMC13618997","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13618997"]},"doi":"10.1042/bst20250090","title":"Unmasking programmed cell death in rhinovirus-driven asthma pathology.","authorString":"Cawthorne W, Zhang Y, Val N, Nelson M, Raevski A, Ito K, Ghildyal R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cawthorne W","lastName":"Cawthorne","firstName":"Wayne","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-8467-8091"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Walter and Eliza Hall Institute of Medical Research, Melbourne, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Biology, University of Melbourne, Melbourne, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Youming","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5403-4044"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Heart and Lung Institute, Imperial College, London, U.K."}]}},{"fullName":"Val N","lastName":"Val","firstName":"Nicole","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Canberra, Canberra, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Nelson M","lastName":"Nelson","firstName":"Michelle","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Canberra, Canberra, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Raevski A","lastName":"Raevski","firstName":"Anna","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Canberra, Canberra, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ito K","lastName":"Ito","firstName":"Kazuhiro","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9320-2717"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Heart and Lung Institute, Imperial College, London, U.K."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghildyal R","lastName":"Ghildyal","firstName":"Reena","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3147-4976"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Canberra, Canberra, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5403-4044"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9320-2717"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3147-4976"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-8467-8091"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"54","journalIssueId":4270246,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Biochem Soc Trans","title":"Biochemical Society transactions","essn":"1470-8752","issn":"0300-5127","nlmid":"7506897","isoabbreviation":"Biochem Soc Trans"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1359-1373","abstractText":"Rhinovirus (RV) infection is responsible for the majority of cases of viral exacerbations of asthma. Understanding the cellular and molecular mechanisms that cause asthma exacerbations is an absolute requirement for the development of effective treatments. Emerging literature suggests that cell death may have an important role in RV-induced exacerbations. Several types of programmed cell death (PCD) have been implicated in asthma, including apoptosis, necroptosis, pyroptosis, and ferroptosis. Increased presence of apoptotic airway epithelial cells in the sputum of adult and paediatric asthma patients is suggestive of defects in apoptosis. Elevated levels of phosphorylated mixed lineage kinase domain-like protein and receptor-interacting serine/threonine-protein kinase 3, markers of active necroptosis, have been found in the serum of asthmatic patients. Gasdermin B, a member of the gasdermin family of pyroptosis executioner proteins, has a strong genetic linkage to severe asthma. Expression level of GSDMB correlated with asthma exacerbations and antiviral pathways. Lipid peroxidation and increased airway iron levels, both suggestive of ferroptosis, have been observed in asthma patients. RV infection of epithelial cells triggers an inflammatory response, which can provide the necessary pro-inflammatory chemokines and cytokines to initiate PCD, such as tumour necrosis factor-α. RV exploits PCD such as apoptosis, necroptosis, pyroptosis, and ferroptosis to aid its replication and release, while modulating them probably to limit immune response. In the present review, we investigate the concept that pro-inflammatory PCD pathways modulated by RV infection in the context of asthma lead to prolonged inflammatory and immune responses that are key to asthma exacerbations.","affiliation":"Walter and Eliza Hall Institute of Medical Research, Melbourne, Australia.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Walter and Eliza Hall Institute (WEHI)","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Department of Education, Australian Government (AusGovEducation)","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Ormond College Peter Callow Memorial Scholarship","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"University of Canberra (UC)","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"UoM | Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, University of Melbourne (MDHS)","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Chism Indigenous PhD Top-up Scholarship","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Ormond College Indigenous Scholarship","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Epithelial Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VI","qualifierName":"virology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Rhinovirus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PY","qualifierName":"pathogenicity"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Picornaviridae Infections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VI","qualifierName":"virology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VI","qualifierName":"virology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Apoptosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pyroptosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Necroptosis"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Programmed cell death","Asthma","Rhinovirus","Asthma Exacerbations"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1042/BST20250090"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13618997"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13618997?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42642957","source":"MED","pmid":"42642957","pmcid":"PMC13507615","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13507615"]},"doi":"10.1111/bjhp.70108","title":"Changes in patient conceptualization of asthma and asthma self-management at key timepoints across the COVID-19 pandemic: A qualitative study.","authorString":"Kite A, Ghio D, Keyworth C, Epton T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kite A","lastName":"Kite","firstName":"Aphra","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Manchester Centre for Health Psychology, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghio D","lastName":"Ghio","firstName":"Daniela","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0580-0205"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Manchester Centre for Health Psychology, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Keyworth C","lastName":"Keyworth","firstName":"Chris","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7815-6174"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Psychology, University of Leeds, Leeds, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Epton T","lastName":"Epton","firstName":"Tracy","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1653-191X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Manchester Centre for Health Psychology, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0580-0205"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1653-191X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7815-6174"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"31","journalIssueId":4202976,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Br J Health Psychol","title":"British journal of health psychology","essn":"2044-8287","issn":"1359-107X","nlmid":"9605409","isoabbreviation":"Br J Health Psychol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70108","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The COVID-19 public health emergency had significant impacts on people living with long-term health conditions. The specific impacts of COVID-19 on people with asthma across key stages of the pandemic are less certain. This study therefore aimed to explore the experiences of people with asthma across the pandemic as risk levels changed in a retrospective cross-sectional study.<h4>Methods</h4>Semi-structured interviews (N = 20), conducted at one timepoint, asked about experiences covering pre-COVID, initial outbreak/lockdown, restrictions lifting, key-stages salient to them (e.g., vaccination) and longer-term. Data were analysed using thematic-analysis.<h4>Findings</h4>There were three themes. 'Doing my own risk assessment' was an active process using pre-existing/acquired knowledge to determine risk and subsequent asthma-management/COVID-19 behaviours at all stages. 'Asthma from background to foreground' highlighted how asthma that was mostly previously unobtrusive was suddenly a 'pre-existing condition' leading to prolonged stress and anxiety. 'Place in society' describes how the participants were included/excluded by society due to safety concerns from the initial outbreak onwards, which was influenced by government, friends, family and neighbours, the media, health care systems and even themselves.<h4>Discussion</h4>Findings highlight how some people with asthma experienced the pandemic as a threat to mortality, with evidence of both poor and good adjustment to their asthma management, as per the Transdiagnostic model. When there are future rapid changes in contexts affecting long term conditions, government agencies should use the present study's findings to (a) guide decision-making, (b) inform public health messaging aimed at targeting at-risk populations and (c) provide support for at-risk populations.","affiliation":"Manchester Centre for Health Psychology, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Assessment"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retrospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Knowledge, Attitudes, Practice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Qualitative Research"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pandemics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Self-Management","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"COVID-19","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"SARS-CoV-2"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Qualitative","Pandemic","Transdiagnostic Model","Covid‐19"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bjhp.70108"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13507615"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13507615?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42903727","source":"MED","pmid":"42903727","pmcid":"PMC13681374","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13681374"]},"doi":"10.3389/fimmu.2026.1927691","title":"Neurogenic regulation of group 2 innate lymphoid cells in asthma.","authorString":"Matsuyama T, Emami Fard N, Nagashima A, Sehmi R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Matsuyama T","lastName":"Matsuyama","firstName":"Takahiro","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Respiratory Research Group, Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Graduate School of Medical and Dental Sciences, Kagoshima University, Kagoshima, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Emami Fard N","lastName":"Emami Fard","firstName":"Nahal","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Respiratory Research Group, Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Firestone Institute for Respiratory Health, St. Joseph's Healthcare, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Nagashima A","lastName":"Nagashima","firstName":"Akimichi","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Respiratory Research Group, Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Sehmi R","lastName":"Sehmi","firstName":"Roma","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Respiratory Research Group, Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Firestone Institute for Respiratory Health, St. Joseph's Healthcare, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4103356,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Immunol","title":"Frontiers in immunology","essn":"1664-3224","issn":"1664-3224","nlmid":"101560960","isoabbreviation":"Front Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1927691","abstractText":"Asthma is a chronic airways disorder characterized by reversible airflow limitation that is variable, airway hyperresponsiveness and bronchial inflammation and is associated with symptoms of wheeze, cough and productive mucus. Asthma can be phenotyped based on airway inflammation, where eosinophilic asthma is the most prevalent phenotype. Group 2 innate lymphoid cells (ILC2) play a central role in the pathogenesis of eosinophilic asthma by producing copious quantities of type 2 cytokines, such as IL-5 and IL-13, in an antigen-independent manner. ILC2 have been shown to promote airway inflammatory response in response to epithelial-derived cytokines (IL-25, IL-33, thymic stromal lymphopoietin and tumor necrosis factor-like peptide 1A (TL1A)), lipid mediators and hormones. Among soluble mediators, there is a growing body of data from murine and human studies showing that neurotransmitters and neuropeptides secreted by local nerve fibers contribute to the modulation of lung ILC2 activation in the pathogenesis of asthma. In this review, evidence supporting the relationship between ILC2 and neurogenic inflammation in asthma pathogenesis is discussed.","affiliation":"Respiratory Research Group, Department of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Lung","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Lymphocytes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neurogenic Inflammation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neuropeptides","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neurotransmitter Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cytokines","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neuroimmunomodulation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Immunity, Innate"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Neurotransmitter","Neuropeptide","Asthma","Airway Innervation","Neuroimmune Interactions","Group 2 Innate Lymphoid Cell"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Neurotransmitter Agents","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Neuropeptides","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Cytokines","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2026.1927691"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13681374"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13681374?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-21","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42762470","source":"MED","pmid":"42762470","doi":"10.1002/ppul.71840","title":"Geographic Inequalities in Pediatric Asthma and Workforce Distribution.","authorString":"Gourishankar A, Suwandhi E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gourishankar A","lastName":"Gourishankar","firstName":"Anand","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4344-7032"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Children's National Hospital/George Washington University, Washington, DC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Suwandhi E","lastName":"Suwandhi","firstName":"Eugene","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-0302-2576"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Children's National Hospital/George Washington University, Washington, DC, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4344-7032"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-0302-2576"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"61","journalIssueId":4248385,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Pulmonol","title":"Pediatric pulmonology","essn":"1099-0496","issn":"8755-6863","nlmid":"8510590","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Pulmonol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e71840","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Asthma is one of the most common chronic diseases affecting children in the United States, with millions impacted annually. There is ongoing concern and disparity in the pediatric workforce. However, there is limited research evaluating how the geographic distribution of these providers aligns with pediatric asthma burden across states.<h4>Objective</h4>To assess the degree of geographic alignment between pediatric asthma prevalence and the distribution of general pediatricians and pediatric hospitalists in the United States using inequality metrics and spatial analysis.<h4>Methods</h4>This was a cross-sectional, state-level ecological study using publicly available data. Pediatric asthma prevalence was obtained from the CDC Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System. Pediatric provider data were sourced from the American Board of Pediatrics, and child population estimates were retrieved from the U.S. Census Bureau. Workforce density was calculated as the number of general pediatricians and pediatric hospitalists per 100,000 children in each state. Lorenz curves and Gini coefficients were used to quantify geographic inequality in asthma burden and workforce distribution. Bivariate choropleth maps and Pearson correlation coefficients were employed to assess spatial mismatch and association between asthma prevalence and provider availability.<h4>Results</h4>General pediatricians had a mean state-level density of 70.6 per 100,000 children, while pediatric hospitalists had a mean density of 5.3 per 100,000 children (p < 0.0001). Gini coefficients revealed moderate inequality in the distribution of general pediatricians (0.20) and higher inequality among pediatric hospitalists (0.33). In contrast, the Gini index for pediatric asthma prevalence was 0.11. Correlation analysis showed a weak, marginally significant association between asthma prevalence and general pediatrician density (r = 0.30, p = 0.032), and a non-significant association with pediatric hospitalist density (r = 0.24, p = 0.094). Bivariate mapping identified several states with high asthma burden and low provider density, revealing substantial spatial mismatches.<h4>Conclusion</h4>There is a misalignment between the burden of pediatric asthma and the distribution of pediatric healthcare providers in the U.S., particularly in pediatric hospital medicine. States with higher asthma prevalence are not consistently supported by proportionally larger pediatric workforces. Policies that support provider redistribution, rural practice incentives, and data-driven workforce modeling are essential to improving health equity and optimizing care for children with asthma.","affiliation":"Children's National Hospital/George Washington University, Washington, DC, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prevalence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Hospitalists","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SD","qualifierName":"supply & distribution"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Healthcare Disparities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Spatial Analysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pediatricians","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SD","qualifierName":"supply & distribution"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Socioeconomic Disparities in Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Lorenz curve","Equity","Gini Index","Regional Disparity","Pediatric Workforce"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ppul.71840"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-20","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42766173","source":"MED","pmid":"42766173","doi":"10.1007/s11882-026-01295-0","title":"Impact of Chronic Stress and Trauma on Asthma Pathophysiology and Outcomes.","authorString":"Friedman R, DePrince A, George E, Balasubramanyam S, Wang E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Friedman R","lastName":"Friedman","firstName":"Ryan","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, National Jewish Health, 1400 Jackson St, Denver, CO, 80206, USA."},{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, 13001 East 17th Place, Aurora, CO, 80045, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"DePrince A","lastName":"DePrince","firstName":"Anne","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, University of Denver, 2155 S Race Street, Denver, CO, 80208, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"George E","lastName":"George","firstName":"Elizabeth","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Allergy, and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, The University of Alabama at Birmingham, 1720 2nd Ave South, Birmingham, AL, 35295, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Balasubramanyam S","lastName":"Balasubramanyam","firstName":"Sadhana","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Immunology, Department of Medicine, Houston Methodist Hospital, 6155 Fannin St, Houston, TX, 77030, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang E","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Eileen","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, National Jewish Health, 1400 Jackson St, Denver, CO, 80206, USA. wange@njhealth.org."},{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, 13001 East 17th Place, Aurora, CO, 80045, USA. wange@njhealth.org."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4252034,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Allergy Asthma Rep","title":"Current allergy and asthma reports","essn":"1534-6315","issn":"1529-7322","nlmid":"101096440","isoabbreviation":"Curr Allergy Asthma Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"52","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose of review</h4>Chronic stress and trauma are potent risk factors for worsened asthma outcomes. We summarize recent research discoveries surrounding chronic stress, adverse childhood experiences (ACEs), and intimate partner abuse (IPA) and their impacts on asthma pathophysiology and outcomes. We also highlight treatment and precision medicine implications.<h4>Recent findings</h4>Chronic stress and trauma have distinct yet persistent effects on asthma pathophysiology through multiple pathways, including the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, neuroimmune signaling, central nervous system activity, and immune shifts. New research highlights stress and trauma's strong associations with increased asthma morbidity. Improvements in the understanding of underlying mechanisms highlight the potential of tailored or novel interventions. Despite significant leaps in our understanding of chronic stress and trauma's pervasive and enduring impacts on asthma, key actions-including clinical screening and broader inclusion in research-remain deficient. Improved recognition paired with targeted therapies offers the opportunity to improve the lives of many who are disproportionately burdened with increased asthma morbidity.","affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, National Jewish Health, 1400 Jackson St, Denver, CO, 80206, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pituitary-Adrenal System"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hypothalamo-Hypophyseal System"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chronic Disease"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Stress, Psychological","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adverse Childhood Experiences"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Trauma","Chronic stress","Adverse Childhood Experiences","Asthma Outcomes","Intimate Partner Abuse","Asthma Pathophysiology"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11882-026-01295-0"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-21","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-22","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41871449","source":"MED","pmid":"41871449","doi":"10.1093/annalsats/aaoag075","title":"Serum dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate and asthma in a nationwide study of U.S. adults.","authorString":"Han YY, Rosser FJ, Celedón JC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Han YY","lastName":"Han","firstName":"Yueh-Ying","initials":"YY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8409-3077"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pediatric Pulmonary Medicine, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, PA 15224, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Rosser FJ","lastName":"Rosser","firstName":"Franziska J","initials":"FJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1202-044X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pediatric Pulmonary Medicine, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, PA 15224, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Celedón JC","lastName":"Celedón","firstName":"Juan C","initials":"JC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6139-5320"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pediatric Pulmonary Medicine, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, PA 15224, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6139-5320"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8409-3077"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1202-044X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"23","journalIssueId":4250518,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Am Thorac Soc","title":"Annals of the American Thoracic Society","essn":"2325-6621","issn":"2329-6933","nlmid":"101600811","isoabbreviation":"Ann Am Thorac Soc"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1320-1327","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-S) has anti-inflammatory and immune-modulating properties but its role in asthma is unclear.<h4>Objective</h4>To examine serum DHEA-S levels and asthma or asthma exacerbations in a nationwide study of U.S. adults.<h4>Methods</h4>Cross-sectional study of serum DHEA-S and asthma in 4212 adults (2334 females and 1878 males) aged 18-79 years who participated in the 2021-2023 U.S. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey. Eosinophilic and noneosinophilic asthma were defined by absolute blood eosinophil counts ≥ 300 and < 300 cells/uL, respectively. An asthma exacerbation was defined as an asthma attack or an emergency room or urgent care visit due to asthma in the previous year. Logistic regression was used for the multivariable analysis of DHEA-S and asthma or asthma exacerbations, which was conducted separately in females and males.<h4>Results</h4>Serum DHEA-S levels decreased with age in male and female participants. In an analysis adjusting for other sex hormones and covariates, serum DHEA-S levels in the fourth quartile were associated with lower odds of asthma in females (odds ratio [OR] for quartile [Q]4 vs Q1, 0.55; 95% CI, 0.30-0.98) and in males (OR for Q4 to Q1, 0.42; 95% CI, 0.20-0.87). Similar associations were found for noneosinophilic asthma but not for eosinophilic asthma. There was a nonsignificant trend for an association between higher DHEA-S levels and reduced odds of asthma exacerbations in females with asthma (OR for Q4 to Q1, 0.45; 95% CI, 0.20-1.01).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Higher serum DHEA-S levels are associated with lower odds of asthma in a representative sample of U.S. adults.","affiliation":"Division of Pediatric Pulmonary Medicine, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, PA 15224, United States.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"U.S. National Institutes of Health","grantId":"HL168539","orderIn":0},{"agency":"U.S. National Institutes of Health","grantId":"K08 HL159333","orderIn":0},{"agency":"U.S. National Institutes of Health","grantId":"HL152475","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Eosinophils"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dehydroepiandrosterone Sulfate","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nutrition Surveys"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Logistic Models"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Young Adult"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Dehydroepiandrosterone Sulfate, Asthma, Asthma Exacerbation, Adults, Nhanes"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Dehydroepiandrosterone Sulfate","registryNumber":"57B09Q7FJR"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/annalsats/aaoag075"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"42150763","reference":"Ann Am Thorac Soc. 2026 Sep 1;23(9):1278-1280. doi: 10.1093/annalsats/aaoag132.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42699833","source":"MED","pmid":"42699833","pmcid":"PMC13544550","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13544550"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.114017","title":"Preventable Factors in Fatal and Near-Fatal Asthma: Lessons From the British Thoracic Society (BTS) and the National Review of Asthma Deaths (NRAD).","authorString":"Meer M, Mumtaz M, Zuhair S, Mukhtar N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Meer M","lastName":"Meer","firstName":"Muhammad","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Acute Medicine, Northampton General Hospital, Northampton, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Mumtaz M","lastName":"Mumtaz","firstName":"Mahnoor","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine, King's College Hospital NHS Foundation Trust, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Zuhair S","lastName":"Zuhair","firstName":"Syed","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"General Medicine, Northampton General Hospital, Northampton, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Mukhtar N","lastName":"Mukhtar","firstName":"Nafiu","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Acute Medicine, Northampton General Hospital, Northampton, GBR."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4236712,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","title":"Cureus","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184","nlmid":"101596737","isoabbreviation":"Cureus"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e114017","abstractText":"Asthma is one of the most widespread chronic respiratory illnesses that is still a leading cause of high morbidity and mortality even in the context of effective therapies and evidence-based clinical practice. The British Thoracic Society (BTS)/Scottish Intercollegiate Guidelines Network (SIGN) provides a structured guideline on the management of asthma, and the National Review of Asthma Deaths (NRAD) has outlined the most significant gaps in routine asthma care that lead to preventable deaths. The purpose of the review is to consider the preventable aspects related to fatal and near-fatal asthma and to comment on the major learning points from the BTS/SIGN asthma guidelines and the results of the NRAD. A narrative database literature review was carried out using PubMed, Scopus, and Google Scholar to obtain relevant peer-reviewed literature published since 2005 till December 2025. Search words were fatal asthma, near-fatal asthma, asthma mortality, preventable factors, NRAD, and the BTS asthma guideline. Articles on asthma mortality, severe exacerbations, and asthma management using the guidelines were selected. The literature recognizes various preventable causes of fatal and near-fatal asthma; among them are poor adherence to inhaled corticosteroid treatment, overuse of short-acting β2-agonists (SABA), the presence of poor follow-up following an asthma attack, absence of individualized asthma action plans, and failure to recognize worsening symptoms. Findings in the NRAD research also indicated the shortcomings of standard check-ups of asthma, minimal use of controller drugs, and the inability to recognize at-risk patients as the key contributors to asthma death. It is possible to prevent many asthma deaths by increasing observance of guidelines, early recognition of high-risk patients, and providing them with more education and self-management support. Strengthening the implementation of the BTS/SIGN guidelines and addressing gaps in NRAD insights could be key to improving the quality of asthma care and preventing asthma-related deaths.","affiliation":"Acute Medicine, Northampton General Hospital, Northampton, GBR.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Near-fatal Asthma","Asthma Deaths","Bts/sign Guidelines","Nrad"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.114017"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13544550"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13544550?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-05","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42577747","source":"MED","pmid":"42577747","pmcid":"PMC13455042","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13455042"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmed.2026.1840272","title":"Identification of endoplasmic reticulum stress-related biomarkers in asthma and asthma exacerbation.","authorString":"Xuan L, Lv Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xuan L","lastName":"Xuan","firstName":"Lingling","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ethics Office, Beijing Chao-Yang Hospital, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lv Y","lastName":"Lv","firstName":"Yali","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ethics Office, Beijing Chao-Yang Hospital, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4110742,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)","title":"Frontiers in medicine","essn":"2296-858X","issn":"2296-858X","nlmid":"101648047","isoabbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1840272","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress plays a crucial role in airway inflammation and other asthma-related processes. This study aims to identify potential ER stress-related biomarkers in asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>Sputum transcriptome data (GSE76262) from 118 moderate-to-severe asthma patients and 21 healthy controls were analyzed to identify ER stress-related differentially expressed genes (DEGs). Functional enrichment, weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA), and protein-protein interaction (PPI) network construction were performed. Machine learning algorithms (LASSO logistic regression and random forest) were applied to screen key gene signatures. The diagnostic performance was evaluated by ROC curve analysis. An OVA-induced murine asthma model with LPS or poly(I:C) co-administration was established to validate the Expression of the ER stress-related biomarkers.<h4>Results</h4>By integrating bioinformatic analysis of the GEO dataset GSE76262 with machine learning approaches, we identified two ER stress-related key genes, <i>endoplasmic reticulum to nucleus signaling 1 (ERN1)</i> and <i>transmembrane protein 129 (TMEM129)</i>, whose combined signature can distinguish asthma from healthy controls with an AUC of 0.811. The diagnostic performance of the <i>ERN1/TMEM129</i> signature was independently validated in an external cohort (GSE256534), achieving an AUC of 0.907. In human induced sputum, <i>ERN1</i> was upregulated while <i>TMEM129</i> was downregulated (<i>P</i> < 0.001). These changes were validated in an ovalbumin-induced murine asthma model: lung inositol-requiring protein 1 (IRE1, protein encoded by <i>ERN1</i>) levels were upregulated while <i>TMEM129</i> mRNA was downregulated versus saline controls. Co-exposure to lipopolysaccharides (LPS) or poly(I:C) potentiated airway inflammation and elevated the ER stress chaperone BiP/GRP78; however, only IRE1 levels increased further, whereas <i>TMEM129</i> remained unchanged upon LPS and poly(I:C) co-administration.<h4>Conclusion</h4>We identify <i>ERN1</i> and <i>TMEM129</i> as a two-gene ER stress signature associated with asthma and propose them as potential diagnostic biomarkers for asthma.","affiliation":"Ethics Office, Beijing Chao-Yang Hospital, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Exacerbation","endoplasmic reticulum stress","Ern1","Tmem129"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2026.1840272"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13455042"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13455042?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["geo","refsnp"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-11","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42985417","source":"MED","pmid":"42985417","pmcid":"PMC13732517","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13732517"]},"doi":"10.29219/fnr.v70.14782","title":"Association of dietary fiber intake with current asthma and recent asthma outcomes: a cross-sectional analysis of National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2017-March 2020 pre-pandemic data.","authorString":"Zhao T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhao T","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Tingting","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Zhuji People's Hospital, Zhejiang, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"70","journalIssueId":4101519,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Food Nutr Res","title":"Food & nutrition research","essn":"1654-661X","issn":"1654-661X","nlmid":"101488795","isoabbreviation":"Food Nutr Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>Dietary fiber may influence asthma through the gut-lung axis and systemic inflammation, but evidence in contemporary U.S. adults remains limited.<h4>Methods</h4>We analyzed 6,145 adults aged 20 years or older from National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 2017-March 2020 pre-pandemic data using a common complete-case sample for the staged regression models. Day-1 dietary fiber was the primary exposure; a separate 2-day mean sensitivity analysis was conducted in 5,364 participants with a reliable second recall. Survey-weighted logistic regression assessed current asthma, asthma attacks and asthma-related acute-care visits with adjustment for demographic, socioeconomic, lifestyle, energy-intake and clinical covariates; body mass index (BMI) was evaluated separately. False-discovery-rate correction across the three outcomes and multiple imputation were used as sensitivity analyses.<h4>Results</h4>Current asthma was present in 596 participants (weighted prevalence, 8.7%). Each 1-standard-deviation increase in Day-1 fiber was associated with lower odds of current asthma in the primary model (odds ratio [OR], 0.80; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.64-0.99; <i>P</i> = 0.043), but not after false-discovery-rate correction (<i>q</i> = 0.129). BMI adjustment yielded OR 0.82 (95% CI, 0.66-1.02; <i>P</i> = 0.076). Separate repeated-recall and multiple-imputation analyses were directionally consistent (2-day mean: OR, 0.74; 95% CI, 0.61-0.90; multiple imputation: OR, 0.81; 95% CI, 0.66-0.99). Fiber was not associated with asthma attacks or acute-care visits.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Higher reported fiber intake showed an inverse association with current asthma, but statistical evidence was modest after multiple-outcome correction. Repeated-recall and missing-data analyses were directionally consistent. Cross-sectional design, reverse causation and residual confounding preclude causal interpretation.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Zhuji People's Hospital, Zhejiang, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","dietary fiber","Cross-sectional studies","Nutrition Surveys","Fatty Acids Volatile"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.29219/fnr.v70.14782"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13732517"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13732517?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-05","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-06","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42491097","source":"MED","pmid":"42491097","pmcid":"PMC13376247","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13376247"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmed.2026.1861336","title":"Effect of acupuncture on asthma control and body weight changes in obese patients with asthma.","authorString":"Shi Z, Quan Q, Bai X, Wang Y, Lai X, Hu X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Shi Z","lastName":"Shi","firstName":"Zhihong","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mongolian Medical Department, Affiliated Hospital of Inner Mongolia Medical University, Hohhot, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Quan Q","lastName":"Quan","firstName":"Qing","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"General Surgery, Xing'an League Mongolian Medicine Hospital, Ulanhot City, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Bai X","lastName":"Bai","firstName":"Xiaohong","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Mongolian Medicine, Inner Mongolia Minzu University, Tongliao, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Yanan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Imaging Department, Xilingol League Mongolian Medical Hospital, Xilinhot, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lai X","lastName":"Lai","firstName":"Xiao","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Brucellosis, International Mongolian Hospital, Hohhot, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu X","lastName":"Hu","firstName":"Xiaoyan","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine, Hangjin Banner Mongolian Medical General Hospital, Ordos, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4110742,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)","title":"Frontiers in medicine","essn":"2296-858X","issn":"2296-858X","nlmid":"101648047","isoabbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1861336","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Chronic persistent asthma is difficult to control and is often comorbid with obesity. Acupuncture has been shown to reduce airway inflammation and improve lung function, and it may also help regulate body weight to some extent. Currently, obesity combined with chronic persistent asthma is a research hotspot, but interventional studies involving acupuncture remain relatively limited. This study aims to fill this gap.<h4>Methods</h4>Patients were divided into a control group (conventional western medical treatment) and a treatment group (conventional western medical treatment plus acupuncture). Baseline characteristics between the two groups were compared using univariate analysis. Changes before and after treatment were assessed for pulmonary function indicators (PEF, FEV1, FEV1/FVC), Asthma Control Test (ACT) scores, Asthma Quality of Life Questionnaire (AQLQ) scores, serum inflammatory markers (IL-17, CRP), and BMI. Multivariate logistic regression and multiple linear regression analyses were conducted to evaluate the effects of treatment modality on asthma control and body weight changes. A response model was constructed to predict asthma control outcomes, and its performance was evaluated using the ROC curve. Further stratified analyses were performed to assess the predictive ability of the model in different subgroups.<h4>Results</h4>After treatment, the treatment group showed improvements in pulmonary function, asthma control, quality of life, inflammatory markers, and BMI. Treatment modality was independently associated with asthma control and weight changes. Asthma severity, comorbidities, baseline FEV1, and baseline CRP levels were also associated with asthma control. The response model demonstrated moderate discriminative ability (AUC = 0.656), with stronger effects observed in patients aged ≥50 years, males, and those without acute exacerbations in the past year.<h4>Conclusion</h4>This study provides a new intervention strategy and supporting evidence for individualized treatment in obese patients with chronic persistent asthma.","affiliation":"Mongolian Medical Department, Affiliated Hospital of Inner Mongolia Medical University, Hohhot, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Body weight","Obesity","Acupuncture","Asthma","Asthma Control"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2026.1861336"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13376247"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13376247?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42949886","source":"MED","pmid":"42949886","pmcid":"PMC13716200","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13716200"]},"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0361651","title":"Association of airway CCL28 with type 2 inflammation and secretory IgA in patients with asthma.","authorString":"Jiang X, Zhang J, Xu K, Yang X, Yan Y, Chai H, Ma J, Shi Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jiang X","lastName":"Jiang","firstName":"Xiaohan","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6361-4153"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang J","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Jinmei","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Nanjing First Hospital, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu K","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Ke","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Nanjing YuHua Hospital, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yang X","lastName":"Yang","firstName":"Xiaobing","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Nanjing First Hospital, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan Y","lastName":"Yan","firstName":"Yuanfei","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Nanjing YuHua Hospital, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chai H","lastName":"Chai","firstName":"Hao","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Nanjing First Hospital, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ma J","lastName":"Ma","firstName":"Jiyong","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Nanjing First Hospital, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi Y","lastName":"Shi","firstName":"Ying","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Nanjing First Hospital, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6361-4153"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4275831,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"PLoS One","title":"PloS one","essn":"1932-6203","issn":"1932-6203","nlmid":"101285081","isoabbreviation":"PLoS One"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0361651","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to investigate sputum CCL28 expression in patients with acute asthma exacerbations and its association with type 2 airway inflammation and secretory IgA. We further evaluated whether sputum CCL28, alone or in combination with routine clinical indicators, could assist in identifying eosinophilic asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>This study included 75 patients with non-severe acute asthma and 27 healthy controls. Induced sputum samples were collected, and the levels of CCL28, IL-4, IL-5, IL-10, IL-13, and secretory immunoglobulin A (sIgA) were measured in both sputum and serum samples. Sputum cytology was performed to classify airway inflammatory phenotypes as eosinophilic asthma (EA), neutrophilic asthma (NA), mixed granulocytic asthma (MA), and paucigranulocytic asthma (PA). Intergroup differences were analyzed using nonparametric tests, and correlations between variables were assessed using Spearman's rank correlation analysis. A predictive model for asthma phenotype identification was subsequently developed, and its predictive performance and potential clinical utility were further evaluated.<h4>Results</h4>CCL28 levels in induced sputum were significantly higher in patients with asthma than in healthy controls (P < 0.01), while the correlation between sputum and serum CCL28 levels was weak (ρ = 0.29). Sputum levels of IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, and secretory immunoglobulin A (sIgA) were significantly increased, whereas IL-10 levels were decreased (P < 0.01). CCL28 levels were significantly higher in the EA group than in the NA and PA groups (P < 0.05) and were positively correlated with IL-5, IL-13, sIgA, and sputum eosinophils (r = 0.83, 0.75, 0.66, and 0.55). In the EA prediction model, the combination of CCL28, fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO), and lung function parameters showed the best predictive performance (AUC = 0.79). Incorporation of CCL28 improved model discrimination and reclassification performance (IDI = 0.246, continuous NRI = 0.880, P < 0.001) suggesting additional predictive value beyond routine clinical indicators, and decision curve analysis demonstrated a greater net clinical benefit.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Sputum CCL28 was associated with type 2 inflammation and airway mucosal immunity in patients with asthma. An exploratory prediction model incorporating sputum CCL28 showed potential value in assisting the identification of eosinophilic asthma.","affiliation":"Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Eosinophils"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sputum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":