liation":[{"affiliation":"Centre of Excellence in Treatable Traits, College of Health Medicine and Wellbeing, University of Newcastle, Australia; Asthma and Breathing Program, Hunter Medical Research Institute, Newcastle, Australia; Department of Respiratory and Sleep Medicine, John Hunter Hospital, Newcastle, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ulrik CS","lastName":"Ulrik","firstName":"Charlotte Suppli","initials":"CS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Copenhagen, Denmark; Department of Clinical Medicine, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Backer V","lastName":"Backer","firstName":"Vibeke","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Medicine, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark; Department of Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery & Audiology, Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Freiesleben NC","lastName":"Freiesleben","firstName":"Nina la Cour","initials":"NC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5670-2015"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department Obstetrics and Gynaecology, The Fertility Clinic, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Copenhagen, Denmark; Department of Clinical Medicine, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5622-9347"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5865-489X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7447-6560"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8689-3695"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5670-2015"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2106-8103"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"261","journalIssueId":4242846,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"108951","abstractText":"Prolonged time to pregnancy, increased need for fertility treatment, and increased pregnancy loss have been reported in women with asthma. However, it is unknown whether uterine inflammatory cells negatively affects fertility in this group. We explored whether women with asthma undergoing fertility treatment have increased inflammatory cells in the endometrium compared to women without asthma. Thirty women with and without asthma (1:1) undergoing fertility treatment were included in this explorative observational study. Asthma was defined by symptoms and a positive bronchial challenge test. Participants underwent questionnaires, fractional exhaled nitric oxide measurement, blood sampling, spirometry, methacholine challenge, induced sputum, and 2/3D ultrasound scan of the uterus followed by endometrial biopsy near ovulation. Endometrial biopsies revealed no significant differences in inflammatory cell types between women with asthma versus women without asthma. Women with asthma had higher airway responsiveness, atopy, IgE, and eosinophil counts, as expected, but similar reproductive history and treatment characteristics. No differences were observed in plasma cells/mm2 (median(IQR) 0.15(0.14-0.65); 0.33(0.21-0.92), ns), mast cells/mm2 (median(IQR) 2.99(1.31-3.47); 3.72(3.25-9.57), ns) nor CD3<sup>+</sup> or CD20<sup>+</sup> lymphocytes or eosinophils. Biomarker analysis across tissues showed no significant association with asthma after multiple testing correction. This exploratory study found no evidence of differences in endometrial inflammatory cells between infertile women with and without asthma. However, the small sample size limits statistical power and a Type II error cannot be excluded.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Copenhagen, Denmark. Electronic address: anne.vejen.hansen@regionh.dk.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article","Observational Study"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Uterus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Endometrium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Eosinophils"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Infertility, Female","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunoglobulin E"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bronchial Provocation Tests","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Spirometry"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biopsy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biomarkers","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fractional Exhaled Nitric Oxide Testing"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Endometrium","Reproduction","Infertility","Asthma","In Vitro Fertilization","Meso Scale"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Immunoglobulin E","registryNumber":"37341-29-0"},{"name":"Biomarkers","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmed.2026.108951"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-08","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42505343","source":"MED","pmid":"42505343","pmcid":"PMC13406670","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13406670"]},"doi":"10.3390/cells15141233","title":"Asthma: Is It Time for Monocytes to Share the Spotlight?","authorString":"Vliagoftis H, Shrestha Palikhe N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Vliagoftis H","lastName":"Vliagoftis","firstName":"Harissios","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine and Alberta Respiratory Centre, University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB T6G 2S2, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Shrestha Palikhe N","lastName":"Shrestha Palikhe","firstName":"Nami","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine and Alberta Respiratory Centre, University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB T6G 2S2, Canada."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"14","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4233937,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cells","title":"Cells","essn":"2073-4409","issn":"2073-4409","nlmid":"101600052","isoabbreviation":"Cells"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1233","abstractText":"The evolving understanding of the regulation of allergic airway inflammation has offered new approaches for asthma therapy. For example, our understanding of the role of alarmins, Th2 cytokines and eosinophils has allowed the development of biologics that have revolutionized therapy for severe asthma. However, many questions remain and several of our patients are still not controlled with the current available therapies. Many immune cells have been implicated in asthma pathophysiology, but one cell that is missing from these studies is the monocyte. Monocytes (Mos) are bone marrow-derived cells that circulate in the blood and develop into macrophages and/or dendritic cells following migration to peripheral tissues. Macrophages (Møs) and dendritic cells have been implicated in the development and progression, of allergic airway inflammation, but also in tissue repair after inflammation. Recent studies in animal models suggest a major role for Mos in allergic airway inflammation, primarily through their ability to mediate recruitment of eosinophils and/or neutrophils to the airways. However, there is little information regarding the role of monocytes in human asthma. Here we review the literature regarding the presence and functions of peripheral blood and airway Mos in human asthma and suggest further work that needs to be done to consolidate the information on Mo functions. Studies show changes in Mo numbers and activation status in the peripheral blood of patients with asthma, changes that in many cases correlate with disease severity and/or activity. Studies also show altered phenotype of Mos present in the airways of patients with asthma. Detailed human studies need to be performed, if possible, studies that include therapeutic interventions, to allow for a full understanding of the role of Mos in asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Medicine and Alberta Respiratory Centre, University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB T6G 2S2, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Monocytes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Macrophages","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Inflammation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Monocytes","Asthma","airway"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells15141233"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13406670"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13406670?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":