 Brisbane, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Sly PD","lastName":"Sly","firstName":"Peter D","initials":"PD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6305-2201"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Children's Health and Environment Program, Child Health Research Centre, The University of Queensland, Brisbane, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Peake J","lastName":"Peake","firstName":"Jane","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Faculty of Health, Medicine and Behavioural Sciences, The University of Queensland, Brisbane, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Queensland Paediatric Immunology and Allergy Service, Queensland Children's Hospital, South Brisbane, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Clifton VL","lastName":"Clifton","firstName":"Vicki L","initials":"VL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4892-6748"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mater Research Institute, the University of Queensland, Brisbane, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6305-2201"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1050-4574"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1569-934X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4892-6748"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3321-6388"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4303966,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol","title":"Pediatric allergy and immunology : official publication of the European Society of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology","essn":"1399-3038","issn":"0905-6157","nlmid":"9106718","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70547","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Little is known about whether increasing maternal asthma symptom frequency is associated with adverse childhood health outcomes beyond respiratory and allergic diseases.<h4>Methods</h4>Data were obtained from a prospective cohort of 3033 mothers and 5768 children in the Australian Longitudinal Study on Women's Health (ALSWH) and the Mothers and their Children's Health (MatCH) substudy. Maternal self-reported asthma symptom frequency was classified as never, rarely, sometimes, or often (18-23 years). Childhood health outcomes were maternally reported (mean age 7 years). Models accounted for sibling clustering, were adjusted for maternal and child characteristics, and interactions by child sex were assessed.<h4>Results</h4>Graded associations were observed between maternal asthma symptom frequency and multiple offspring outcomes. The largest effects were for childhood asthma (aOR 3.11, 95% CI 2.16-4.48), chest infections (2.50, 1.82-3.44), parent-reported anxiety (2.37, 1.62-3.48), respiratory allergy (2.21, 1.54-3.15), non-food allergy (2.04, 1.33-3.12), and food intolerance (2.03, 1.28-3.20) among children of mothers reporting asthma symptoms often. Graded relationships were also evident for food allergy and eczema. No robust evidence of effect modification by child sex was observed.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Asthma symptom frequency in young women may have long-term implications for the health of their future children. These findings underscore the importance of optimizing asthma management before and throughout the reproductive years.","affiliation":"Mater Research Institute, the University of Queensland, Brisbane, Australia.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC)","grantId":"APP1059550","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Prenatal Exposure Delayed Effects","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Longitudinal Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mothers"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Australia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Young Adult"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Infection","Allergy","Anxiety","Cohort study","Child Health","Maternal Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/pai.70547"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42662812","source":"MED","pmid":"42662812","pmcid":"PMC13520886","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13520886"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.waojou.2026.101449","title":"Prevalence and cost implications of broadening asthma biologic eligibility criteria.","authorString":"Yang F, Geisler BP, d'Ancona G, Lee B, Jackson DJ, Bloom CI.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang F","lastName":"Yang","firstName":"Freda","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2858-1559"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Heart and Lung Institute, Imperial College London, London, UK."},{"affiliation":"Guy's & Brompton Asthma Network, Royal Brompton Hospital, Guy's and St Thomas' NHS Foundation Trust Hospital, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Geisler BP","lastName":"Geisler","firstName":"Benjamin P","initials":"BP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Management and Health Economics, University of Oslo, Oslo, Norway."}]}},{"fullName":"d'Ancona G","lastName":"d'Ancona","firstName":"Grainne","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Guy's & Brompton Asthma Network, Guy's Hospital, Guy's and St Thomas' NHS Foundation Trust Hospital, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee B","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Bohee","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9671-386X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Heart and Lung Institute, Imperial College London, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Jackson DJ","lastName":"Jackson","firstName":"David J","initials":"DJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Guy's & Brompton Asthma Network, Guy's Hospital, Guy's and St Thomas' NHS Foundation Trust Hospital, London, UK."},{"affiliation":"School of Immunology and Microbial Sciences, King's College London, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Bloom CI","lastName":"Bloom","firstName":"Chloe I","initials":"CI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Heart and Lung Institute, Imperial College London, London, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9671-386X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2858-1559"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4246066,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"World Allergy Organ J","title":"The World Allergy Organization journal","essn":"1939-4551","issn":"1939-4551","nlmid":"101481283","isoabbreviation":"World Allergy Organ J"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101449","abstractText":"<h4>Rationale</h4>Eligibility for biologic therapy in severe asthma varies internationally because government-funded schemes apply stricter criteria than pivotal trials. While relaxing eligibility thresholds may expand access, the effects on prevalence and healthcare costs remain uncertain.<h4>Objective</h4>To quantify how alternative biologic eligibility criteria affect prevalence, clinical characteristics, and budget impact in adults with asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>We studied adults with active asthma (2004-2021) using nationally representative primary care and hospital records. Biologic eligibility was assessed yearly using asthma severity (inhaled corticosteroid dose, additional controllers, rescue therapy) and exacerbations (oral corticosteroid prescriptions, emergency visits and hospitalisations). We assessed prevalence using United Kingdom (UK) criteria (severe asthma, ≥3 exacerbations), Global criteria (severe asthma, ≥1 or ≥2 exacerbations) and RCT criteria (moderate asthma, ≥1 or ≥2 exacerbations). Anti-IL-5/5Rα eligibility was evaluated using blood eosinophil counts. Budget impact was modelled over 5 years.<h4>Results</h4>Among 1,306,307 asthma patients, 7.2% had severe asthma. Under UK criteria, 19,093 patients were biologic-eligible (20.4% of severe asthma; 1.5% of all asthma). Global criteria increased eligibility to 2.4% (≥2 exacerbations) to 4.3% (≥1 exacerbation), while RCT criteria expanded eligibility to 4.7% (≥2 exacerbations) and 9% (≥1 exacerbation). Among patients with eosinophil data, 38-45% met anti-IL-5/5Rα criteria. UK-eligible patients had higher prevalence of lower respiratory tract infections, pneumonia, and bronchiectasis. Five-year costs were £261 M under UK criteria, £436-767 M under Global criteria, and £878M-1.6B under RCT criteria.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Only one-fifth of patients with severe asthma met UK biologic eligibility. Global criteria tripled eligibility and RCT criteria increased it six-fold, with substantial budget implications.","affiliation":"National Heart and Lung Institute, Imperial College London, London, UK.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Severe asthma","Exacerbations","Biologic","Anti-il5","Anti-tslp"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.waojou.2026.101449"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13520886"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13520886?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-10","hasEvaluations":"N