A","lastName":"Havard","firstName":"Alys","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6563-8804"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Population Health, University of New South Wales, Sydney, New South Wales, Australia."},{"affiliation":"National Drug and Alcohol Research Centre, University of New South Wales, Sydney, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Robijn AL","lastName":"Robijn","firstName":"Annelies L","initials":"AL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Population Health, University of New South Wales, Sydney, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Gong T","lastName":"Gong","firstName":"Tong","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6887-9432"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Stockholm County, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Smew AI","lastName":"Smew","firstName":"Awad I","initials":"AI","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4653-6615"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Stockholm County, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Department of Perioperative Medicine and Intensive Care, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Rejnö G","lastName":"Rejnö","firstName":"Gustaf","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8095-5629"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Stockholm County, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynecology Unit, Stockholm South General Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Chambers G","lastName":"Chambers","firstName":"Georgina","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Perinatal Epidemiology and Statistical Unit, Centre for Big Data Research in Health, University of New South Wales, Sydney, New South Wales, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Almqvist C","lastName":"Almqvist","firstName":"Catarina","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Stockholm County, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Pediatric Allergy and Pulmonology Unit at Astrid Lindgren Children's Hospital, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6563-8804"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4653-6615"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6546-3650"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6887-9432"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8095-5629"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9885-8261"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3282-1324"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4206001,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Open Respir Res","title":"BMJ open respiratory research","essn":"2052-4439","issn":"2052-4439","nlmid":"101638061","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Open Respir Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e004010","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) use in pregnancy has shown associations with decreased offspring asthma in childhood, whereas exacerbations in pregnancy may increase the risk. However, these findings have not been tested in population-based cohorts with rigorous assessment of confounding structures. The aim was to describe asthma exacerbations and ICS utilisation during pregnancy and to assess causal pathways between pregnancy exacerbations, ICS use and offspring asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>Register-based cohort study of pregnancies in New South Wales (NSW), Australia and Sweden. Maternal monthly asthma exacerbations (hospital visits and/or oral corticosteroid (OCS) dispensings) and ICS dispensing rates were calculated 12 months prepregnancy to 12 months post partum. Among mothers with asthma (35 194 NSW, 102 248 Sweden), Hazard Ratios (HR)s were calculated for pregnancy exacerbations or ICS use and offspring asthma adjusting for confounders including asthma severity. Associations between paternal exposures and offspring asthma and sibling analyses assessed the likelihood of unmeasured confounding.<h4>Results</h4>Maternal asthma prevalence was 8.6% in NSW and 7.1% in Sweden. While ICS use was maintained over the course of pregnancy, a postpartum decline was observed, accompanied by a rise in OCS use. Adjusted HRs for offspring asthma were 1.47 (95% CI 1.32 to 1.64, NSW) and 1.30 (95% CI 1.24 to 1.36, Sweden) following maternal exacerbations, and 1.37 (95% CI 1.26 to 1.49, NSW) and 1.23 (95% CI 1.19 to 1.27, Sweden) following maternal ICS use. Paternal exposures were also associated with offspring asthma, while associations in sibling analyses were not significant.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Observed associations between maternal exacerbations, ICS use and offspring asthma appear to be due to unmeasured confounding. Management of maternal asthma remains a priority in pregnancy and post partum.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Stockholm County, Sweden bronwyn.haasdyk.brew@ki.se.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pregnancy Complications","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Prenatal Exposure Delayed Effects","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AD","qualifierName":"administration & dosage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Administration, Inhalation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Registries"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prevalence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cohort Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Australia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"New South Wales","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sweden","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Paediatric Asthma","Asthma Epidemiology"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjresp-2025-004010"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13331117"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13331117?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42486256","source":"MED","pmid":"42486256","doi":"10.1016/j.eprac.2026.07.013","title":"Effect of Testosterone on Asthma Exacerbations.","authorString":"Nayak Y, Deol E, Doherty JM, Liebich J, Yu K, Ayoub Y, Amini M, Ghimire A, Portillo-Canales S, Patel P, Kamel G, Dhindsa S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nayak Y","lastName":"Nayak","firstName":"Yash","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6356-0263"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Deol E","lastName":"Deol","firstName":"Ekamjit","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Doherty JM","lastName":"Doherty","firstName":"Jason M","initials":"JM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Advanced HEAlth Data (AHEAD) Research Institute, Saint Louis University School of Medicine, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Liebich J","lastName":"Liebich","firstName":"Jessica","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu K","lastName":"Yu","firstName":"Kevin","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Ayoub Y","lastName":"Ayoub","firstName":"Yasmeen","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Amini M","lastName":"Amini","firstName":"Mehdia","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghimire A","lastName":"Ghimire","firstName":"Allina","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Portillo-Canales S","lastName":"Portillo-Canales","firstName":"Shellsea","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Patel P","lastName":"Patel","firstName":"Parishi","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamel G","lastName":"Kamel","firstName":"Ghassan","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care & Sleep Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri."}]}},{"fullName":"Dhindsa S","lastName":"Dhindsa","firstName":"Sandeep","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri. Electronic address: sandeep.dhindsa@health.slu.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6356-0263"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4226734,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Endocr Pract","title":"Endocrine practice : official journal of the American College of Endocrinology and the American Association of Clinical Endocrinologists","essn":"1934-2403","issn":"1530-891X","nlmid":"9607439","isoabbreviation":"Endocr Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S1530-891X(26)01078-5","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>Epidemiologic studies have suggested that serum testosterone (T) concentrations are inversely related to asthma prevalence as well as exacerbations in men. It is not known if testosterone therapy (TTh) and androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) have any effect on asthma exacerbations.<h4>Methods</h4>This is a retrospective study conducted in the electronic health records of a tertiary hospital database. We included adult men with asthma who had received either TTh or ADT. Asthma exacerbation was defined as hospitalization due to asthma or usage of oral prednisone if exacerbation was treated in the outpatient setting.<h4>Results</h4>The study included 229 men who had received TTh (mean age 60 ± 12 years, body mass index 34 ± 7 kg/m<sup>2</sup>) and 93 men who had received ADT (mean age 75 ± 8 years, body mass index 31 ± 7 kg/m<sup>2</sup>). Patients took TTh for a median of 5.3 (IQR 2.4, 9.1) years. The rate of asthma exacerbations per 1,000 patient-years before TTh and on TTh was similar (96.4 [95% CI 75.2, 121.8] before vs 87.2 [95% CI 66.2, 112.8] after, rate ratio = 1.1 [95% CI 0.0, 1.5], P = .70). Results were similar in ADT group. Patients took ADT for median time of 2.05 (IQR 1.0, 4.0) years. The rate of asthma exacerbations before ADT and during ADT was similar (60.44 [95% CI 40.8, 86.3] vs 58.3 [95% CI 30.1, 101.8] respectively, rate ratio = 1.0 [95% CI 0.0, undefined], P = .50).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Our study shows that TTh or ADT in men with asthma is not associated with a change in asthma exacerbations. These data are informative since hypogonadism, prostate cancer, and asthma are commonly encountered comorbidities in the ambulatory setting.","affiliation":"School of Medicine, Saint Louis University, St. Louis, Missouri.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Testosterone","Asthma","Hypogonadism"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eprac.2026.07.013"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-15","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-23","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42742871","source":"MED","pmid":"42742871","doi":"10.1007/s11882-026-01293-2","title":"Early Life Exposure to Air Pollutants and Childhood Asthma.","authorString":"Gorska MM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gorska MM","lastName":"Gorska","firstName":"Magdalena M","initials":"MM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7364-8703"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, National Jewish Health, Denver, CO, USA. gorskam@njhealth.org."},{"affiliation":"Section of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Division of Pulmonary, Allergy and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus, Aurora, CO, USA. gorskam@njhealth.org."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7364-8703"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4252034,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Allergy Asthma Rep","title":"Current allergy and asthma reports","essn":"1534-6315","issn":"1529-7322","nlmid":"101096440","isoabbreviation":"Curr Allergy Asthma Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"50","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose of review</h4>This manuscript summarizes and comments on recent studies linking prenatal and early postnatal exposure to air pollution and childhood asthma.<h4>Recent findings</h4>Recent human cohort studies linked early life exposure to air pollutants with epigenetic modifications in airway epithelial cells, revealed a novel epigenetic convergence point for tobacco smoke exposure and genetic predisposition to asthma, uncovered novel serum biomarkers of air pollution exposure, and new airborne toxic compound combinations with relevance to asthma. A new mechanistic study in mice has revealed that transmission of asthma predisposing information from the air pollutant-exposed mother to offspring occurs via NK cells. Recent studies uncovered novel biomarkers and mechanisms of pollutant-induced asthma susceptibility in early life. Although valuable discoveries were made, knowledge on the impact of early life air pollution on asthma remains far from complete. There is a need for more mechanistic studies to better understand how environmental information is sensed, processed, encoded, transmitted between cells, tissues and generations, and transformed into biological outcomes.","affiliation":"Division of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, Department of Medicine, National Jewish Health, Denver, CO, USA. gorskam@njhealth.org.","publication