on":"Izmir City Hospital, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Izmir, Turkey; Izmir Katip Celebi University, Faculty of Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology. Electronic address: ilke_66@icloud.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Tunca S","lastName":"Tunca","firstName":"Seda","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Izmir City Hospital, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Izmir, Turkey. Electronic address: tuncaseda@hotmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Akarcan SE","lastName":"Akarcan","firstName":"Sanem Eren","initials":"SE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Izmir City Hospital, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Izmir, Turkey. Electronic address: saneren@yahoo.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Demir BK","lastName":"Demir","firstName":"Belde Kasap","initials":"BK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Izmir City Hospital, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Nephrology, Izmir, Turkey; Izmir Katip Celebi University, Faculty of Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Nephrology. Electronic address: beldekasap@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Tuncel T","lastName":"Tuncel","firstName":"Tuba","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Izmir City Hospital, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Izmir, Turkey; Izmir Katip Celebi University, Faculty of Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology. Electronic address: ttuncel@yahoo.com.tr."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6927-1261"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9326-2541"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1324-232X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2675-1150"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4277025,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol","title":"Annals of allergy, asthma & immunology : official publication of the American College of Allergy, Asthma, & Immunology","essn":"1534-4436","issn":"1081-1206","nlmid":"9503580","isoabbreviation":"Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S1081-1206(26)01304-9","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Airway vascular remodeling is well described in asthma, but whether it extends to the peripheral microcirculation is unknown. Nailfold videocapillaroscopy (NVC) allows non-invasive assessment of the microvasculature.<h4>Objective</h4>We aimed to evaluate NVC findings in children with asthma and their associations with disease characteristics.<h4>Methods</h4>This single-center, cross-sectional study was conducted in children aged 8-18 years with asthma at a tertiary pediatric allergy clinic. NVC was performed at 200 × magnification per the EULAR protocol. Findings were compared with age-specific reference values from healthy children using identical methodology.<h4>Results</h4>Thirty-six children with asthma (mean age 12.01 ± 2.66 years; 66.7% male) were enrolled. Versus healthy references, patients had lower capillary density (p < 0.0001), with greater capillary loop diameter (p = 0.03) and width (p < 0.001). Dilated and tortuous capillaries were also more frequent (p < 0.003). Capillary density correlated inversely with BMI SDS (r = -0.33, p = 0.047). Moderate asthma was independently associated with lower capillary density (p = 0.03). Non-inhalant-sensitized patients had lower capillary density (p = 0.02), with greater capillary loop diameter (p = 0.01) and intercapillary distance (p = 0.03). Microhemorrhages (p < 0.01) as well as non-specific patterns (p = 0.02) were also more frequent in this group. Neither giant capillaries nor a scleroderma pattern was detected in any participant.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Children with asthma show distinct peripheral microvascular alterations on NVC, more pronounced with greater severity, higher BMI, and a non-atopic phenotype. NVC may enable non-invasive vascular phenotyping in pediatric asthma.","affiliation":"Izmir City Hospital, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology, Izmir, Turkey. Electronic address: riza_yildirim@windowslive.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Children","Asthma","Capillary density","Atopy","Microcirculation, Nailfold Videocapillaroscopy"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anai.2026.10.027"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-23","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42671407","source":"MED","pmid":"42671407","pmcid":"PMC13568552","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13568552"]},"doi":"10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_751_25","title":"A prospective observational study to evaluate the prevalence of severe asthma among subjects with difficult-to-treat asthma at a tertiary care center in North India.","authorString":"Sharma S, Agarwal R, Muthu V, Rudramurthy SM, Kumar Y, Prabhakar N, Sehgal IS, Dhooria S, Aggarwal AN, Prasad KT.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sharma S","lastName":"Sharma","firstName":"Sandeep","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Agarwal R","lastName":"Agarwal","firstName":"Ritesh","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Muthu V","lastName":"Muthu","firstName":"Valliappan","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Rudramurthy SM","lastName":"Rudramurthy","firstName":"Shivaprakash M","initials":"SM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Microbiology, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar Y","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Yashwant","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunopathology, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Prabhakar N","lastName":"Prabhakar","firstName":"Nidhi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiodiagnosis and Imaging, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Sehgal IS","lastName":"Sehgal","firstName":"Inderpaul Singh","initials":"IS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Dhooria S","lastName":"Dhooria","firstName":"Sahajal","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Aggarwal AN","lastName":"Aggarwal","firstName":"Ashutosh N","initials":"AN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Prasad KT","lastName":"Prasad","firstName":"Kuruswamy Thurai","initials":"KT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"43","journalIssueId":4250552,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Lung India","title":"Lung India : official organ of Indian Chest Society","essn":"0974-598X","issn":"0970-2113","nlmid":"8405380","isoabbreviation":"Lung India"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"488-495","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Severe asthma accounts for disproportionate asthma-related morbidity, mortality, and healthcare costs, yet prospective data on its prevalence remain limited, particularly for developing countries. We aimed to determine the prevalence of severe asthma among subjects with difficult-to-treat asthma at a tertiary care center in North India.<h4>Methods</h4>Between January and December 2023, we prospectively enrolled consecutive adults with difficult-to-treat asthma that remained uncontrolled despite treatment with medium- or high-dose inhaled corticosteroids-long-acting beta-agonist (ICS-LABA) or required high-dose ICS-LABA to maintain good control. All subjects underwent systematic evaluation, including diagnosis confirmation, assessment of inhaler technique and adherence, comorbidity screening, and treatment optimization, with reassessment after 3-6 months. We diagnosed severe asthma (uncontrolled despite high-dose ICS-LABA or required high-dose ICS-LABA to maintain good control) after addressing all modifiable factors and assessed type 2 (T2)-targeted biological therapy eligibility.<h4>Results</h4>Among 240 subjects (mean age 47.4 ± 14.9 years; 68.8% female), we identified modifiable factors in 218 (90.8%), including suboptimal therapy (74.2%), poor adherence (64.2%), and incorrect technique (58.8%). Following interventions, 154 completed reassessment; 102 (66.2%) no longer met severe asthma criteria. Overall, 67 subjects had severe asthma (27.9%; 95% CI: 22.4-33.9%). Among them, 66.7% had blood eosinophils ≥150 cells/µL, and 68.7% met criteria for T2-targeted biological therapy.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Approximately one-quarter of subjects with difficult-to-treat asthma have confirmed severe asthma after systematic assessment. A significant proportion improve with targeted interventions, emphasizing the importance of comprehensive evaluation. Most patients with confirmed severe asthma are candidates for T2-targeted biologicals, highlighting the need for structured assessment protocols and improved access to biological therapies.","affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Severe asthma","Biological Therapy","Abpa","Difficult-to-treat Asthma","Type 2 Inflammation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_751_25"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13568552"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13568552?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-31","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-01","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42962920","source":"MED","pmid":"42962920","doi":"10.1016/j.jaip.2026.10.028","title":"Sex differences in the clinical phenotype of difficult-to-treat asthma in adults.","authorString":"Ahsan Z, Freeman A, Moyses HE, Charles D, Ainsworth B, Wei L, Michael Haitchi H, Eames C, Hudson-Colby JJ, Lewis A, Varkonyi-Sepp J, Rupani H, Dennison P, Arshad SH, Djukanovic R, Dabrio-Reina E, Vladimirovna Vorobeva E, Kurukulaaratchy R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ahsan Z","lastName":"Ahsan","firstName":"Zoya","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Clinical and Experimental Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Freeman A","lastName":"Freeman","firstName":"Anna","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Clinical and Experimental Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, UK; National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR) Southampton Biomedical Research Centre at University Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK; Respiratory Medicine Department, University Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Moyses HE","lastName":"Moyses","firstName":"Helen E","initials":"HE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Institute for Health and Care Research (NI