0000-0001-8021-2780"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8086-6653"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8393-5060"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8601-6887"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8639-0473"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8741-3067"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8795-5528"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9058-7383"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9204-1923"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9860-7588"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0287-9915"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0708-4281"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0925-3851"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2220-7745"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2549-0523"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2933-3111"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2996-8248"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3042-0765"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3091-7237"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4448-3468"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4675-9616"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5160-7502"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5175-908X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5189-4265"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5296-3349"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5609-4160"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6302-6115"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6667-4708"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6932-712X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7300-928X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7586-2276"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8067-1204"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8135-0568"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8961-8055"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9046-759X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9728-9992"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0133-0100"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0432-2704"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0902-0860"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0930-1654"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1628-9948"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1738-1826"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1837-2980"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2006-7221"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2401-0070"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2497-4128"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4540-9822"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4557-2515"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4852-3102"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-1493-7101"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-4249-9323"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-7176-2562"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4303966,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol","title":"Pediatric allergy and immunology : official publication of the European Society of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology","essn":"1399-3038","issn":"0905-6157","nlmid":"9106718","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70526","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Achieving and maintaining pediatric asthma control depends on effective and regular monitoring. While a wide range of monitoring tools are available, how and why clinicians use these in routine practice has not been formally characterized. Here we describe distinct real-world monitoring patterns among healthcare professionals (HCPs) caring for children with asthma in an international convenience survey, examining the geographic, professional, and institutional factors associated with them.<h4>Methods</h4>Data from the international cross-sectional Pediatric Asthma in Real Life (PeARL) HCP survey, administered to clinicians with expertise in childhood asthma, were analyzed. Monitoring patterns were derived by Ward agglomerative hierarchical clustering on four standardized domains: clinical evaluation, spirometry, fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO), and home-monitoring. Independent associations between cluster membership and region, specialty, sector, and level of care were estimated by multinomial logistic regression (MLR).<h4>Results</h4>951 HCPs from 78 countries were included. Four monitoring patterns emerged: Low-intensity (n = 88, 9.3%), lower overall monitoring intensity but moderate clinical assessment; Clinical (n = 449, 47.2%), predominantly clinical assessment with moderate spirometry but infrequent FeNO and home monitoring; Clinical & Objective (n = 308, 32.4%) with additional regular spirometry with moderate FeNO use and Comprehensive (n = 106, 11.1%), also providing home monitoring. In MLR (n = 933), region and care level were significantly associated with cluster membership (p < .001), and specialty (p = .012) and sector (p = .039) also contributed independently. The most pronounced gradients involved objective in-clinic testing: specialists were 3.5-fold (95% CI 1.68-7.37) more likely than generalists, and tertiary-care HCPs 3.3-fold (95% CI 1.99-5.58) more likely than primary-care HCPs, to belong to the Clinical & Objective pattern, with routine use of objective measures concentrated in Western and Northern European practices. Home monitoring showed no consistent gradient across the measured characteristics.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Real-world pediatric asthma monitoring follows recognizable patterns, distinguished by the use of objective in-clinic testing and between-visit home-based practices. Nearly half of HCPs relied predominantly on clinical assessment with limited use of FeNO and home-based monitoring, and only a minority integrated home-monitoring tools. Objective in-clinic testing concentrated in specialist services and Western/Northern European practices, while home-monitoring adoption showed no consistent gradient. These findings identify factors associated with pediatric asthma monitoring and suggest system-level factors that warrant direct evaluation in dedicated implementation studies.","affiliation":"Allergy Department, Second Pediatric Clinic, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institute for Health Research (NIHR)","grantId":"NIHR203308","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nitric Oxide"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Spirometry"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Monitoring, Physiologic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Health Personnel"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Practice Patterns, Physicians'","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fractional Exhaled Nitric Oxide Testing"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical practice","Spirometry","Lung Function Tests","Pediatric Asthma","Asthma Monitoring","Home‐monitoring"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Nitric Oxide","registryNumber":"31C4KY9ESH"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/pai.70526"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13746309"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13746309?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42710878","source":"MED","pmid":"42710878","doi":"10.1542/peds.2025-074217","title":"Medicaid ACO Implementation and Pediatric Asthma Care Equity.","authorString":"Geissler KH, Shieh MS, Poronsky KE, Lindenauer PK, Ash AS, Krishnan JA, Goff SL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Geissler KH","lastName":"Geissler","firstName":"Kimberley H","initials":"KH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Delivery and Population Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School-Baystate, Springfield, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Shieh MS","lastName":"Shieh","firstName":"Meng-Shiou","initials":"MS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Delivery and Population Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School-Baystate, Springfield, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Poronsky KE","lastName":"Poronsky","firstName":"Kye E","initials":"KE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Delivery and Population Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School-Baystate, Springfield, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Lindenauer PK","lastName":"Lindenauer","firstName":"Peter K","initials":"PK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Delivery and Population Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School-Baystate, Springfield, Massachusetts."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School-Baystate, Springfield, Massachusetts."},{"affiliation":"Department of Population and Quantitative Health Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School, Worcester, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Ash AS","lastName":"Ash","firstName":"Arlene S","initials":"AS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Population and Quantitative Health Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School, Worcester, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Krishnan JA","lastName":"Krishnan","firstName":"Jerry A","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics, School of Public Health, University of Illinois Chicago, Chicago, Illinois."},{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, Sleep and Allergy, College of Medicine, University of Illinois Chicago, Chicago, Illinois."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Healthcare Delivery Design, University of Illinois Chicago, Chicago, Illinois."}]}},{"fullName":"Goff SL","lastName":"Goff","firstName":"Sarah L","initials":"SL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Promotion and Policy, School of Public Health & Health Sciences, University of Massachusetts Amherst, Amherst, Massachusetts."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"158","journalIssueId":4275300,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatrics","title":"Pediatrics","essn":"1098-4275","issn":"0031-4005","nlmid":"0376422","isoabbreviation":"Pediatrics"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e2025074217","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>Poorly controlled asthma disproportionately impacts children from lower-income and racially or ethnically minority groups. We examined whether Massachusetts Medicaid Accountable Care Organization (ACO) implementation was associated with improved pediatric asthma care and outcomes and whether associations varied by race and ethnicity.<h4>Methods</h4>Using a difference-in-differences approach, we compared pre-implementation (2016-2017) to post-implementation (2018-2021) outcomes for Medicaid-insured children assigned to ACOs to those not assigned to ACOs. We examined differences overall and by race and ethnicity, using regression models accounting for differences in child demographics, health status, year, and ZIP code characteristics.<h4>Results</h4>Among 282 334 child-year observations (117 757 children), 80.7% were attributed to ACOs. Racial and ethnic categories included non-Hispanic White (37.9%), Hispanic (36.2%), and non-Hispanic Black (18.0%). ACO implementation was associated with a 3.0 percentage point (pp) increase in the probability of a routine asthma visit (95% CI: 2.1-3.8 pp), a decrease in an asthma medication ratio greater than 0.5 of 2.7 pp (95% CI: -5.3 to -0.2 pp), and a 2.1-pp decrease in the probability of any acute care (95% CI: -3.0 to -1.3 pp) for those in Medicaid ACOs vs other Medicaid-insured children. We found no consistent evidence that ACO implementation reduced racial and ethnic disparities in asthma care or outcomes.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Implementation of Massachusetts Medicaid ACOs was associated with modest improvements in asthma care for routine asthma visits and acute care use but declines in appropriate medication use. Importantly, ACOs did not consistently reduce existing racial and ethnic disparities. Additional changes in the design of value-based care models in Medicaid are needed to address racial and ethnic disparities.","affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Delivery and Population Sciences, University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School-Baystate, Springfield, Massachusetts.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EH","qualifierName":"ethnology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child, Preschool"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Medicaid"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Massachusetts"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Healthcare Disparities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EH","qualifierName":"ethnology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Accountable Care Organizations"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Socioeconomic Disparities in Health"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2025-074217"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"42710892","reference":"Pediatrics. 2026 Oct 1;158(4):e2026076869. doi: 10.1542/peds.2026-076869.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42377150","source":"MED","pmid":"42377150","pmcid":"PMC13367551","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13367551"]},"doi":"10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_605_25","title":"Asthma, COPD, and asthma-COPD overlap: A comparative study of inflammatory biomarkers and quality of life.","authorString":"Safwan MA, Goel N, Kumar R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Safwan MA","lastName":"Safwan","firstName":"M Ahmed","initials":"MA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, MES Medical College, Perinthalmanna, Kerala, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Goel N","lastName":"Goel","firstName":"Nitin","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Vallabhbhai Patel Chest Institute, University of Delhi, Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar R","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Raj","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, Vallabhbhai Patel Chest Institute, University of Delhi, Delhi, India."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"43","journalIssueId":4222964,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Lung India","title":"Lung India : official organ of Indian Chest Society","essn":"0974-598X","issn":"0970-2113","nlmid":"8405380","isoabbreviation":"Lung India"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"364-370","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Asthma, COPD, and ACO are chronic airway disorders with distinct and overlapping features. This study aimed to compare clinical profiles, inflammatory biomarkers, and quality of life among these groups.<h4>Methods</h4>A cross-sectional study was conducted on 168 patients (56 each with asthma, COPD, and ACO). All participants underwent clinical assessment, spirometry, St George's Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ), skin prick testing, and measurement of inflammatory markers, including FeNO, eosinophil count, total IgE, vitamin D, hs-CRP, and interleukins (IL-4, 5, 6, 8, 13, 17, 33).<h4>Results</h4>ACO patients were younger than COPD patients but older than asthma patients. Asthma patients had the highest prevalence of allergic rhinitis and skin prick positivity. FEV 1 and FEV 1 /FVC were significantly lower in COPD and ACO compared to asthma, but not different between COPD and ACO. Bronchodilator response was higher in asthma and ACO than in COPD. Asthma patients had significantly higher FeNO, eosinophil counts, and IgE levels compared to COPD, with ACO showing intermediate values. IL-4 and IL-5 were highest in asthma, IL-6 and IL-8 in COPD, with ACO again showing intermediate levels. ACO patients had the highest SGRQ scores, indicating the worst quality of life. Correlation analysis showed group-specific associations between interleukins and clinical parameters.<h4>Conclusion</h4>ACO exhibits intermediate clinical and inflammatory profiles between asthma and COPD. While individual biomarkers assist in characterizing these diseases, a combination of clinical, functional, and inflammatory parameters offers better differentiation. ACO patients experience a significant symptom burden, necessitating tailored diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.","affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Medicine, MES Medical College, Perinthalmanna, Kerala, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Interleukins","Asthma","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)","Inflammatory Biomarkers","St George’s Respiratory Questionnaire (Sgrq)","Asthma–copd Overlap (Aco)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_605_25"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13367551"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13367551?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42957060","source":"MED","pmid":"42957060","doi":"10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109240","title":"Respiratory and gastrointestinal mycobiome profiles identify distinct patient clusters associated with asthma control.","authorString":"Kanj AN, Wireko FW, Zhao CY, Limper AH, Skalski JH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kanj AN","lastName":"Kanj","firstName":"Amjad N","initials":"AN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0470-3603"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wireko FW","lastName":"Wireko","firstName":"Felix W","initials":"FW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9731-3481"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao CY","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Conan Y","initials":"CY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5714-2286"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mayo Clinic Alix School of Medicine, Rochester, MN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Limper AH","lastName":"Limper","firstName":"Andrew H","initials":"AH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Skalski JH","lastName":"Skalski","firstName":"Joseph H","initials":"JH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9639-0220"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, USA. Electronic address: Skalski.Joseph@mayo.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5714-2286"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9639-0220"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0470-3603"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9731-3481"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"264","journalIssueId":4294503,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109240","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>The human microbiome contributes to asthma pathogenesis, yet most studies have focused on bacterial communities. This pilot study aimed to characterize fungal communities across multiple body sites in adults with asthma and evaluate whether integrated multisite mycobiome profiles are associated with asthma control.<h4>Patients and methods</h4>We enrolled 24 adults with asthma evaluated at Mayo Clinic between 2020 and 2022. Oral wash, nasal swab, sputum, and stool samples were collected, and fungal communities were characterized using ITS2 sequencing. Multisite mycobiome data were integrated using an unsupervised clustering approach, with a two-cluster solution selected for exploratory clinical comparisons. Clusters were compared with respect to fungal composition, site-specific diversity metrics, and asthma control.<h4>Results</h4>Two patient clusters with distinct multisite mycobiome profiles were identified. Cluster 1 demonstrated poorer asthma control, with lower Asthma Control Test scores than Cluster 2 (median [IQR], 15.0 [13.0-17.5] vs. 18.0 [17.0-19.0]; p = 0.040). Compared with Cluster 2, Cluster 1 exhibited a higher relative abundance of Cladosporium in nasal and sputum samples, and greater sputum fungal diversity (p = 0.016). In contrast, Cluster 2 exhibited a higher relative abundance of Candida in stool and sputum samples. No significant differences in fungal diversity were observed in oral, nasal, or stool samples.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Integrated multisite mycobiome profiling identified distinct fungal community patterns associated with asthma control, highlighting the need for larger, longitudinal studies to define their clinical relevance.","affiliation":"Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"T32 HL105355","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cluster analysis","Asthma","Microbial Dysbiosis","Mycobiome","Fungal Microbiome","Gut-lung Axis"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109240"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1360478","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9634309/v1","title":"Association of asthma control with household particulate matter less than 2.5 microns, carbon monoxide and second-hand-smoking among children with asthma in Lagos, Nigeria: protocol of an ongoing observational cross-sectional study","authorString":"Ubuane PO, Ademokoya O, Awokola BI, Ojo OT, Fapohunda-Ayanda TV, Adigun A, Sodipo O, Odusote OA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ubuane PO","lastName":"Ubuane","firstName":"Peter Odion","initials":"PO"},{"fullName":"Ademokoya O","lastName":"Ademokoya","firstName":"Olufemi","initials":"O"},{"fullName":"Awokola BI","lastName":"Awokola","firstName":"Babatunde","initials":"BI"},{"fullName":"Ojo OT","lastName":"Ojo","firstName":"Olufemi Tunde","initials":"OT"},{"fullName":"Fapohunda-Ayanda TV","lastName":"Fapohunda-Ayanda","firstName":"Temitope Victoria","initials":"TV"},{"fullName":"Adigun A","lastName":"Adigun","firstName":"Abass","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Sodipo O","lastName":"Sodipo","firstName":"Oluwajimi","initials":"O"},{"fullName":"Odusote OA","lastName":"Odusote","firstName":"Olatunde Adegboyega","initials":"OA"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>  <bold>Background</bold>  Childhood asthma is increasing in developing countries, partly due to household air pollution (HAP). Asthma care aims for optimal symptoms control and minimal future risk of exacerbations, known as asthma control (AC). There is a high prevalence of suboptimal AC among Nigerian children and adolescents with asthma, with consequent increased risk of psychosocial, academic and financial burden and, sometimes, fatality. The adverse role of HAP on AC may be significant because of high levels of air pollutants like particulate matter less than 2.5 microns in aerodynamic diameter (  <italic>PM</italic>  <sub>  <italic>2.5</italic>  </sub>  ) and carbon monoxide (CO) in Nigerian homes, and prevalent exposures to second-hand smokes (SHS). Previous studies mostly evaluated the association between HAP and asthma with questionnaires, with no detectable association, possibly because of recall and social desirability biases. Thus, this study plans to determine the association between AC and objectively-quantified household  <italic>PM</italic>  <sub>  <italic>2.5</italic>  </sub>  and  <italic>CO</italic>  and SHS (urinary cotinine) among children with asthma accessing routine outpatient care at the Asthma Clinics of the Paediatric Allergology, Dermatology & Respiratory Medicine Unit, and the Adult Respiratory Clinic of LASUTH.  <bold>Methods</bold>  Eligible children and adolescents (up to age 18 years) are being consecutively recruited into this observational cross-sectional study in which the following data are obtained: socio-demographics, medical history, AC, medication adherence, inhalation technique, parental asthma-related knowledge, lung function (peak flow, FEV  <sub>1</sub>  , home personal PEF variability), quality of life, urine cotinine level, household  <italic>PM</italic>  <sub>  <italic>2.5</italic>  </sub>  and CO. Hierarchical multivariable logistic regression will be used to quantify the association of AC with PM  <sub>2.5</sub>  , CO and urinary cotinine while controlling for potential confounders.  <bold>Discussion</bold>  The findings from this study may be useful for education of families of children with asthma, advocacy with policymakers on air pollution control, as well as the design or strengthening of educational interventions to mitigate air pollution in households of children with asthma, for example counselling on cessation of smoking or use of cleaner cooking fuels.  <bold>Trial Registration:</bold>  Research project was registered at OSF with the project DOI: https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/2JS7P.  </p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9634309/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-07","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1360478","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-11-06","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42860276","source":"MED","pmid":"42860276","doi":"10.1183/13993003.00317-2026","title":"Dysregulated NK cell phenotype in exacerbation-prone severe asthma.","authorString":"Duvall MG, Dorazio MH, Chiha T, Reilly RB, Ramdour SK, Krishnamoorthy N, Cagnina RE, Phipatanakul W, Israel E, Levy BD, National Heart Lung and Blood Institute's Severe Asthma Research Program 3.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Duvall MG","lastName":"Duvall","firstName":"Melody G","initials":"MG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4173-3628"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA melody.duvall@mgb.org."},{"affiliation":"Division of Critical Care Medicine, Department of Anesthesiology, Critical Care and Pain Medicine, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Dorazio MH","lastName":"Dorazio","firstName":"Madison H","initials":"MH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chiha T","lastName":"Chiha","firstName":"Tania","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Reilly RB","lastName":"Reilly","firstName":"Roisin B","initials":"RB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ramdour SK","lastName":"Ramdour","firstName":"Saïsha K","initials":"SK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Krishnamoorthy N","lastName":"Krishnamoorthy","firstName":"Nandini","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Cagnina RE","lastName":"Cagnina","firstName":"R Elaine","initials":"RE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Phipatanakul W","lastName":"Phipatanakul","firstName":"Wanda","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Allergy, Dermatology, Rheumatology, and Immunology, Department of Pediatrics, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Israel E","lastName":"Israel","firstName":"Elliot","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Levy BD","lastName":"Levy","firstName":"Bruce D","initials":"BD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9515-5731"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"collectiveName":"National Heart Lung and Blood Institute's Severe Asthma Research Program 3"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9515-5731"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4173-3628"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4276231,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Eur Respir J","title":"The European respiratory journal","essn":"1399-3003","issn":"0903-1936","nlmid":"8803460","isoabbreviation":"Eur Respir J"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2600317","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Natural killer (NK) cell dysfunction and defective inflammation resolution pathways are associated with chronic airway inflammation in asthma. NK cells are targets for regulation by essential polyunsaturated fatty acid-derived specialized pro-resolving mediators (SPMs), including resolvin D2 (RvD2), to stimulate inflammation resolution.<h4>Methods</h4>Multiparameter flow cytometry was utilized to examine expression patterns of select activating and inhibitory receptors, including the RvD2 receptor DRV2, on circulating NK cells in SARP3 severe and non-severe asthma subjects and healthy controls. Plasma RvD2 and serum amyloid A (SAA) was measured, and RvD2 and SAA regulation of NK cell cytotoxicity was examined <i>in vitro</i>.<h4>Results</h4>Expansion of circulating NK cells with downregulated activating receptors NKp30 and NKp46 and inhibitory receptor CD161 were linked with exacerbation susceptibility and longitudinal lung function trajectory in asthma. Circulating SAA levels were higher in severe asthma patients (median 8.8 μg·mL<sup>-1</sup>) than healthy individuals (2.2 μg·mL<sup>-1</sup>; p=0.003). In contrast, plasma RvD2 levels were lower in severe asthma (153 pg·mL<sup>-1</sup> <i>versus</i> 208 pg·mL<sup>-1</sup> in health; p=0.041), concomitant with a two-fold higher NK cell DRV2 receptor expression than in non-severe asthma subjects (3973 MFI <i>versus</i> 1798; p=0.003). High-dose SAA exposure downregulated NK cell triggering receptors and resulted in a 22% suppression of cytotoxicity (48.9±9.7% <i>versus</i> 62.5±9.2% in vehicle; p=0.001). In contrast, RvD2 enhanced NK cell cytotoxicity (38.1±8.8% <i>versus</i> 33.1±8.3%; p=0.007) and partially reversed SAA-mediated suppression (5.4±2.7% change; p=0.031).<h4>Conclusions</h4>These findings identify a perturbed counterregulatory network for inflammation resolution in severe asthma comprised of excess pro-inflammatory SAA, deficient pro-resolving RvD2, and altered NK cell immune signatures that were associated with increased asthma morbidity. The beneficial impact of RvD2 in counteracting SAA-mediated suppression of NK cell effector function supports a pivotal mechanistic role for SPMs to enhance cell-type specific resolution pathways to combat persistent airway inflammation in asthma.","affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA melody.duvall@mgb.org.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.00317-2026"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-11","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42956882","source":"MED","pmid":"42956882","doi":"10.1016/j.cbi.2026.112406","title":"Involvement of PINK1-related mitophagy and necroptosis in TDI-induced experimental asthma.","authorString":"He X, Fu S, Yao D, Zuo L, Yuan X, Du Y, Qian Z, Ban J, He Q, You M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"He X","lastName":"He","firstName":"Xiu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China. Electronic address: hexiu@gmc.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Fu S","lastName":"Fu","firstName":"Shuai","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yao D","lastName":"Yao","firstName":"Dengxiang","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zuo L","lastName":"Zuo","firstName":"Lijuan","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yuan X","lastName":"Yuan","firstName":"Xiaoli","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Du Y","lastName":"Du","firstName":"Yiyang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qian Z","lastName":"Qian","firstName":"Zhiyao","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ban J","lastName":"Ban","firstName":"Jiaqi","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"He Q","lastName":"He","firstName":"Qian","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, The Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China. Electronic address: 2023140020721@stu.gmc.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"You M","lastName":"You","firstName":"Mingdan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China. Electronic address: youmingdan@gmc.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"441","journalIssueId":4303535,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Chem Biol Interact","title":"Chemico-biological interactions","essn":"1872-7786","issn":"0009-2797","nlmid":"0227276","isoabbreviation":"Chem Biol Interact"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"112406","abstractText":"Toluene diisocyanate (TDI) is a prevalent cause of occupational asthma. Although mitophagy contributes to various pulmonary diseases, its role and downstream signaling in TDI-induced asthma remain unclear. In this study, we first utilized BALB/c mice to investigate the effects of TDI exposure on pulmonary injury, mitophagy, and associated regulatory mechanisms. Based on these findings, we constructed an adverse outcome pathway (AOP) network for TDI-induced asthma. Mechanistic connections predicted by the AOP were validated in vitro using PINK1-knockdown bronchial epithelial cells, assessing PINK1-related mitophagy, mitochondrial function, cytosolic mtDNA release, attenuated the increased expression of cGAS/STING pathway-related proteins, and necroptosis. Finally, we predicted potential natural product therapeutics through bioactivity screening and toxicity profiling, targeting key genes implicated in TDI-induced asthma. Integration of in vivo, in vitro, and AOP network analyses indicated that TDI exposure induced airway hyperresponsiveness and pulmonary histopathological changes, along with elevated Th2 cytokine production. This exposure selectively upregulated PINK1 and Parkin expression, without altering levels of BNIP3, NIX, or FUNDC1. PINK1 knockdown mitigated TDI-induced mitophagy-related alterations, restored mitochondrial function, reduced cytosolic mtDNA release, attenuated the increased expression of cGAS/STING pathway-related proteins, and reduced necroptosis. In silico screening predicted β-lapachone, genistein, and apigenin as promising candidate therapeutics for TDI-induced asthma. Overall, PINK1-related mitophagy may contribute to TDI-induced occupational asthma, and natural compounds identified via in silico screening are potential candidates that require further in vitro and in vivo validation of their safety and efficacy.","affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China. Electronic address: hexiu@gmc.edu.cn.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Necroptosis","Pink1","Occupational Asthma","Mitophagy","Toluene Diisocyanate","Adverse Outcome Pathway"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cbi.2026.112406"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42872540","source":"MED","pmid":"42872540","doi":"10.1002/jpn3.70605","title":"The relationship between gut microbiota-friendly diet and the prevalence and severity of asthma in children.","authorString":"Tan LJ, Tang X, Shan Y, Ren Y, Sun T, Zhou Y, Fu J, Zhang Y, Cui Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tan LJ","lastName":"Tan","firstName":"Li-Juan","initials":"LJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8970-0884"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Pediatric Infection, Immunity, and Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine, Shanghai, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Food and Nutrition, Chung-Ang University, Anseong-Si, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Tang X","lastName":"Tang","firstName":"Xifeng","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shan Y","lastName":"Shan","firstName":"Yijun","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ren Y","lastName":"Ren","firstName":"Yuqian","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun T","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Ting","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou Y","lastName":"Zhou","firstName":"Yiping","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Fu J","lastName":"Fu","firstName":"Jialei","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6691-6834"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Vanke School of Public Health, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Yucai","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Pediatric Infection, Immunity, and Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cui Y","lastName":"Cui","firstName":"Yun","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3649-5274"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Pediatric Infection, Immunity, and Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3649-5274"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6691-6834"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8970-0884"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4291191,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr","title":"Journal of pediatric gastroenterology and nutrition","essn":"1536-4801","issn":"0277-2116","nlmid":"8211545","isoabbreviation":"J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>Dietary factors may impact asthma indirectly by modulating the composition and activity of the gut microbiota. This study aimed to explore the potential relationship between gut microbiota-friendly diet and both the prevalence and severity of asthma in children.<h4>Methods</h4>We selected a total of 2256 children from the 2021-2023 National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey. The gut microbiota-friendly diet was assessed using the Diet-Gut Microbiome Index (DI-GM), which incorporates 14 specific food constituents. To calculate the DI-GM, two non-consecutive 24-h dietary recalls data were used, from which the relevant food constituents were derived. A higher DI-GM score indicates greater alignment with a gut microbiota-friendly diet.<h4>Results</h4>Among the 2256 U.S. children, the weighted prevalence of asthma is 13.36%. In fully adjusted models, DI-GM was not significantly associated with asthma prevalence overall or within any subgroups. However, higher DI-GM (T2) was significantly associated with a lower risk of the mild asthma phenotype in the 5-16 age group. In contrast, no significant associations were observed between DI-GM and asthma severity in the other age groups. Subgroup analysis showed a higher DI-GM score was significantly correlated with a lower risk for asthma in subgroups stratified by obesity status.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Higher DI-GM scores were significantly inversely associated with asthma severity risk in children aged 5-16 years and in obese individuals. These cross-sectional findings support the need for longitudinal studies to determine whether dietary modulation may affect asthma severity via microbiota-dependent mechanisms.","affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai Children's Hospital, School of Medicine, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Shanghai Municipal Talent Work Bureau","grantId":"25PJA120","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nutritional Epidemiology","Diet Assessment","Gut‐Lung Axis","Dietary Index For Gut Microbiota"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jpn3.70605"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42968511","source":"MED","pmid":"42968511","pmcid":"PMC13725122","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13725122"]},"doi":"10.1111/jcmm.71410","title":"The Cytokine Response of Circulating Immune Cells to Rhinovirus (RV-A16) is Reduced in Severe Asthma.","authorString":"Jamal Jameel K, Yanik SD, Pfeifer KR, Weidinger D, Rohde S, Struck B, Bülthoff E, Bürger P, Kronsbein J, Tenbusch M, Knobloch J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jamal Jameel K","lastName":"Jamal Jameel","firstName":"Kaschin","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Yanik SD","lastName":"Yanik","firstName":"Sarah D","initials":"SD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Pfeifer KR","lastName":"Pfeifer","firstName":"Kai Robin","initials":"KR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Weidinger D","lastName":"Weidinger","firstName":"Daniel","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Rohde S","lastName":"Rohde","firstName":"Simon","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Struck B","lastName":"Struck","firstName":"Birte","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Bülthoff E","lastName":"Bülthoff","firstName":"Eike","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Bürger P","lastName":"Bürger","firstName":"Paul","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Kronsbein J","lastName":"Kronsbein","firstName":"Juliane","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Tenbusch M","lastName":"Tenbusch","firstName":"Matthias","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Harald Zur Hausen Institute of Virology, Uniklinikum Erlangen, Friedrich-Alexander-Universität (FAU) Erlangen-Nürnberg, Erlangen, Germany."},{"affiliation":"FAU Profile Center Immunomedicine (FAU I-MED), Friedrich-Alexander-Universität (FAU) Erlangen-Nürnberg, Erlangen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Knobloch J","lastName":"Knobloch","firstName":"Jürgen","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"21","volume":"30","journalIssueId":4300689,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Cell Mol Med","title":"Journal of cellular and molecular medicine","essn":"1582-4934","issn":"1582-1838","nlmid":"101083777","isoabbreviation":"J Cell Mol Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e71410","abstractText":"Severe asthma patients (SA) are more susceptible to respiratory infections with human rhinoviruses (HRV) than healthy subjects (H), causing exacerbations. In response to HRV, circulating immune cells are recruited to and activated at the local sites of infection. Hypothesis: The activation of circulating immune cells in response to HRV is impaired in severe asthma. We infected peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC) of H (n = 20) and SA (n = 50) with RV-A16 ex vivo. Cytokines were measured in culture supernatants by ELISA. Data were compared between SA and H and correlated to asthma phenotype biomarkers. RV-A16 induced IFNα, IFNγ, CCL2, CCL5, TNFα, IL-1β, IL-6 and IL-8. Except for CCL2, these cytokine responses were reduced in SA versus H. In SA, IFNα, IL-6 and CCL5 responses correlated negatively, but IL-1β correlated positively to FeNO. IFNγ correlated positively to blood eosinophils and serum IL-33, and INFα correlated positively to serum total IgE. Cytokine responses of circulating immune cells to RV-A16 are reduced in SA. Cytokines are differentially affected in allergic, eosinophilic or T2 low asthma. This could help to explain the increased risk for exacerbations in HRV infections in severe asthma and differences between phenotypes. Circulating immune cells might be considered as drug targets to reduce exacerbation rates.","affiliation":"Medical Clinic III for Pneumology, Allergology, and Sleep Medicine, Bergmannsheil University Hospital, Ruhr University Bochum, Bochum, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"FoRUM program of the Ruhr-University of Bochum","grantId":"F801-2014","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Leukocytes, Mononuclear","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VI","qualifierName":"virology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Rhinovirus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Picornaviridae Infections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VI","qualifierName":"virology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VI","qualifierName":"virology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cytokines","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biomarkers"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Infection","Asthma","Biomarker","Phenotypes","Exacerbations","Human Rhinovirus"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Cytokines","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Biomarkers","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jcmm.71410"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13725122"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13725122?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42829568","source":"MED","pmid":"42829568","pmcid":"PMC13633673","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13633673"]},"doi":"10.2147/jaa.s609810","title":"Adherence to Inhaled Corticosteroids and Inflammatory Phenotypes in Asthma.","authorString":"Mosbech CH, Håkansson KEJ, Hansen ESH, Meteran H, Jacobsen PA, Brustad N, Brask EW, Janns L, Westergaard CG, Godtfredsen NS, Ulrik CS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mosbech CH","lastName":"Mosbech","firstName":"Christiane Hammershaimb","initials":"CH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Håkansson KEJ","lastName":"Håkansson","firstName":"Kjell Erik Julius","initials":"KEJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5804-0740"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Hansen ESH","lastName":"Hansen","firstName":"Erik Sören Halvard","initials":"ESH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Meteran H","lastName":"Meteran","firstName":"Howraman","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5994-8064"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Jacobsen PA","lastName":"Jacobsen","firstName":"Peter Ascanius","initials":"PA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6083-7441"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Diseases, Aalborg University Hospital, Aalborg C, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University, Aalborg Ø, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Brustad N","lastName":"Brustad","firstName":"Nicklas","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6383-8845"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Copenhagen Prospective Studies on Asthma in Childhood (COPSAC), Copenhagen University Hospital - Herlev and Gentofte, Gentofte, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Brask EW","lastName":"Brask","firstName":"Erica Woer","initials":"EW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Janns L","lastName":"Janns","firstName":"Laurits","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Westergaard CG","lastName":"Westergaard","firstName":"Christian Grabow","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Lung Clinic, Helsingør, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Godtfredsen NS","lastName":"Godtfredsen","firstName":"Nina Skavlan","initials":"NS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1468-2869"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Medicine, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Ulrik CS","lastName":"Ulrik","firstName":"Charlotte Suppli","initials":"CS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8689-3695"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Medicine, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5804-0740"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5994-8064"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6083-7441"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6383-8845"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8689-3695"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1468-2869"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4095019,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy","title":"Journal of asthma and allergy","essn":"1178-6965","issn":"1178-6965","nlmid":"101543450","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"609810","abstractText":"<h4>Background and aim</h4>Adherence to inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) is essential for asthma control, yet little is known about whether adherence differs across inflammatory phenotypes. Patients with type 2 (T2)-low biomarkers have reduced corticosteroid responsiveness, which may influence adherence. We aimed to evaluate adherence to ICS across inflammatory phenotypes and to examine whether associations between adherence and clinical risk factors differ by inflammatory phenotype.<h4>Material and methods</h4>This retrospective cohort study included adult asthma patients managed at a respiratory outpatient clinic between May 2020 and May 2022. Inflammatory phenotype was defined using blood eosinophils (cut-off 0.15×10<sup>9</sup>/L) and FeNO (cut-off 25 ppb). Patients were categorized into T2-low (two negative T2-biomarkers), possible T2-high (one positive T2-biomarker), and T2-high (two positive T2-biomarkers). Adherence was calculated as medication possession ratio (MPR), with >0.8 defining good adherence. Data was obtained from electronic health records and national registries. Logistic regression models adjusted for relevant covariates were performed.<h4>Results</h4>Of 960 phenotyped patients (165 T2-low, 538 possible T2-high, 257 T2-high), 484 were included in the adherence analysis. Adherence did not differ significantly between phenotypes, although patients with T2-low asthma showed a tendency towards lower adherence (0.66) than patients with possible T2-high and T2-high asthma (both 0.74). Across phenotypes, increasing age was associated with higher odds of good adherence (OR 1.25, p <0.001). Among male patients, those with T2-high (OR 0.13, p = 0.022) and possible T2-high (OR 0.14, p = 0.023) phenotypes had lower odds of good adherence than those with T2-low phenotype.<h4>Conclusion</h4>In patients with asthma, adherence to ICS did not differ significantly across inflammatory phenotypes, although patients with T2-low asthma tended to show lower adherence. Among males, adherence varied by phenotype, with T2-high and possible T2-high asthma associated with lower odds of good adherence.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Copenhagen University Hospital - Hvidovre, Hvidovre, Denmark.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biomarkers","Medication Possession Ratio","Feno","Blood Eosinophils","T2 Inflammation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JAA.S609810"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13633673"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13633673?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026